WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.760
- The following is a conversation with
Preston Stewart and Michael Kofman

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:08.060
about the war in Ukraine.
Preston is a West Point graduate

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:12.260
and Afghanistan veteran who
covers the war extensively on

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.040
his YouTube channel called Preston
Stewart, his podcast called

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.200
War and Conflict, and his
newsletter called Defense

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.320
Tech News. Michael is a world-class
military analysis expert

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.960
specializing in the Russian
and Ukrainian militaries.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.220
He is a senior fellow at the
Carnegie Endowment, and he

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.200
hosts the Russia
Contingency Podcast on the

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:41.440
War on the Rocks platform. Please
follow and support their work.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.360
This is the Lex Fridman Podcast. To support
it, please check out our sponsors in

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.940
the description, where you
can also find links to

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.940
contact me, ask questions,
give feedback, and so on.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.120
And now, dear friends, here's
Preston Stewart and Michael Kofman.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.379
Gentlemen, thank you for doing this late-night
conversation. Let's see how long we go.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:09.080
The war in Ukraine is in
its fifth year. Let's

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.040
lay out what is the state of the war in
terms of territories, in terms of battles,

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.220
in terms of everything that you talk about from
a military perspective. Mike, do you want to

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:17.560
go first?

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.800
- Yeah, sure. I think that
the war had probably an

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.460
inflection point last year, and most
people recognize that this year

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.240
for Ukraine is the best year of combat
performance for them on the ground since

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.460
probably about the summer of
twenty twenty-three offensive.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:37.540
But there's a dichotomy to it because
on the one hand, Ukraine's doing

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:41.500
really well along the front line, but it
faces the challenge of an expansive Russian

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.960
strike campaign and the cumulative damage
that has been inflicted to Ukrainian

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:50.280
infrastructure, both energy and all sorts
of civilian types of infrastructure

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.280
over the course of several years.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.000
And so the winter is quite uncertain
on what's going to happen in Ukraine's

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.940
population centers. Now, I know
Ukraine has proven resilient and is

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.180
going to get through this winter, but
the level of damage to the economy,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.180
remains unclear.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.280
The Russian military is doing quite poorly this
year in the war. For them, this is probably

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.680
their worst year of combat performance
since winter of twenty twenty-three.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.420
And it's clear that the way
they've been fighting has reached

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.420
diminishing returns, not in twenty twenty-six,
but I would probably peg it as far back as

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.100
summer of twenty twenty-five. They
increasingly will face some manpower issues,

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.920
and their effort now fixates
more on strategic bombardment,

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.280
which, while inflicting a lot of damage
on Ukraine, has historically a challenge

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:42.380
in that strategic bombardment does not
have a great track record or even a good

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.620
track record of achieving maximalist
political aims in the war,

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.740
especially if a ground
campaign is unsuccessful or is

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.740
floundering as Russia's is at this
stage of the conflict. So the history

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:57.720
of strategic bombardment as a
way to try to win the war in the

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.220
absence of the success of other
efforts is not a good one.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:05.080
Although as an analyst, I'll tell you, most political
leaders always think that it might work for them,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.360
even if you tell them that it doesn't.
And so it's unsurprising to see

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.080
that Russia's now leaning heavily
towards strategic bombardment

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:14.860
as a key pillar of its strategy.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.420
- I think it would also be nice to lay out
the basics every time you bring them up.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:24.920
Maybe can you give a quick tangent of the
twenty twenty-three offensive and then,

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:29.860
what strategic bombardment is as
opposed to the other mechanisms of war?

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.280
- Sure. So in twenty twenty-three,

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.560
there were a series of fairly
set piece offensives and

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.540
counter-offensives. After a
partial mobilization with

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:44.020
manpower replenished and some reserves
established, Russia attempted

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:48.620
a winter offensive, but its forces were unready.
And so the winter campaign for Russia in twenty

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.420
twenty-three was largely a failure,
perhaps with the exception of the grinding

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:53.920
battle over the city of Bakhmut.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.320
Then Ukraine launched a
much-anticipated summer offensive

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.640
using units that were freshly trained
and equipped by Western countries.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.880
And there was a great
deal of kind of hope and

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:11.820
expectations that were placed
upon this offensive, but it too

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:15.540
failed. It lasted several months. I think
in reality, it went from June twenty

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.600
twenty-three till about November
of twenty twenty-three.

00:04:18.779 --> 00:04:22.620
And then Russia tried to seize the initiative
again and started its own offensive in fall twenty

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:24.100
twenty-three with the Battle of Avdiivka.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.780
But so summer of twenty twenty-three is
one of the periods where we still see,

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.740
an exchange in terms of initiative
between the two sides and fairly

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.360
clear-cut major offensive operations
and counter-offensive operations.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.300
And in twenty twenty-three early on, you
have a failed Russian winter offensive.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:43.980
This is probably one of the worst points for the
Russian military in the war, fall through winter,

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.320
fall twenty twenty-two through
winter of twenty twenty-three.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:50.860
And then you have the Ukrainian summer twenty
twenty-three offensive, where Ukraine does gain some

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:56.700
ground, but it's not what people hoped
for. And since then, for much of the war,

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.480
you know, you see Russia trying to establish and
having an advantage in manpower and material.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.820
And for much of twenty-four and
twenty- five, Russia actually tends to

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:09.380
have the initiative, even
though it's largely pursuing a

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.860
grinding offensive on a very broad front.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:16.540
And if I sort of fast-forward and take
us into twenty twenty-six, you really

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.920
start to see the inflection point probably
around fall of twenty twenty-five.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:24.180
And then in twenty twenty-six, the
Russian effort is quite floundering, and

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.140
Russian forces face significant reversals
with the battle space a lot more

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:33.400
dynamic this year. Ukraine has two of
its own localized offensives running.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.700
and you see there's actually much
greater fluidity in twenty twenty-six

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:42.100
on the battlefield, certainly at the tactical
level than you would in twenty-five or

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.500
twenty-four. There's often a
perception that it's just a positional

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.380
stalemate. But in fact, even
more recently, some of Ukraine's

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.980
offensive operations had
gained more terrain than

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:56.580
Ukrainian forces were able to do during
the summer twenty twenty-three offensive,

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:59.420
which was planned to be
a much bigger operation.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:03.520
- Also, it might be useful to
kind of state perhaps the

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.988
obvious, but the nature of this war --

00:06:08.188 --> 00:06:11.248
one of the things, especially as
it evolved, the role of drones

00:06:12.528 --> 00:06:16.068
on the front, and maybe this is
where you can loop in strategic

00:06:16.108 --> 00:06:19.048
bombardment as well. Like, what are
the main mechanisms of war here?

00:06:19.768 --> 00:06:23.588
- So we look at this war beyond what's happening
at the front line. Russia has, from the

00:06:23.628 --> 00:06:27.548
very beginning of the war,
had a series of strikes, an

00:06:27.608 --> 00:06:31.408
expansive strike campaign,
but it really begins in fall

00:06:31.568 --> 00:06:35.308
2022, where they begin
launching combination strikes

00:06:35.648 --> 00:06:39.208
of long-range air-launched cruise
missiles and short-range ballistic

00:06:39.288 --> 00:06:42.708
missiles against Ukrainian critical
infrastructure. And by critical

00:06:42.748 --> 00:06:46.408
infrastructure, I mean
power plants, substations,

00:06:47.188 --> 00:06:51.128
various types of heating
plants, things that

00:06:51.188 --> 00:06:55.048
a country needs in order to sustain
the economy and the population.

00:06:55.408 --> 00:06:58.808
And over time, those targets
expand, right? Russia targets

00:06:59.108 --> 00:07:03.048
various types of civilian
infrastructure. And actually, at this

00:07:03.088 --> 00:07:06.968
point in 2026, we've seen that
campaign really broaden to include

00:07:08.108 --> 00:07:11.648
warehouses, data centers, schools,

00:07:11.808 --> 00:07:15.488
medical facilities, malls, actually quite
a few shopping malls in the last few

00:07:15.548 --> 00:07:19.108
months, trains. So it's gone far beyond

00:07:19.268 --> 00:07:22.588
energy infrastructure to include a host
of other civilian types of targets.

00:07:23.268 --> 00:07:27.188
And it changed over time from being primarily
done with cruise missiles and ballistic missiles

00:07:27.868 --> 00:07:31.788
to being mostly conducted via saturation

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:35.048
strikes using large numbers
of one-way attack drones.

00:07:36.368 --> 00:07:39.828
And those drones themselves
evolved in terms of the systems,

00:07:40.868 --> 00:07:43.668
gone now from being largely done

00:07:44.408 --> 00:07:47.988
with cheaper, easier to shoot
down propeller-driven drones

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:52.088
to ones that are jet-powered and are, in
effect, light ground-launched cruise missiles,

00:07:52.788 --> 00:07:56.748
and a much larger number of ballistic
missiles, some which are more sophisticated.

00:07:57.368 --> 00:08:00.348
And so Ukraine's been facing
both large combination strikes,

00:08:01.148 --> 00:08:04.248
but also daily sustained pressure,

00:08:05.808 --> 00:08:09.528
constant attacks with
jet-powered drones that generate

00:08:09.588 --> 00:08:13.948
alerts and that disrupt economic life and
try to exhaust Ukraine's air defense.

00:08:14.808 --> 00:08:18.508
- By the way, I forgot to mention that I'm a
big fan of both of yours. It's an honor,

00:08:18.928 --> 00:08:22.828
to have the both of you here, Mike and
Preston. Preston, I've been watching

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:26.788
you for a long time, so
thank you for being here. So

00:08:26.948 --> 00:08:30.788
yes, to ask you, do you
have, to build on what Mike

00:08:30.828 --> 00:08:34.748
was saying, and maybe can you also speak
to some of the geographic areas that

00:08:34.788 --> 00:08:36.548
are in question here
that are most contested?

00:08:37.068 --> 00:08:40.988
- Yeah, absolutely, Lex. Honor to be here. Always a
challenge to add anything to what Mike has to say.

00:08:41.048 --> 00:08:44.728
We'll give it a shot here. I think
it's good to get an idea of the

00:08:44.848 --> 00:08:48.548
areas that we're gonna be talking about. In
Ukraine, the fight still is predominantly in the

00:08:48.628 --> 00:08:52.188
east and the southeast. So Kyiv,
kind of in the middle of the

00:08:52.228 --> 00:08:56.088
country, is not facing direct
Russian attacks in terms of

00:08:56.168 --> 00:08:59.768
infantry and short-range drones. They,
of course, are on the receiving end

00:08:59.868 --> 00:09:03.748
daily of long-range drones and missile
strikes. But as you move through the

00:09:03.768 --> 00:09:07.668
border region, starting in the north,
there are Russian infiltrations in

00:09:07.748 --> 00:09:11.268
Sumy. There's also fighting
in Ukrainian territory in

00:09:11.308 --> 00:09:14.708
Kharkiv. And then you get down
into what's known as the Donbas,

00:09:15.228 --> 00:09:18.968
Luhansk, and Donetsk, and this
is where some of the heaviest

00:09:19.028 --> 00:09:22.988
fighting throughout the war has taken place.
And this is, I'm sure we'll get into it

00:09:23.048 --> 00:09:26.948
later, but an area referred to as the
Fortress Belt that Ukraine has turned into

00:09:27.008 --> 00:09:30.908
just an absolutely brutal area for
Russia to try to advance through. It

00:09:30.928 --> 00:09:34.888
is costing Russia a lot of people
day in and day out. Moving

00:09:34.928 --> 00:09:38.708
from there, we're into Zaporizhzhia,
and then Kherson butts up

00:09:38.748 --> 00:09:39.528
against the Black Sea.

00:09:39.988 --> 00:09:43.508
- And some of the strategic bombardment
that Mike was talking about. So that's

00:09:43.768 --> 00:09:46.288
beyond the, even these areas. So even

00:09:47.268 --> 00:09:50.608
uh, Kyiv is being hit, so a lot of the
places throughout Ukraine is being hit.

00:09:50.888 --> 00:09:54.088
- And some in the western portion of the
country, not as frequently. Kyiv is a-

00:09:54.088 --> 00:09:55.088
... a focus.

00:09:55.468 --> 00:09:56.828
- If you look along the front today,

00:09:57.788 --> 00:10:01.568
you have essentially some Russian
border incursions trying to

00:10:01.588 --> 00:10:03.708
establish a small buffer
across the border,

00:10:04.708 --> 00:10:08.208
which are lower intensity areas of
fighting in Sumy, Kharkiv. You have an

00:10:08.328 --> 00:10:11.828
ongoing fighting around city of
Kupyansk and Kharkiv region.

00:10:12.928 --> 00:10:16.608
And then you have the focus of Russian
offensive efforts, Preston was saying,

00:10:17.288 --> 00:10:20.508
still trying to take the
remainder of the Donbas,

00:10:21.328 --> 00:10:25.208
and that fighting ranges all
the way down from cities like

00:10:25.407 --> 00:10:28.848
areas like Liman, all
the way towards the main

00:10:28.888 --> 00:10:32.508
conglomeration of cities. And we should mention them
now because I think they're gonna come up, that are

00:10:32.548 --> 00:10:37.688
remaining in the Donetsk region,
which are Sloviansk, Kramatorsk,

00:10:37.888 --> 00:10:41.848
Druzhkivka, and Kostiantynivka,
which troops all colloquially call

00:10:41.888 --> 00:10:45.808
Kostya, because its name's too long. And
then further west of that is Dnipropetrovsk,

00:10:45.908 --> 00:10:49.848
which is a main city now that's
been essentially an anchor in

00:10:49.928 --> 00:10:53.688
Ukrainian defense, west of that
conglomeration, with another chain of cities

00:10:53.748 --> 00:10:59.288
behind it. And if we head
southwest, we get into the Dnipro

00:10:59.348 --> 00:11:03.028
region and then Zaporizhzhia. And
Zaporizhzhia is an interesting, fairly

00:11:03.108 --> 00:11:07.008
dynamic area because you have both Russian
and Ukrainian forces on the offensive at the

00:11:07.068 --> 00:11:11.208
same time, where you have Russian
forces trying to push west of,

00:11:12.068 --> 00:11:15.748
um, Huliaipole, and you have
northeast of that, Ukrainian

00:11:15.828 --> 00:11:19.568
forces having had the initiative
for much of the year. A tactical

00:11:19.748 --> 00:11:23.548
grouping of Ukraine air assault brigades has
been running kind of a creeping offensive there,

00:11:23.948 --> 00:11:27.748
has been pretty successful in making gains.
And the other area which we might get

00:11:27.788 --> 00:11:31.508
into later around Liman was
Ukraine's Third Corps having

00:11:31.548 --> 00:11:35.248
conducted a successful offensive operation
as well called Vihod. So those are two

00:11:35.308 --> 00:11:39.108
areas where Ukraine's not only contesting
the initiative, but actually it's liberating

00:11:39.148 --> 00:11:42.288
territory, and in many ways
wiping out Russian gains from

00:11:42.428 --> 00:11:45.948
2025. And that's what I meant when I was
saying earlier that this is a much more

00:11:45.968 --> 00:11:49.948
dynamic battle space than people
think, in that territory is a

00:11:49.988 --> 00:11:53.528
lot more fluid. There's a vast gray
zone, in fact, between the two lines.

00:11:54.248 --> 00:11:58.048
Ukraine does not hold cohesive
defensive lines. It often has

00:11:58.088 --> 00:12:01.908
essentially pickets with small number
of infantry that mark positions,

00:12:02.388 --> 00:12:06.328
but much of the territory is
essentially contested such that in

00:12:06.348 --> 00:12:12.496
a battle space defined by dense
control via drones and a sort

00:12:12.556 --> 00:12:14.516
of a sizable engagement zone,

00:12:15.416 --> 00:12:19.076
which varies from place to place, but you
might say roughly it might be 15 kilometers

00:12:19.836 --> 00:12:23.536
forward and back of the front line. Although in
some areas it's more, in other areas it's less.

00:12:23.636 --> 00:12:28.496
It depends. What happens is, in reality,
that it becomes more of a gray zone,

00:12:29.476 --> 00:12:33.076
and as Preston was saying, Russian
forces try to get past this drone

00:12:33.116 --> 00:12:36.836
engagement zone or defeat zone through
infiltration. That's their main challenge,

00:12:37.116 --> 00:12:40.736
either through individual infantry,
through light motorized attacks on

00:12:40.756 --> 00:12:44.676
motorcycles and ATVs, and they try
to essentially get as far back past

00:12:44.716 --> 00:12:48.236
Ukrainian positions as they can, whereas
Ukraine's largely defending with a

00:12:48.296 --> 00:12:51.616
combination of prepared
defenses, minefields,

00:12:52.776 --> 00:12:56.436
and a large array of
drones and drone units

00:12:56.956 --> 00:13:00.796
that are effectively engaging Russian forces
before they get to their, even their own

00:13:00.836 --> 00:13:04.796
front line, their forward line of
troops. And if they get past that

00:13:04.836 --> 00:13:08.416
point, they get past Ukrainian initial
positions, then Ukrainian drone units

00:13:08.456 --> 00:13:11.996
essentially pick them up there. So it's,
in many respects, kind of a zonal defense.

00:13:12.756 --> 00:13:14.896
But we should unpack that a bit because

00:13:16.036 --> 00:13:19.716
people see this as a drone war, and the
statistics out there do show that drones

00:13:19.756 --> 00:13:21.236
inflict 80% of the losses.

00:13:22.056 --> 00:13:26.236
However, it is really a combination of
things, and the dynamic is one that

00:13:27.116 --> 00:13:30.656
involves traditional defense and
minefields that make it very hard

00:13:31.116 --> 00:13:34.576
for mechanized assaults or light
motorized assaults to break through.

00:13:35.296 --> 00:13:38.316
Artillery, which still plays a role
in the battlefield because it forces,

00:13:39.216 --> 00:13:43.116
the enemy forces to disperse,
it has a shaping dynamic.

00:13:43.156 --> 00:13:47.076
Even if you don't use it much, the fact
that it's present, it makes it hard for

00:13:47.136 --> 00:13:50.756
troops to concentrate or suppresses
them. It slows them down in the

00:13:50.796 --> 00:13:53.536
assault, making them
easy targets for drones.

00:13:54.536 --> 00:13:58.256
And it's still relevant because drones
also have their own limitations,

00:13:58.696 --> 00:14:02.376
and two periods of the war when they
often have limitations seasonally

00:14:03.256 --> 00:14:07.036
are spring and fall, again to
the winter, because the weather

00:14:07.076 --> 00:14:10.956
becomes poor. You get high wind, you get
rain. Drones don't work nearly as effectively

00:14:11.076 --> 00:14:14.916
during poor weather, and so artillery's
a very important backup. And if you

00:14:15.016 --> 00:14:18.736
just had drones and you didn't have
anything else, no minefields, no

00:14:18.776 --> 00:14:22.616
artillery, I don't think Ukraine would
be able to hold the terrain this

00:14:22.696 --> 00:14:26.636
effectively with the low density of
forces, the men that they currently have

00:14:26.676 --> 00:14:29.976
deployed. So it's important to
describe as a combination of means,

00:14:30.556 --> 00:14:33.836
even if drones play such a central
role, that folks don't ignore

00:14:34.536 --> 00:14:38.796
the interaction of these different
capabilities in Ukraine's approach.

00:14:39.416 --> 00:14:41.416
- So sorry to linger on the basics, but, uh-

00:14:41.516 --> 00:14:45.436
... can we, like, lay out, so
artillery is big guns. What's

00:14:45.476 --> 00:14:49.016
the role of individual infantry?
What's the role of tanks?

00:14:49.456 --> 00:14:53.396
What is this gray zone? What does it actually
look like? How many drones are floating

00:14:53.456 --> 00:14:57.436
above? What does it look like, as Preston
was saying, for an individual infantry

00:14:57.676 --> 00:15:03.156
soldier to, to run through that? Can you
paint a picture of some of the basic

00:15:03.196 --> 00:15:03.576
things here?

00:15:03.896 --> 00:15:05.776
- Not good to run through that. It-

00:15:05.776 --> 00:15:09.656
... it's terrifying. There's constant
eyes in the sky, but to kind of

00:15:10.116 --> 00:15:13.956
walk this out a little bit,
artillery generally speaking,

00:15:14.136 --> 00:15:17.896
105 or 155 millimeter cannons. That's
what we use here in the United States.

00:15:17.956 --> 00:15:21.636
It's primarily what Ukraine has been using
now as it kind of shifted over to a NATO

00:15:21.716 --> 00:15:28.196
standard. You're talking about 20-ish
kilometer range, which at a time

00:15:28.276 --> 00:15:32.196
was big, and there was a phase
in this war where that was

00:15:32.916 --> 00:15:36.736
seemingly to determine the direction that it
was gonna go. It was who had more artillery-

00:15:36.736 --> 00:15:40.256
... who could mass fires in certain
directions. It's just changed so

00:15:40.316 --> 00:15:43.936
drastically now to where the drone range
has pushed those further and further back.

00:15:44.016 --> 00:15:47.816
So cannons are on the battlefield.
There are some that are dug

00:15:47.896 --> 00:15:51.576
in, kind of in entrenched positions.
Others are mobile on tracks or

00:15:51.636 --> 00:15:55.256
wheels 'cause they gotta get out of there. As soon
as they fire, there's so many drones overhead

00:15:55.316 --> 00:15:55.876
watching them.

00:15:56.776 --> 00:16:00.756
When you get into the drones then on
the battlefield, there's a number

00:16:00.856 --> 00:16:04.816
of them being utilized. Reconnaissance
drones is kind of where everything

00:16:05.016 --> 00:16:08.856
starts, and those are just
big fixed wing drones for the

00:16:08.916 --> 00:16:12.176
most part that they can stay
airborne for quite a while. They're

00:16:12.576 --> 00:16:16.516
generally low cost, and that's been a dynamic that
we've seen with both Russia and Ukraine in this

00:16:16.576 --> 00:16:24.056
war, is they've gone from the very few but
high cost, maybe larger drones, down to a

00:16:24.136 --> 00:16:28.076
ton of low cost, borderline
attritable. They don't wanna lose all

00:16:28.115 --> 00:16:31.756
these drones, but it's not that big
of a deal to lose one compared to,

00:16:32.336 --> 00:16:36.096
say, a US Predator Reaper that costs,
you know, 20-plus million dollars.

00:16:36.696 --> 00:16:40.496
So they've got these reconnaissance drones
up constantly. Both sides have these up.

00:16:41.076 --> 00:16:44.996
When they spot something, that is passed
along to the appropriate drone unit

00:16:45.216 --> 00:16:48.656
and it is engaged based off of their

00:16:49.316 --> 00:16:53.216
what they have available to them. The,
I would say, most common one that we

00:16:53.276 --> 00:16:56.976
see, we see in terms of the media that
has the most attention, would be the

00:16:57.096 --> 00:17:02.336
FPV drones. Those are small kind of
quadcopters. You see them flying

00:17:03.176 --> 00:17:06.836
12 inches, 18 inches. I think they
might get up to 20 inches at times.

00:17:07.736 --> 00:17:11.716
Generally, there's a munition attached to the
end of that. They crash it into something and

00:17:11.736 --> 00:17:14.816
it detonates. They can swap that out for

00:17:15.016 --> 00:17:18.596
anti-personnel munitions or go
after vehicles or fortifications,

00:17:19.156 --> 00:17:23.036
and they can do that on the spot. So there are
mountains of these FPVs. There was a picture

00:17:23.056 --> 00:17:26.916
of Kim Jong Un, actually, North
Korea walking through a hallway of

00:17:27.176 --> 00:17:30.896
stacked FPVs. So these are the very small,

00:17:31.276 --> 00:17:36.876
low-cost, attritable drones, and they're
being used. The numbers are staggering.

00:17:37.416 --> 00:17:41.176
- I'd say for FPVs, it's probably
somewhere eight to 10,000 per day,

00:17:41.376 --> 00:17:45.256
just those, not including other types
of tactical drones. And that would

00:17:45.296 --> 00:17:49.116
be for one side of the war. So to give
you a sense that these are hundreds of

00:17:49.156 --> 00:17:51.036
thousands of these being
employed per month.

00:17:51.936 --> 00:17:55.756
These strike capabilities exceed
all the numbers of personnel and

00:17:55.796 --> 00:17:58.596
equipment on the battlefield
by a very substantial amount.

00:17:58.636 --> 00:18:02.136
- And just to clarify, behind every
FPV drone, there's a human being

00:18:02.196 --> 00:18:06.036
operating it. So there's also, from the
news or something, there's a sense that

00:18:06.076 --> 00:18:09.836
autonomy is being utilized.
It is not. So this is

00:18:09.856 --> 00:18:12.576
something you guys talked about. There's
usually humans behind the drones.

00:18:13.548 --> 00:18:17.088
- Well, I'll be frank, most of this
war are systems that are remotely

00:18:17.188 --> 00:18:20.848
operated, but they are operated by
people. It is actually quite manpower-

00:18:20.888 --> 00:18:23.748
intensive. And in fact, it's,
in many cases, if you look at

00:18:24.748 --> 00:18:28.568
both Russian and Ukrainian military,
they had to build out substantial

00:18:29.228 --> 00:18:32.548
numbers of drone units
and channel manpower

00:18:32.808 --> 00:18:36.688
into the drone component of
the fight. It's not even a

00:18:36.768 --> 00:18:40.468
ratio of one to one, so one person
to one drone. A typical drone team

00:18:41.088 --> 00:18:44.708
might require two or three people. You have
a pilot, you have a co-pilot navigator.

00:18:45.088 --> 00:18:48.468
This is exhausting, tiring
work. I'm not saying that

00:18:49.528 --> 00:18:53.428
other traditional missions are easy, but with
a drone you have to be looking at a screen

00:18:53.468 --> 00:18:57.208
the whole time, all day. You have to be flying it,
it's very intensive. Somebody else has to navigate.

00:18:57.548 --> 00:19:01.988
Another person might be the technician
or the person who arms the system.

00:19:03.108 --> 00:19:07.068
And so almost all the systems in this war are
remotely operated, whether they're flying

00:19:07.148 --> 00:19:10.788
drones, one-way attack strike
drones, whether they're UGVs,

00:19:10.928 --> 00:19:14.488
uncrewed, you know, ground
vehicles. And they require

00:19:14.528 --> 00:19:18.447
considerable support. At the end of
the day, these are physical objects

00:19:18.508 --> 00:19:22.368
that require equipping, they require
troubleshooting. And like most things in war,

00:19:22.808 --> 00:19:26.548
doesn't even - they don't even necessarily
work well or right out of the box as well.

00:19:26.868 --> 00:19:30.828
It's what a lot of people don't necessarily
appreciate about military affairs, that a lot of

00:19:30.868 --> 00:19:34.728
things don't necessarily work as intended on the
battlefield. Somebody's gotta be there to fix it, they

00:19:34.768 --> 00:19:38.568
have to be there to check it. So the
bottom line is that it is actually quite

00:19:38.608 --> 00:19:39.388
manpower intensive.

00:19:40.188 --> 00:19:44.108
There is a degree of semi-autonomy,
terminal guidance and targeting at the very

00:19:44.188 --> 00:19:47.948
last point, or maybe
the pilot can hit a box

00:19:48.028 --> 00:19:51.708
over a vehicle and then the drone knows
that that's what it should go after,

00:19:52.028 --> 00:19:55.848
these kind of moments. And ways
that the drone can overcome

00:19:56.008 --> 00:19:59.928
enemy electronic warfare or jamming with
this kind of terminal guidance. But

00:20:00.048 --> 00:20:03.848
most of these use cases are a
tiny percentage of the overall

00:20:03.968 --> 00:20:07.908
fight and the overall
number of drones being

00:20:07.948 --> 00:20:11.648
employed in this war. I think
in some ways, I hate to

00:20:12.028 --> 00:20:15.788
take away from the autonomy and
artificial intelligence conversation.

00:20:16.868 --> 00:20:20.848
This war is testing that, and there are plenty
of areas where I see it's being tested, but

00:20:21.228 --> 00:20:26.848
to some extent it's been over-hyped in
terms of the percentages being, the

00:20:26.868 --> 00:20:31.188
percentage of the fight that's actually
being run that way. It is very much, um,

00:20:32.048 --> 00:20:35.648
still a human-centric war with
a lot of manpower involved

00:20:35.848 --> 00:20:39.648
running, operating, and maintaining
all of these capabilities.

00:20:40.308 --> 00:20:44.088
- So one of the other novel aspects
of this war is so much of it is

00:20:44.408 --> 00:20:48.028
televised, so that you could see
videos from the drone perspective

00:20:48.448 --> 00:20:52.268
on both sides of that drone
killing another human being.

00:20:53.148 --> 00:20:57.088
And one misconception I heard you
talk about is that it takes one

00:20:57.468 --> 00:21:01.368
drone to accomplish that
task, and it actually takes a

00:21:01.408 --> 00:21:04.948
lot more. Can you speak to that in
terms of the number of drones it takes

00:21:05.268 --> 00:21:10.448
to accomplish a particular task, like
to destroy a piece of technology or to

00:21:11.348 --> 00:21:13.388
I don't know the right way
of speaking about this-

00:21:13.388 --> 00:21:13.468
- That's it.

00:21:13.488 --> 00:21:14.728
- But to kill another person.

00:21:15.008 --> 00:21:18.908
- Yeah. Eliminate a target might
be a cold way of saying that.

00:21:19.588 --> 00:21:23.268
Um, we're in an interesting
place where there's a lot of

00:21:23.948 --> 00:21:27.768
propaganda value in footage for anybody

00:21:27.848 --> 00:21:31.708
using it in any c- in any military
right now in a way that we didn't have

00:21:31.788 --> 00:21:35.708
access to even just a few years
ago. So there's a lot of interest

00:21:35.748 --> 00:21:38.908
in being able to show... I mean, we
have seen now people will not take,

00:21:39.548 --> 00:21:42.548
reports that something happened
unless there's a video of it.

00:21:42.568 --> 00:21:46.408
Which is insane because that, you know, a few
years ago, you would never have expected to

00:21:46.428 --> 00:21:50.328
have video of a missile strike in Crimea on a
Russian airfield. Now, if there's no video,

00:21:50.408 --> 00:21:52.268
it's maybe it didn't even happen.

00:21:52.308 --> 00:21:56.268
So the expectation is there and countries
are meeting that by being able to say like,

00:21:56.388 --> 00:22:00.148
"Look, we penetrated your defenses.
Look what we did," or, "Look at all the

00:22:00.188 --> 00:22:04.028
soldiers we killed as they were, we
were making this approach." So it's

00:22:04.088 --> 00:22:07.708
out there. There's only a tiny fraction
that's made public in the first place.

00:22:08.368 --> 00:22:12.168
And of those, that's only a
tiny fraction of what actually

00:22:12.228 --> 00:22:16.008
accomplishes the mission. So drones,
the batteries run out, they get jammed.

00:22:16.028 --> 00:22:19.868
All of a sudden the screen just goes dark.
Sometimes they impact and they don't detonate.

00:22:20.088 --> 00:22:23.468
You know, all sorts of little things like
that. That footage doesn't make it out.

00:22:23.728 --> 00:22:27.428
It in turn kind of creates
this perception that one

00:22:27.648 --> 00:22:32.127
$300 drone or one $500 drone is eliminating
a tank, and that's not happening.

00:22:33.288 --> 00:22:37.148
Maybe with the right munition and
with a lucky shot and the tank

00:22:37.208 --> 00:22:41.128
isn't expecting it, maybe it's
possible. Realistically, we're

00:22:41.188 --> 00:22:45.068
seeing 10, 20, I think upwards
of 30 of these small drones at

00:22:45.128 --> 00:22:48.528
times being utilized to
actually eliminate a tank.

00:22:48.988 --> 00:22:52.968
- So we should be imagining a
very large number of FPV drones

00:22:53.028 --> 00:22:55.508
just hunting humans in
tanks and technology.

00:22:55.848 --> 00:22:59.808
- The problem with understanding
what the ratios are is the

00:22:59.848 --> 00:23:03.648
videos have a strong anchoring effect, and
the videos are all videos of successes.

00:23:03.828 --> 00:23:07.708
There are no videos of misses. So unless
you're actually there, you're able

00:23:07.768 --> 00:23:11.688
to collect the data, you don't
know what percentage of hits or w-

00:23:11.968 --> 00:23:15.528
percentage of drones launched that
really is. You might get easily the

00:23:15.548 --> 00:23:19.388
impression that 100% of the drones launch
a hit. As Preston said, that's very

00:23:19.488 --> 00:23:21.388
far from the case. So you
need quite a few drones.

00:23:22.428 --> 00:23:26.148
For people, even though on videos it
looks like a one-to-one ratio, it's often

00:23:26.208 --> 00:23:29.948
not. I think it's often a lot closer to
maybe five drones or something like that.

00:23:29.988 --> 00:23:30.508
- Per person?

00:23:31.228 --> 00:23:34.408
- Yeah, in practice. And I've seen,
I'll be frank, I've myself seen

00:23:35.168 --> 00:23:38.828
videos that aren't public, where in
the most extreme case, I saw one on a

00:23:39.008 --> 00:23:42.688
field trip in July where it took
12. On tanks, the most outside

00:23:42.728 --> 00:23:46.568
example I heard of a well-armored
protected Russian tank with

00:23:46.588 --> 00:23:50.428
electronic warfare, one of these tanks that
looks like a large barn, essentially, it's so,

00:23:51.268 --> 00:23:54.528
so heavily protected last year was

00:23:54.808 --> 00:23:58.748
79. That's an extreme case, but
most, as Preston said, is about

00:23:58.888 --> 00:24:02.508
30 to 40. For an infantry
fighting vehicle, about 15, maybe

00:24:02.528 --> 00:24:10.248
15 to 20, depending, again. And you
have some percentage of drones that are

00:24:10.308 --> 00:24:14.188
jammed, they don't make it to a target. You
have some percentage of drones that may fail to

00:24:14.228 --> 00:24:16.568
detonate. So it's quite a few. And-

00:24:18.104 --> 00:24:22.004
You know, in talking to folks who are
trying to return mobility and maneuver

00:24:22.024 --> 00:24:25.904
to the battlefield that specialize
in drone-enabled mechanized assault,

00:24:26.023 --> 00:24:29.564
where they have both mechanized units
and drone units supporting them,

00:24:30.344 --> 00:24:34.143
a company that, mechanized company
that goes on the attack now expects to

00:24:34.184 --> 00:24:37.664
take about close to 300 hits
in that assault. They expect

00:24:37.744 --> 00:24:41.344
vehicles to take quite a few hits, and
most to be able to withstand them.

00:24:42.604 --> 00:24:46.384
So it does take quite a few drones, but
the sheer saturation numbers of drones out

00:24:46.444 --> 00:24:49.384
there is very difficult to overstate.

00:24:49.784 --> 00:24:50.704
- So given that,

00:24:51.904 --> 00:24:55.784
what does a battle look like? How do you
gain territory given these drone units, this

00:24:55.864 --> 00:24:59.744
huge amount of drones, this
gray zone, also mentioning

00:24:59.804 --> 00:25:03.504
artillery, like you mentioned, and tanks?

00:25:04.704 --> 00:25:10.064
How do they work together in order to be
able to do something like gain territory?

00:25:10.684 --> 00:25:14.544
- Well, they're doing it differently now. We're seeing
a very different approach from Ukraine than we

00:25:14.624 --> 00:25:18.424
are from Russia. I can try to speak to the Russian part if
you want to maybe take the Ukrainian offensive here in a

00:25:18.464 --> 00:25:22.824
second. But Russia has defaulted
to more of a infiltration

00:25:23.484 --> 00:25:27.164
type tactic, where rather than large
formations that get spotted, again,

00:25:27.704 --> 00:25:31.624
10, 20, 30 kilometers away from the front,
and they're then hunted down with long range

00:25:31.684 --> 00:25:35.444
drones. They don't even have the opportunity
to get near the front line, so they

00:25:35.504 --> 00:25:39.224
stopped doing that. We started to see
Russia move towards more motorized

00:25:39.264 --> 00:25:43.264
assaults with just pickup trucks, ATVs,
even a lot of motorcycles at a point.

00:25:44.084 --> 00:25:47.924
Now it's mostly just individual
infantrymen. They have checkpoints on a

00:25:47.984 --> 00:25:51.684
map. There's-- they're released,
and they're sent forward five,

00:25:51.784 --> 00:25:55.404
10, 20 kilometers trying
to move to a forward

00:25:55.444 --> 00:25:59.424
location where they can consolidate.
And again, the Ukrainian lines, they

00:25:59.464 --> 00:26:03.424
look clean on a map. They're not.
There's a lot of gaps in there because

00:26:03.484 --> 00:26:07.524
a lot of it is being held with overhead
drone coverage to just monitor approaches,

00:26:08.444 --> 00:26:12.224
and the Russians will get through.
And one is a bit of a headache.

00:26:12.844 --> 00:26:16.764
Three becomes a bigger problem, and
all of a sudden they're harassing

00:26:16.924 --> 00:26:20.544
supply lines, and that forces Ukrainian
units to move back a little bit,

00:26:20.924 --> 00:26:24.464
forces Ukrainian drone pilots to move
back a little bit, and that opens up the

00:26:24.484 --> 00:26:28.364
battlefield for more Russian soldiers to
come forward just a little. There's a

00:26:28.443 --> 00:26:31.804
reason that the lines are moving
so slowly right now. That

00:26:31.864 --> 00:26:35.584
infiltration is incredibly
costly. We talked with an

00:26:35.584 --> 00:26:39.444
American volunteer, Marine, fighting
with the First National Guard

00:26:39.544 --> 00:26:43.004
Corps as of in the Dobropillia
direction a little over a year ago.

00:26:43.584 --> 00:26:47.904
He described this as an incredible tactic
if you don't care about losing soldiers.

00:26:48.704 --> 00:26:52.144
He said, "It's great. You can just kind of chew
through terrain. You can get there eventually.

00:26:52.564 --> 00:26:55.644
You're just gonna lose 90, 95%
of your guys along the way."

00:26:56.844 --> 00:27:00.744
That's generally what we've seen from
Russia over the course of the last at

00:27:00.964 --> 00:27:04.904
least six months, maybe
a full year. Ukraine

00:27:04.964 --> 00:27:08.744
has been going about it in a different manner,
especially with some of their recent counteroffensive

00:27:08.804 --> 00:27:08.964
stuff.

00:27:09.664 --> 00:27:16.564
- The way I look at it is first that in 2025,
this war really began to be much more

00:27:17.404 --> 00:27:21.044
of a tug of war over who has
superiority in this drone

00:27:21.084 --> 00:27:24.244
engagement zone, which forces
locally call the kill zone.

00:27:25.024 --> 00:27:28.644
And the side that is able to suppress
the other side's drone units

00:27:29.044 --> 00:27:32.824
through drone strikes, traditional
artillery strikes, for Russia

00:27:32.904 --> 00:27:36.384
also heavy use of glide bombs,
standoff glide bombs launched by

00:27:36.684 --> 00:27:40.564
Russian fixed wing aircraft,
that side is then able to

00:27:40.624 --> 00:27:43.864
push the drone support further
away from the front line,

00:27:44.964 --> 00:27:48.884
and then by displacing it, then
be able to actually advance in

00:27:48.944 --> 00:27:52.564
that way. And so a lot of it's been a tug
of war actually over who has superiority

00:27:53.344 --> 00:27:57.084
in drones and fire support. And increasingly
on the Ukrainian side, where we see

00:27:57.124 --> 00:28:01.064
successful offensive operations,
it's because you have,

00:28:01.384 --> 00:28:05.504
a very well thought through plan to
set the conditions for the operation,

00:28:06.364 --> 00:28:10.024
begin to suppress Russian drone

00:28:10.104 --> 00:28:13.964
units, begin to interdict
logistics behind the drone units

00:28:14.284 --> 00:28:15.944
so that they don't get
sufficient supplies,

00:28:16.884 --> 00:28:20.804
begin to figure out how to potentially
channel them by essentially

00:28:20.924 --> 00:28:24.844
pushing them into certain zones of operations where you
can then localize, figure out where they're going to

00:28:24.864 --> 00:28:26.124
be when you're going to do the assault,

00:28:27.024 --> 00:28:30.764
then having a coordinated strike plan for how
you're going to suppress them during the assault,

00:28:31.684 --> 00:28:35.384
and then now you've actually
established the conditions for your

00:28:35.423 --> 00:28:37.584
forces to advance with some mobility

00:28:38.784 --> 00:28:42.764
and to dramatically reduce your losses.
Because Ukraine cannot afford to

00:28:42.804 --> 00:28:46.544
fight the way Russia does. It doesn't have the
manpower, and it doesn't... Even if it did have the

00:28:46.604 --> 00:28:50.564
manpower, it's not a question of numbers, it's
a question of culture. Ukraine would not treat

00:28:50.604 --> 00:28:54.404
its forces in the same way. It wouldn't
accept those kind of losses of

00:28:54.444 --> 00:28:56.964
its own troops.

00:28:57.904 --> 00:29:01.764
and so you've seen a couple different approaches
on the Ukrainian side probably over the past year,

00:29:02.064 --> 00:29:05.224
and different assault units experimenting
with them. You know, you have,

00:29:06.624 --> 00:29:10.264
uh, I think some advanced
drone assault units like

00:29:10.484 --> 00:29:14.444
Code Nine Two and First Separate Assault
Regiment that often work together, who are

00:29:14.444 --> 00:29:21.524
essentially using a combination of drones
and very well-equipped and adapted

00:29:22.284 --> 00:29:25.684
mechanized formations to try
to return maneuver to the

00:29:25.724 --> 00:29:29.444
battlefield and try to take
terrain in a more mobile

00:29:30.044 --> 00:29:33.924
way, do it faster, rather than
having a kind of slow-moving,

00:29:34.004 --> 00:29:35.044
grinding, creeping offensive.

00:29:35.884 --> 00:29:39.724
And you've seen all the way
down to maybe the opposite

00:29:39.764 --> 00:29:43.644
side of the spectrum, air assault brigades
very effectively employing three-man

00:29:43.724 --> 00:29:47.644
fire teams with drone support,
systematically clearing tree line

00:29:47.704 --> 00:29:51.624
by tree line by tree line, slow
and steady, but keeping their

00:29:51.664 --> 00:29:55.644
casualties low. This would be in
the South and Zaporizhzhia. And on

00:29:55.704 --> 00:29:59.644
a broad front, still over time, over
the course of this year, taking back

00:29:59.704 --> 00:30:03.624
a significant amount of terrain. And a bunch
of other methods in between. So I won't

00:30:03.664 --> 00:30:07.644
say that there's been one method, but different
units experimenting with different ways of doing it.

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:11.760
And on the Russian side, as Preston said,

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:16.260
It's a combination of mechanized
light motorized assault

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:19.900
sometimes, but increasingly Russians
are using mechanized assault simply to

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:23.860
consume the attention of Ukrainian
drone units, to distract them or

00:30:23.920 --> 00:30:27.820
try to basically get them to focus
on the mechanized assault so that

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:31.740
light motorized assaults can get through,
can get past their initial positions,

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:35.660
all these soldiers on motorcycles and

00:30:35.720 --> 00:30:39.440
ATVs, and even believe it or not, I've
seen Ladas and things of that nature.

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:45.180
- For the infiltration side, like
literally soldiers on motorcycles-

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:47.300
... trying to gain position-

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:51.320
... so they can then attack the supply
chains to force Ukrainian forces to

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:51.760
push back.

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:55.880
- Let me maybe even take one minute just to
explain why that's, that can be a challenge for

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:59.900
Ukraine, 'cause it may sound to someone
listening to this a bit preposterous, right?

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:04.060
So let me offer why that can pose a
challenge, and sometimes light motorized

00:31:04.100 --> 00:31:08.040
attacks have proven more successful than traditional
mechanized attacks. Because mechanized attacks are

00:31:08.140 --> 00:31:11.860
a small number of vehicles. Typically,
the leading vehicle has a big trawl

00:31:12.060 --> 00:31:15.820
to clear mines. You know, Ukrainian
drones can easily gang up

00:31:15.900 --> 00:31:19.760
on it and then start isolating the
column. And light motorized attacks is a

00:31:19.820 --> 00:31:23.800
whole number of soldiers. It could be 100 motorcycles,
it could be more than 100 motorcycles, who are

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:28.900
trying to use speed to overcome
this sort of engagement zone.

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:33.700
The reason why that can be an issue sometimes, even
though most of them get taken out, I want to be clear,

00:31:33.780 --> 00:31:38.540
most of them are not successful. But
Ukrainian forward positions are often

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:43.760
three-man posts. They're not even really firing
position observation posts. They're essentially

00:31:43.800 --> 00:31:47.760
pickets with considerable separation
between them of several hundred

00:31:47.880 --> 00:31:51.620
meters, right? So Ukraine might
have three of these for a

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:54.860
kilometer. That means there's considerable
space between them, so you could

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:56.300
drive through.

00:31:57.180 --> 00:32:00.780
Then behind them, there might be secondary positions,
but they're still pretty sparse in between.

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:05.840
Most of the units involved in the fight
are essentially supporting units,

00:32:05.980 --> 00:32:09.720
different type of fire support,
drone support, traditional artillery

00:32:09.820 --> 00:32:16.659
fires, logistics, what have you. Many
Ukrainian units don't really have the manpower

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:20.880
to merely launch a
counterattack if Russian

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:24.920
forces break through. Some do, some
have a quick reaction force ready,

00:32:25.240 --> 00:32:29.200
but for some, the manpower isn't necessarily
there. So it can be a challenge if units

00:32:29.240 --> 00:32:32.700
do get through and they begin to accumulate.
And what can happen, and this is how

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:34.120
infiltration works for Russia,

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:38.520
even though it's incredibly costly. You
can imagine 10 soldiers going forward,

00:32:39.600 --> 00:32:43.420
and seven of them don't make it,
but three do, and they found the

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:47.400
path in between positions. So now
the people that sent them know

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:51.160
that this is the gap in Ukrainian positions,
and they can then daisy chain send the

00:32:51.220 --> 00:32:55.100
next group down this path.
Now, somewhere behind

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:58.780
Ukrainian lines are now three Russian
troops, but maybe it's five, six,

00:32:58.900 --> 00:32:59.960
seven. Now they're accumulating.

00:33:00.820 --> 00:33:04.240
Well, behind those positions, if you
get three to five kilometers back,

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:08.360
now you're reaching the first drone
reconnaissance units that are flying Mavics.

00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:12.400
They are not prepared to receive an enemy
assault team. They are flying drones,

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:16.600
okay? So for them, this is already an
issue because if Russian assault troops,

00:33:16.960 --> 00:33:20.900
if only three or four or five of them are
operating your depth, and Ukrainian units do

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:24.920
try to hunt them down, because if they
let any number escape, then they get

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:28.420
to the drone units, then you start losing
your drone units, your support personnel.

00:33:28.540 --> 00:33:31.880
It is a real issue in the battle space. So

00:33:32.960 --> 00:33:36.860
that's essentially how infiltration works
over time, although it's incredibly

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:39.740
costly for Russia. It's a grim calculus.

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:44.560
But it's a combination of that and
also trying to fight for superiority

00:33:45.140 --> 00:33:49.000
in drone employment overall. Who
has more drones? Who has more drone

00:33:49.060 --> 00:33:53.020
units in the fight? Who has more fire
support? Who's able to suppress whose drone

00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:57.320
units to then allow their offensive
tactics to have some purchase and success?

00:33:57.860 --> 00:34:01.620
- Quick pause. There's coffee downstairs
for people just listening to this.

00:34:01.840 --> 00:34:04.800
This is late at night. We're
pushing through. This is amazing.

00:34:05.900 --> 00:34:09.840
Okay, we're back. Uh, I guess I should
also ask, what kind of drones are we

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:14.860
talking about? So I just pulled out
the, what is this? The Mini Pro-

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:15.760
- It's a great one.

00:34:15.780 --> 00:34:18.140
- ... DJI drones. Uh, five.

00:34:18.680 --> 00:34:22.200
How many drones like this are there?
And also, I should mention that

00:34:22.660 --> 00:34:26.140
some of them have a cable for
them, right? Fiber optic cable.

00:34:26.660 --> 00:34:29.739
And I think Preston, you've
talked about throughout all this.

00:34:29.780 --> 00:34:33.720
At first, you were skeptical when you visited
initially, but then you kind of saw the,

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:37.780
the wisdom, the effectiveness
of the, of fiber optic drones.

00:34:38.260 --> 00:34:42.120
- The fiber optics are terrifying in a lot of
ways. But to answer your question, the Mavics,

00:34:42.240 --> 00:34:46.160
those are still utilized. All things considered,
they're relatively expensive compared to some

00:34:46.220 --> 00:34:50.160
other systems that Ukraine has.
And those are not manufactured

00:34:50.220 --> 00:34:53.600
in Ukraine. And a lot of what they're using
right now, they're building themselves.

00:34:54.620 --> 00:34:58.460
So FPVs are a bit bigger than
that, but the term drone is so

00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:01.920
broad right now. I mean, all
the way to describe effectively

00:35:02.380 --> 00:35:06.340
missiles that Russia is firing, and Ukraine
is firing into Russia, that are the size

00:35:06.380 --> 00:35:09.920
of cars. So we've got everything
from very, very big to

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:14.080
very small and everything in
between. The fiber optic one is

00:35:14.360 --> 00:35:18.040
interesting. So one of the major defenses
against drones, and we use it in the U.S.

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:21.960
military, is jamming, electronic
warfare, making it to where

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:25.620
these munitions or anything doesn't
function in a certain area.

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:30.400
And I think it was Russia originally that
did it. Mike can correct me here, but they

00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:34.360
certainly rolled it out relatively
quickly, and they attached a

00:35:34.440 --> 00:35:38.420
very thin fiber optic cable
that spooled out of the drone

00:35:39.060 --> 00:35:42.660
back to the operator. And what
that did then was it brought that

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:46.580
signal internal, and the
jammers stopped working.

00:35:47.080 --> 00:35:50.800
So all of the electronic warfare in the
world isn't doing anything. That drone flies

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:54.660
directly through it. Now, the limitations
are that there's a cable behind this drone,

00:35:55.440 --> 00:35:59.360
That as thin as it is,
it does have some weight

00:35:59.420 --> 00:36:01.220
to it, which means a smaller payload or a-

00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:03.780
... smaller distance or shorter
distance that the drone can fly.

00:36:04.660 --> 00:36:07.920
Those can get hung up at times. But

00:36:08.840 --> 00:36:12.380
- We saw Russia roll that out. Ukraine
quickly matched it, and that is a

00:36:12.740 --> 00:36:16.660
serious threat right now at longer
and longer distances. I think we've

00:36:16.719 --> 00:36:19.300
seen them upwards of around 50
kilometers- ... at this point.

00:36:19.740 --> 00:36:23.640
- The first time I heard this, I
thought... It didn't compute in

00:36:23.680 --> 00:36:26.080
my head. So you just said 50 kilometers.

00:36:26.380 --> 00:36:31.000
So that's a very long cable. It's
a drone connected by a cable.

00:36:31.200 --> 00:36:32.480
It didn't make any sense to me-

00:36:32.560 --> 00:36:32.620
- I know.

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:36.400
- ... like that could be a reality. Maybe
this is a good place to kind of ask,

00:36:36.620 --> 00:36:40.000
like, the nature of war,
which is figuring out

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:44.260
different technologies that are actually
effective on the ground. Like figuring

00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:46.280
out what's effective
even if it seems crazy.

00:36:46.460 --> 00:36:49.900
- Yeah, I mean, as Preston said, this...
Ukrainians initially experimented with them,

00:36:50.360 --> 00:36:54.200
didn't adopt them, but then Russia started
adopting them pretty heavily after

00:36:54.380 --> 00:36:58.100
employing them in Kursk. When Ukraine
had the Kursk offensive, that's when

00:36:58.120 --> 00:37:01.820
Russian units really started first using fiber
optic cables. They saw them as being effective.

00:37:01.960 --> 00:37:04.120
I'm demonstrating this as a case.

00:37:04.580 --> 00:37:09.340
As an example of a case at least. And once
they saw that it was quite effective,

00:37:10.160 --> 00:37:13.960
impossible to jam, right? Because you literally
have a cable attached to a drone, that the drone

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:16.720
lays behind itself as it
flies. Think of it like,

00:37:17.520 --> 00:37:21.220
A very thin fiber optic cable
filament, like on a fishing rod, the

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:22.260
reel that you would use.

00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:23.960
- It's about that width,
too. It's very thin.

00:37:24.020 --> 00:37:27.920
- It's very thin, and it's a spool like
a reel on a fishing rod. That's,

00:37:28.220 --> 00:37:32.140
that's literally what the thing is on it. It's a
spool, and it's just this cable's coming out behind.

00:37:32.480 --> 00:37:36.400
You have to fly it carefully. You have to fly it
slowly. You can't fly it like your the drone you

00:37:36.420 --> 00:37:37.040
had here, this,

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:41.960
this Mini Pro. You have to be more cautious because
you have to think how the cable's being laid

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:45.880
on trees, on roads, on other things. You have
to remember that you have a cable, not a

00:37:45.920 --> 00:37:49.840
radio signal connecting yourself to it.
But nonetheless, it's quite effective.

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:54.800
And then it spread from the units
using in Kursk because other

00:37:54.840 --> 00:37:58.540
units saw it as a successful practice. Once
people see something that's effective, they say,

00:37:59.080 --> 00:38:01.620
"I want to try that, too." Then
it spreads to other units.

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:04.760
Then, as Russians were
using it, Ukrainian units,

00:38:06.280 --> 00:38:09.620
Ukrainian units began adopting it
as well. Not at the same rate.

00:38:10.500 --> 00:38:14.020
Fiber optic cable drones are still a
fairly small percentage of the overall

00:38:14.200 --> 00:38:18.079
tactical strike drones used, and I'm not sure.
I hate to say I'm making this number up,

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:24.980
but it's 15% or less, I think, overall.
That said they play a very important

00:38:25.040 --> 00:38:30.780
role because you can't jam them, and also
they're much more difficult to detect.

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:39.460
Ukrainian units, everyone commonly has
systems of detection force drones that

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:45.120
are flown via an analog radio
signal because it's open

00:38:45.140 --> 00:38:48.840
and it's easy to intercept. So you often have
a device, for example, it's nicknamed the

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:52.880
Chuika, one type of it. I've always had one
in the vehicle when driving. There's three

00:38:52.920 --> 00:38:58.020
antennas coming out of it which receive
signal on three different frequency

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:02.680
spectrums. And if there's a
drone coming towards you,

00:39:03.440 --> 00:39:07.320
the way things work is once
a drone that's using an

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:11.300
analog radio signal is closer to you than
to the operator, the signal strength

00:39:11.360 --> 00:39:15.060
can be stronger to you, so you can start to receive
the signal from it, and you have like a little

00:39:15.140 --> 00:39:19.100
screen that you can see the video feed of an
enemy drone coming. And with a fiber optic cable

00:39:19.180 --> 00:39:22.120
drone, you have nothing in terms of warning
because you can't receive that signal.

00:39:22.640 --> 00:39:27.720
But the way I look at it is that there's
been about a three to four-month cycle of

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:31.680
evolution of technology and tactics
in this war between Russian and

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:37.300
Ukrainian forces, and they iterate fairly
quickly. So both sides have enough

00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:41.800
defense industrial capacity, enough
capital, enough folks working on this,

00:39:42.520 --> 00:39:45.820
that if one side's using something and
it's demonstrated to be effective, they

00:39:46.080 --> 00:39:49.880
assume and anticipate that the
other side is going to begin

00:39:50.360 --> 00:39:54.200
to imitate them and adopt those
technology capabilities. And usually the

00:39:54.220 --> 00:39:57.880
interaction between the two, and I'm very
broad-brushing this, is when Ukraine has often

00:39:58.020 --> 00:40:01.840
led in terms of tactical development
and bottom-up innovation

00:40:02.300 --> 00:40:06.020
because of the way Ukrainian units
all experiment on their own and

00:40:06.220 --> 00:40:09.960
all have... They're kind of all defense
startups. They all have kind of their

00:40:10.120 --> 00:40:13.860
own R&amp;D section. They have their
own defense shop that's, or

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:20.220
company that's building drones
for them. So technology tends to,

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:24.879
you know, bloom or like,
I remember when EW was

00:40:24.939 --> 00:40:28.780
really exploding in 2023, 2024, and
folks were calling it like an electronic

00:40:28.840 --> 00:40:31.400
warfare jungle because all these
different systems were appearing.

00:40:32.360 --> 00:40:37.260
And so Ukraine tended to innovate
faster. Russian military was slower,

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:42.060
but Russia had in some ways more
capacity and tended to be better

00:40:42.100 --> 00:40:45.880
at scaling a solution once they
identified it, and this was Ukraine's

00:40:45.920 --> 00:40:49.740
frustration. That once the Russian
military identified something as

00:40:49.780 --> 00:40:52.440
effective and they saw that
Ukraine is doing something well,

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:57.120
they would then allocate capital, industrial
capacity, scale it, deploy it across the

00:40:57.180 --> 00:41:01.140
force in a standardized
way a bit faster. If I

00:41:01.160 --> 00:41:04.780
can add something to this, Lex, 'cause
it's an aspect of this that we just

00:41:05.040 --> 00:41:08.960
kind of... And Mike did it here, and I do it all
the time, too, is you just rattle it off like it's

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:10.880
normal, and it's so far from normal.

00:41:10.900 --> 00:41:15.420
The Chuika drone detector, what that
means is it looks like an old Game Boy.

00:41:15.460 --> 00:41:19.140
It has a screen on it. So you're driving
down the road in Ukraine, watching the

00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:23.380
screen. It turns on, and it is
the enemy's view of the drone.

00:41:23.800 --> 00:41:25.300
Mm-hmm. Is it your vehicle?

00:41:25.880 --> 00:41:29.740
Or is it a different vehicle? And you will
watch, you can watch as the enemy drone comes

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:33.560
in at you. For soldiers in tree lines,
they might be watching and trying to

00:41:33.600 --> 00:41:37.540
decide, "Does that drone see us? Do
we need to move? It doesn't see us.

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:38.480
It's looking the other direction."

00:41:39.420 --> 00:41:43.180
It's literally watching the feed
of the Russian soldiers that are

00:41:43.240 --> 00:41:44.400
hunting you. That is

00:41:45.680 --> 00:41:49.180
just about everybody along the front has that
right now. It's just a weird dynamic that

00:41:50.100 --> 00:41:51.020
brand-new to war.

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:55.160
- It beeps and when it picks
up the signal, and so when

00:41:55.200 --> 00:41:58.960
you're holding it, the... it's not a
pleasant feeling for everybody else in the

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:02.300
vehicle that you can see it's beeping, it's
picking up the signal on the enemy drone.

00:42:02.900 --> 00:42:06.820
And I have had times where folks seeing me looking
at the screen, it's a small screen, looking at

00:42:06.860 --> 00:42:07.280
the screen

00:42:08.260 --> 00:42:11.940
of the enemy drone, and then looking, you know,
like, to your right to kind of look at your

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:14.020
surroundings to see, is this

00:42:14.792 --> 00:42:18.452
- You know, is this like close to me? Is
it kind of looking in the same area or

00:42:18.512 --> 00:42:21.532
not? So it is exactly as
Preston describes it.

00:42:21.792 --> 00:42:25.672
- So there's a surreal aspect to
this. It feels almost like video

00:42:25.772 --> 00:42:29.312
game-like with real life,
life and death consequences.

00:42:29.892 --> 00:42:33.812
The soldiers on both sides are looking at
screens, and then the whole world is looking

00:42:33.832 --> 00:42:37.152
at the screens that the soldier's
filming the thing. It just feels

00:42:38.112 --> 00:42:42.032
like the nature of war is completely
different. Can you speak to the degree

00:42:42.132 --> 00:42:46.092
this war represents modern
warfare and the future of

00:42:46.132 --> 00:42:49.452
modern warfare, and to what
degree does it represents

00:42:49.992 --> 00:42:52.252
leftover from old way of fighting wars?

00:42:52.792 --> 00:42:54.252
- I think we're at a transition point-

00:42:54.432 --> 00:42:58.792
... right now. This war
represents Russia and Ukraine.

00:42:59.792 --> 00:43:03.772
It is not representative of what every war
in the world would look like should it spark

00:43:03.852 --> 00:43:07.792
today, tomorrow or next year.
There's just very unique aspects to

00:43:07.832 --> 00:43:11.652
this. The lack of air superiority on
Russia's part plays a major role in how the

00:43:11.692 --> 00:43:15.572
battlefield looks today. That may or
may not be the situation that the U.S.

00:43:15.692 --> 00:43:19.612
military or NATO per se would be
in, in another conflict. Maybe

00:43:19.652 --> 00:43:23.972
it would, but there's a lot of
dynamics at play here. So, it's...

00:43:25.332 --> 00:43:29.912
We haven't seen this in our lifetime,
where we have... You know, one of the,

00:43:31.392 --> 00:43:35.312
one of the aspects of warfare is when people
are fighting for their life day in and day

00:43:35.392 --> 00:43:38.792
out, they tend to come up with
really good ways to stay alive and

00:43:38.852 --> 00:43:40.952
alternatively really good
ways to kill the enemy.

00:43:41.832 --> 00:43:43.932
Ways that you don't come
up with when you're just

00:43:44.892 --> 00:43:48.412
between wars and thinking about what might
be a good solution. So we are seeing

00:43:49.072 --> 00:43:52.732
rapid innovation in both Russia
and Ukraine, and these are

00:43:53.092 --> 00:43:56.932
modern economies with modern
technology. We've never, we haven't

00:43:56.992 --> 00:44:00.612
seen this, in my lifetime at
least. You know, the Taliban were

00:44:00.632 --> 00:44:04.452
innovating. Al-Qaeda was innovating. ISIS,
they were innovating. The enemies that we

00:44:04.492 --> 00:44:08.372
fought were innovating. They didn't have
the scale to do it. They didn't have this

00:44:08.412 --> 00:44:15.692
technology to do it. So we, I think we
jumped decades in terms of what warfare will

00:44:15.732 --> 00:44:18.832
be in a very short period of time
because of where it's playing out.

00:44:19.352 --> 00:44:23.032
- As a thought experiment, how would the United
States military fight this differently?

00:44:23.392 --> 00:44:27.372
Say there was no nuclear weapons on the
table. You said something, air superiority.

00:44:27.432 --> 00:44:28.052
What does that mean?

00:44:28.472 --> 00:44:32.372
- So air superiority is the ability
to conduct operations in an

00:44:32.412 --> 00:44:36.392
airspace pretty, pretty much
at will. So very similar to

00:44:36.532 --> 00:44:40.472
Iraq and Afghanistan as we were fighting
there. We had air power overhead at all

00:44:40.512 --> 00:44:44.052
times. They were really at no
risk of being shot down or even

00:44:44.112 --> 00:44:47.952
suppressed to the point of not being
able to carry out operations. What

00:44:47.992 --> 00:44:51.652
you're seeing in Ukraine right
now is air denial. Neither

00:44:51.912 --> 00:44:55.692
side is flying aircraft into enemy territory,
and that's been the case for a long time.

00:44:56.492 --> 00:44:59.932
The U.S. military is
very much built around

00:45:00.372 --> 00:45:03.812
air power and delivering long
range precision munitions,

00:45:04.372 --> 00:45:08.132
And a lot of munitions from B1
bombers, B2 bombers, things like

00:45:08.192 --> 00:45:10.572
that onto enemy positions.

00:45:10.772 --> 00:45:12.092
- But by the way, small tangent,

00:45:13.032 --> 00:45:16.812
which branch of the military is responsible
for drones? I would love to know,

00:45:17.432 --> 00:45:21.372
like you were at West Point, is there
like set of courses now on drones,

00:45:21.452 --> 00:45:22.352
on drone warfare?

00:45:22.392 --> 00:45:22.812
- They're getting there.

00:45:22.872 --> 00:45:26.532
- So is that part of the Air Force? Is that part of
air power? Does that count as air power, or is that

00:45:26.772 --> 00:45:26.992
not?

00:45:27.312 --> 00:45:28.092
- We're figuring it out-

00:45:28.112 --> 00:45:31.832
... I think is the best way to put it, and
this is the thing, that it's happening so

00:45:31.892 --> 00:45:35.792
fast, right? They haven't had the
opportunity to create new courses

00:45:35.832 --> 00:45:39.792
and then put cadets through this for three
or four years at a point because there's new

00:45:39.812 --> 00:45:43.292
technology right now that wasn't necessarily
being utilized just 18 months ago.

00:45:43.392 --> 00:45:47.192
So the Pentagon is working on this.
We've seen a lot of kind of press

00:45:47.232 --> 00:45:51.052
releases and positive statements about
the direction we're headed. There's

00:45:51.092 --> 00:45:54.512
a drone dominance program right now
that the military is spearheading,

00:45:54.992 --> 00:45:58.912
trying to identify small
manufacturers that can

00:45:58.952 --> 00:46:02.852
be agile and come up with new solutions
to field to the broader force.

00:46:03.892 --> 00:46:07.732
We're trying to catch up in a lot
of ways. In terms of who owns it,

00:46:08.492 --> 00:46:12.212
I don't know that that answer is there yet. It
seems like everybody's got a little piece of it.

00:46:12.212 --> 00:46:14.612
Uh, but that's part of it. We're trying
to work our way through it right now.

00:46:14.652 --> 00:46:18.452
- But the U.S. military is embracing
this new small drone. Like to,

00:46:18.592 --> 00:46:25.052
to what degree is this an intermediate stage
of having these kind of ad hoc drones?

00:46:25.892 --> 00:46:29.832
Is this an intermediate stage of something
else that's coming, or is this going to

00:46:29.872 --> 00:46:33.652
be the new way of fighting
these kinds of wars?

00:46:34.452 --> 00:46:38.412
- That's a good question. There's
debate whether an analogy

00:46:38.452 --> 00:46:41.292
is maybe like aviation
of World War I, where

00:46:42.332 --> 00:46:47.012
we see the Russia-Ukraine war as a
transitional war, like Preston described it.

00:46:47.952 --> 00:46:51.832
It starts off as a actually
fairly traditional 20th century

00:46:51.952 --> 00:46:55.852
war with two forces that are
principally artillery armies, that are

00:46:55.892 --> 00:46:59.792
both successor armies to the Soviet
military, which was an artillery army.

00:47:00.672 --> 00:47:04.592
And much is decided in the war through
interaction artillery, armor, anti-tank guided

00:47:04.632 --> 00:47:08.572
missiles, and a lot of modernized Soviet
equipment. If you were around in '70s

00:47:08.592 --> 00:47:12.572
and 1980s, you would have recognized most of
the things on the battlefield in 2022 through

00:47:12.732 --> 00:47:13.212
2024.

00:47:14.072 --> 00:47:17.932
In fact, most of the equipment NATO sent
was also what NATO had made all the way

00:47:17.952 --> 00:47:22.472
from '60s to, you know, the '90s. Um,

00:47:23.672 --> 00:47:27.592
and then drones evolve quite
rapidly and really start

00:47:27.612 --> 00:47:31.352
to mature over the course of this war.
And the question is, are we close

00:47:31.412 --> 00:47:35.372
to this final stage of maturity, or will
we see a substantial reduction of how

00:47:35.392 --> 00:47:39.232
much manpower is involved? Would drones
become much more autonomous to the

00:47:39.312 --> 00:47:45.032
benefit of both a different set
of technology and artificial

00:47:45.052 --> 00:47:48.832
intelligence being used for navigation,
guidance, what have you? Um,

00:47:49.872 --> 00:47:53.632
and how relevant is that
to the wars we plan to

00:47:53.672 --> 00:47:56.792
fight and also the way we want
to fight? Every military has

00:47:57.712 --> 00:48:01.652
its own conception of its contingencies,
the scenarios that it has

00:48:01.672 --> 00:48:05.372
in mind, and also an idea of how it wants
to fight. And here's the challenge,

00:48:05.472 --> 00:48:09.112
because first, how do we know
when we're looking at a war that

00:48:10.272 --> 00:48:13.592
what we see is generalizable to other wars?
Every war has its own context, right?

00:48:13.972 --> 00:48:17.372
Its own geography. This was a principally
land war with an air and a sea component.

00:48:18.016 --> 00:48:21.835
Second, there's always an issue of when
you're looking at prolonged conventional war,

00:48:21.956 --> 00:48:25.856
what part of the war do you want to draw
lessons from? Because most wars of this type,

00:48:25.936 --> 00:48:29.556
they start out with this dynamic,
intense phase, the clash of

00:48:29.596 --> 00:48:33.416
forces, reconciliation of military
plans. But then they often do

00:48:33.456 --> 00:48:36.956
become positional, attritional
in nature, right? Forces dig in.

00:48:37.536 --> 00:48:41.336
There's way more troops on the battlefield.
Everything's mined. It's very hard to break

00:48:41.356 --> 00:48:41.636
through.

00:48:42.636 --> 00:48:46.496
And drones really came into their own more
in these latter phases, right? So sometimes

00:48:46.536 --> 00:48:50.535
people make the mistake of
they look at the latter phase,

00:48:50.676 --> 00:48:54.556
the late parts of a war, and think,
"Okay, this is how the next war will

00:48:54.696 --> 00:48:58.496
start." But the next opening phase
of a war is often different than

00:48:58.536 --> 00:49:02.476
kind of the middle phase where both sides enter
this dynamic that they're trying to break out

00:49:02.496 --> 00:49:07.256
of. I think for the United States,
part of our challenge is that

00:49:08.396 --> 00:49:12.376
we actually are tactically
an incredibly skilled force,

00:49:12.656 --> 00:49:16.396
right? We spend a lot of money on our
military, on the training for our

00:49:16.436 --> 00:49:17.676
military, and the quality
of our personnel.

00:49:18.596 --> 00:49:22.095
And Preston's absolutely right. We
spend a tremendous amount of funding

00:49:22.396 --> 00:49:26.316
on the expeditionary air component
of our force because it's the best

00:49:26.356 --> 00:49:30.336
combination of mobility and firepower on the
battlefield, which then enables the rest of the force

00:49:31.436 --> 00:49:35.316
to conduct combined arms maneuver. And
what I mean by that is if you think

00:49:35.376 --> 00:49:38.836
of the different combat arms,
right, infantry, armor, artillery,

00:49:39.716 --> 00:49:43.556
if you think of aviation in
combination with that, that force

00:49:43.696 --> 00:49:47.076
works together, right? All those pieces

00:49:47.576 --> 00:49:51.516
are interacting with each other,
supporting each other dynamically on the

00:49:51.556 --> 00:49:55.156
battlefield. And one of the big advantages
of our force is not just technology,

00:49:55.496 --> 00:49:59.356
not just training of personnel,
but the fact that we can run

00:49:59.376 --> 00:50:05.336
a complex operation, have these pieces
working together, at least ideally

00:50:06.436 --> 00:50:09.996
and be able to achieve
a much greater effect

00:50:10.636 --> 00:50:13.616
from the fact that these pieces are
interacting, they're much better coordinated.

00:50:13.716 --> 00:50:17.696
And lastly, one of the biggest separators
of modern militaries is whether

00:50:17.696 --> 00:50:21.436
or not you can do the things you're
doing in exercise or in a small

00:50:21.556 --> 00:50:25.416
scenario against a permissive environment,
like let's say you're fighting the Taliban.

00:50:25.456 --> 00:50:29.316
I'm not saying that wasn't a
challenge, but nonetheless it's

00:50:29.356 --> 00:50:33.256
quite different having a fight like that
than facing the largest conventional war

00:50:33.356 --> 00:50:34.896
Europe has seen since World War II, right?

00:50:36.076 --> 00:50:39.816
The ability to scale
operations, complex operations,

00:50:40.596 --> 00:50:44.536
is a big limiter for other militaries. In fact, the
Russian military really struggled with this extensively at

00:50:44.556 --> 00:50:48.536
the beginning of this war.
To maybe make an analogy to

00:50:48.576 --> 00:50:52.556
folks not in military affairs,
it's one thing to run a

00:50:52.616 --> 00:50:56.496
business with, you know, 10 employees. It's
another thing to run a corporation with 10

00:50:56.536 --> 00:51:00.396
or 20,000, right? The complexity
scales. And I don't know

00:51:00.396 --> 00:51:04.256
anything about running corporations, but I
imagine there's a great deal more complexity to

00:51:04.316 --> 00:51:08.196
it than something like a small business,
even though in principle you're still

00:51:08.256 --> 00:51:12.056
running a business operation. So I use
this kind of analogy to explain that

00:51:12.696 --> 00:51:15.396
this is in some ways how the United
States is very much advantaged.

00:51:16.216 --> 00:51:20.036
But if you're interested, there are some open-ended
questions based on what we saw in this war

00:51:21.236 --> 00:51:24.856
if we need to adapt, if we need to change
the way we operate. There is a degree of

00:51:24.876 --> 00:51:28.716
uncertainty if our traditional advantages
will offer the same benefits in a future

00:51:28.816 --> 00:51:32.716
war if that war features this technology on
the battlefield. And one thing we do know

00:51:33.216 --> 00:51:37.196
is that our opponents like Russia do plan to
fight in this way. We already have a sense of how

00:51:37.236 --> 00:51:40.676
they plan to fight because we see how they're
fighting in this war, right? As, you know, that,

00:51:41.356 --> 00:51:44.936
as Gibson used to say, "The future's already
here. It's not evenly distributed," right?

00:51:45.016 --> 00:51:48.536
So we already see that this, you
know, this is already here on the

00:51:48.576 --> 00:51:49.556
battlefield in Europe.

00:51:50.076 --> 00:51:51.836
- An absurdly large question, but

00:51:53.036 --> 00:51:56.736
who is winning this war right
now? And maybe a better

00:51:57.216 --> 00:52:01.656
version of that question is what is a
good measure of how to score the war

00:52:02.476 --> 00:52:06.176
in terms of the military balance of power
and effectiveness? So territory gain,

00:52:06.296 --> 00:52:09.736
casualties, quality of each
army, maybe other measures.

00:52:10.136 --> 00:52:11.516
Who's winning this war right now?

00:52:11.776 --> 00:52:15.476
- I'll tackle it. Honestly, Lex,
it's a, it's a question I-

00:52:15.856 --> 00:52:19.556
... generally avoid. I understand where the
question comes from. People want to have a

00:52:19.596 --> 00:52:23.396
very clear and concise where do things
stand right now. But the challenge is

00:52:23.896 --> 00:52:27.516
this isn't like a football game where
both teams, you're looking at the

00:52:27.556 --> 00:52:30.956
scoreboard, who has more points at the end of 60
minutes, and that's the winner. And you can say

00:52:31.336 --> 00:52:35.316
whoever has more points at 55 minutes,
they're that much closer, because

00:52:35.355 --> 00:52:38.795
Russia and Ukraine in this case are fighting
for different things. They have different

00:52:38.816 --> 00:52:42.716
objectives. You could overly simplify it
and say that Russia is trying to take

00:52:43.036 --> 00:52:45.236
Ukraine, and that Ukraine is trying
to maintain their sovereignty,

00:52:46.476 --> 00:52:50.856
but then you just go down a rabbit
hole of what is success or what is-

00:52:51.216 --> 00:52:54.676
... what is the fact that Ukraine
still exists today to a degree is

00:52:55.376 --> 00:52:58.736
winning. But they have lost
some territory that they

00:52:59.136 --> 00:53:03.196
don't appear on the verge of
getting back. That is not winning.

00:53:04.156 --> 00:53:07.676
So it's difficult. The other
thing I would say with that is

00:53:08.216 --> 00:53:12.116
when you look at historical
conflicts, say World War II, Nazi

00:53:12.216 --> 00:53:16.196
Germany was winning that war for the
majority of it until they weren't,

00:53:16.316 --> 00:53:20.276
and then they quickly lost.
Uh, Imperial Japan was winning

00:53:20.316 --> 00:53:24.256
much of World War II in the Pacific. They were
unstoppable if you looked at, if you took

00:53:24.296 --> 00:53:28.236
a snapshot in time, and then all of a
sudden things turned, they were losing,

00:53:28.416 --> 00:53:32.616
and they lost. So the winning,
losing in the middle of a conflict,

00:53:33.776 --> 00:53:37.576
I feel like you could paint that, honestly,
something like this, in whichever

00:53:37.616 --> 00:53:38.476
direction you would prefer.

00:53:38.896 --> 00:53:42.856
- Is it fair to say that either
side can collapse quickly, so

00:53:42.896 --> 00:53:44.196
tides can turn dramatically?

00:53:44.716 --> 00:53:48.516
- Historically, we have seen that. I don't
know that there's a lot of evidence right

00:53:48.556 --> 00:53:52.136
now. I think there was a lot
of expectations early on that

00:53:52.196 --> 00:53:55.996
Ukraine would be the country to
collapse, and there's been countless

00:53:56.176 --> 00:54:00.056
analysts just, you know, "They're on the verge. This
winter's gonna be it. This summer's gonna be it,"

00:54:00.116 --> 00:54:04.056
and they're still holding. There's
no indications that either one is on

00:54:04.096 --> 00:54:10.296
the verge of collapse, but some sort of
large change to dynamically shift the

00:54:10.336 --> 00:54:14.256
conflict. You know, the Prigozhin-Wagner
march on Moscow, there was the,

00:54:14.616 --> 00:54:18.436
the possibility there. Maybe that would've
been the thing that would've brought about not

00:54:18.456 --> 00:54:22.176
necessarily a Russian collapse, but a
Russian inability to continue to project

00:54:22.216 --> 00:54:24.256
power into Ukraine. But-

00:54:24.564 --> 00:54:28.544
- Black Swan event is probably a little
too much, but it's that type of thing

00:54:28.584 --> 00:54:32.784
that could precipitate a, a major change
in the direction the war is going.

00:54:33.624 --> 00:54:37.564
- Yeah. I don't see either side right now
being able to impose the kind of collapse I

00:54:37.584 --> 00:54:41.344
think you're thinking of. And part of the reason
is just the way they're fighting, and the

00:54:41.364 --> 00:54:45.204
interaction between the two forces
isn't really conducive to producing

00:54:45.264 --> 00:54:49.084
that. It's very hard to generate
the level of momentum and exploit

00:54:49.164 --> 00:54:50.624
battlefield conditions to really,

00:54:51.524 --> 00:54:56.044
to really kind of force a collapse on
the side. Although, as I often say,

00:54:57.204 --> 00:55:00.944
you know, we have to be mindful. Wars
unfold in phases, right? So there are these

00:55:00.984 --> 00:55:04.644
periods where one side has the initiative. Russia
held the initiative for a long period of time.

00:55:04.824 --> 00:55:08.724
Now in 2026, we see actually Ukraine
increasingly has the initiative on parts of the

00:55:08.804 --> 00:55:11.924
front and is imposing real challenges
on the Russian armed forces.

00:55:12.804 --> 00:55:16.724
You could say that for a time Ukraine
has been advantaged this year on

00:55:16.764 --> 00:55:20.544
the battlefield. The debate
we have as analysts is, is

00:55:20.604 --> 00:55:24.524
that going to be a lasting advantage, is
that a secular trend, or is that a window of

00:55:24.604 --> 00:55:28.504
opportunity for Ukraine? Because, just because
you're advantaged, like Russia might have been in

00:55:28.684 --> 00:55:35.064
'24 and '25, it doesn't mean that that will
hold, that will last. Um, you see that

00:55:37.164 --> 00:55:40.704
the way one side fights can reach
diminishing returns and become cost

00:55:40.744 --> 00:55:44.664
ineffective. That's what happened to
Russia in 2025. You see much of it

00:55:44.704 --> 00:55:48.664
is an interaction between the two
forces, adaptation, new technology comes

00:55:48.724 --> 00:55:52.624
along. Both sides are experimenting
with different ways to fight.

00:55:53.344 --> 00:55:57.284
And so this is... You know, I think for neither
of us is, this is not a cop-out. I think

00:55:57.324 --> 00:56:00.904
as analysts there is always a tendency
to admire the problem. But if you say

00:56:00.944 --> 00:56:04.564
somebody's winning, it often sets for
people, and certainly any audience,

00:56:04.704 --> 00:56:08.244
expectations. Most people think if you say
someone's winning, that that side's going to win.

00:56:08.704 --> 00:56:11.764
In the conventional world, it's very hard to
see things forward even a couple of months

00:56:12.364 --> 00:56:15.744
because so much is contingent
that you can only say

00:56:16.184 --> 00:56:19.264
Ukraine's advantaged on the ground
right now. But Ukraine also faces

00:56:20.104 --> 00:56:24.024
a very, very significant challenge
from the Russian strike campaign. None

00:56:24.064 --> 00:56:27.644
of us are sure how this winter is going to go.
We're not sure how cold the winter is going to be.

00:56:27.704 --> 00:56:28.664
That's a big factor, too.

00:56:29.564 --> 00:56:33.244
And other factors are at play.
What other wars will happen?

00:56:33.864 --> 00:56:37.724
The US war with Iran had
a material and political

00:56:37.864 --> 00:56:41.824
impact on this war, right? So we don't
know what other things will take

00:56:41.864 --> 00:56:45.824
place around the world that may shape this
war because there are all sorts of countries

00:56:46.284 --> 00:56:49.864
that are de facto material parties
to the war or very significantly

00:56:49.924 --> 00:56:53.784
engaged in supporting the countries
fighting this war. So there's

00:56:53.824 --> 00:56:55.704
just a great deal of factors to consider.

00:56:56.504 --> 00:57:00.044
- When you analyze the war,
maybe you can speak to

00:57:00.744 --> 00:57:04.584
the levels of analysis that are most
important to you that we should be

00:57:04.624 --> 00:57:08.484
thinking about. So I've spoken to a bunch
of historians like James Holland, who

00:57:08.564 --> 00:57:11.664
analyzes, for example, World War II,
the Western Front, the Eastern Front-

00:57:11.684 --> 00:57:15.584
Focused on the Western Front from all the
different levels, you know, tactical,

00:57:15.644 --> 00:57:19.504
strategic, operational. There's of
course the political, geopolitical.

00:57:19.584 --> 00:57:22.324
There's, you know, the supply
chain, all this kinda...

00:57:23.604 --> 00:57:27.204
Down to geography and weather, like
you mentioned winter. So what,

00:57:27.624 --> 00:57:30.984
what are some of the levels of analysis that
are important here in this particular war?

00:57:31.364 --> 00:57:34.264
- I mean, I think for a military
analyst, you know, there's,

00:57:35.364 --> 00:57:38.524
you know, at least three levels, and
then there's the central political level

00:57:39.324 --> 00:57:40.964
that's also central to understand. But

00:57:42.124 --> 00:57:46.104
the way I look at first, tactical level of
analysis, right? That's the nitty-gritty

00:57:46.204 --> 00:57:49.844
battlefield details, what's happening
on the ground in these specific fights.

00:57:50.924 --> 00:57:54.884
How are the two sides fighting at this tactical
level? How are force employed? We covered quite a

00:57:54.904 --> 00:57:58.804
bit of that in the conversation
already. How does infantry

00:57:58.844 --> 00:58:02.624
advance? How are drones being employed?
You know, correlation of forces

00:58:02.784 --> 00:58:06.744
and day-to-day battlefield changes.
They inform your understanding of how

00:58:06.784 --> 00:58:10.404
the war is being fought, and also they
help set your expectations for what's

00:58:10.464 --> 00:58:14.404
likely to happen in the months
ahead. They're also relevant because

00:58:15.064 --> 00:58:18.444
even though the war is not going to be
decided by who advances the next 5 or 10

00:58:18.524 --> 00:58:23.604
kilometers, that this tactical level war
very much informs the strategic. Why?

00:58:24.724 --> 00:58:28.584
It sets expectations among military
and political leaders for who

00:58:28.664 --> 00:58:32.504
has greater bargaining power, who has leverage,
who is succeeding on the battlefield,

00:58:32.564 --> 00:58:36.204
provides information to the negotiations
between the two sides as well, right?

00:58:36.964 --> 00:58:41.044
So to some extent, we can't overly fixate
on the map, but the map is important.

00:58:41.924 --> 00:58:48.544
And you can see who is adapting,
who is not adapting and actually is

00:58:48.584 --> 00:58:52.544
increasingly suffering greater casualties and
battlefield reversals, which in this case,

00:58:53.044 --> 00:58:53.904
2026 is Russia.

00:58:54.964 --> 00:58:58.704
Let's take the operational level. And
first, I'll say this is a thorny subject in

00:58:58.744 --> 00:59:02.484
military circles because I know plenty
of people military side that don't

00:59:02.524 --> 00:59:06.264
think there's a discrete operational level of
war. They think that there's operational art,

00:59:06.564 --> 00:59:09.344
but there isn't such thing as a
separate operational level of war.

00:59:09.364 --> 00:59:11.144
We should not get into
that discussion here.

00:59:11.764 --> 00:59:15.604
- Oh, so you're saying that some people listening to this
who are very angry hearing you even say operational

00:59:15.644 --> 00:59:17.044
like it's this discrete layer.

00:59:17.324 --> 00:59:21.124
- I would not say they were angry, but I can
imagine a host of army majors getting ready to

00:59:21.144 --> 00:59:22.464
debate this as I bring the topic up.

00:59:22.484 --> 00:59:22.724
- Okay. Okay.

00:59:22.744 --> 00:59:26.544
- So let's just stop it- ... and say, look,
there are different ways of looking at

00:59:27.384 --> 00:59:31.304
operational level of war, but there is such a
thing as operations and the conduct of operations,

00:59:31.404 --> 00:59:36.864
right? Which is important. And this
is much more the organization of

00:59:37.804 --> 00:59:41.644
intelligence, logistics, fire support,
the coordination of different units

00:59:42.464 --> 00:59:46.344
in a coherent action with a clear
objective, right? And ideally, the

00:59:46.424 --> 00:59:49.124
operations then link, um,

00:59:50.184 --> 00:59:53.864
what you're trying to achieve in terms of
the objectives of the operations with the

00:59:53.904 --> 00:59:57.524
political goals of the actual war,
right? Ideally, the military strategy

00:59:58.264 --> 01:00:02.144
takes the operations and connects them to the political
goals of the war. So you're not just conducting

01:00:02.224 --> 01:00:06.204
successful military operations, but they have a clear purpose
in terms of the political objectives you're trying to

01:00:06.244 --> 01:00:09.884
achieve. And operations are things
that are phased, they are planned,

01:00:10.524 --> 01:00:14.064
they involve coordinating a whole host of units,
but they have a clear objective behind them.

01:00:14.204 --> 01:00:17.004
They have a sequencing. You know, they've
thought through how they're going to

01:00:18.244 --> 01:00:21.964
set the conditions, how the breaching
force is going on, who's the second

01:00:22.084 --> 01:00:25.964
echelon. If you meet with success,
who's going to exploit that success?

01:00:26.364 --> 01:00:30.244
If you don't meet with success, what are the
different alternative courses of actions you're going

01:00:30.264 --> 01:00:34.244
to take? And then ultimately, how are you going to
consult, right? So it has a lot of moving pieces.

01:00:34.304 --> 01:00:37.824
This is what staffs usually plan, and
often they're looking at bigger size

01:00:37.864 --> 01:00:41.136
formations. If we were talking
earlier about infantrymen,

01:00:41.756 --> 01:00:45.636
squads, platoons, companies.
This is more at the size of

01:00:45.816 --> 01:00:49.756
armies, you know, or corps. And then
above that, we have military strategy.

01:00:50.096 --> 01:00:52.916
It's, at its core level, it's
a theory of success, right?

01:00:53.816 --> 01:00:56.096
There's some coherence to the
logic of what you're doing,

01:00:57.176 --> 01:01:01.056
why you're doing it. There are
choices being made. It reveals

01:01:01.096 --> 01:01:04.776
itself often best actually in what you're choosing
not to do rather than what you're choosing to do.

01:01:04.936 --> 01:01:06.916
That's often the most interesting
part for me as an analyst.

01:01:08.036 --> 01:01:11.156
There's some sequencing involved. You've
thought through the sequencing of actions.

01:01:11.756 --> 01:01:15.436
And ideally, it links the actual operations
you're doing and planning with the

01:01:15.456 --> 01:01:18.896
political goals of the war that you receive
from your political leaders, right?

01:01:19.556 --> 01:01:23.496
You know, Preston, you want to add a bit more to that. But
military strategy to me is always one of the more interesting

01:01:23.536 --> 01:01:26.536
topics, at least intellectually. It's...

01:01:28.456 --> 01:01:32.356
And it's often kind of debated,
you know, what, to what ex- to

01:01:32.396 --> 01:01:36.116
what extent, what is the military
strategy, how you understand the

01:01:36.156 --> 01:01:38.536
military strategy. But this
level is important too.

01:01:39.176 --> 01:01:43.056
- So I always wonder at the, at
every layer but the strategy

01:01:43.136 --> 01:01:46.856
layer, how much does strategy look like

01:01:47.736 --> 01:01:51.576
I apologize, but Pinky and the Brain, there's that
cartoon where, you know, what are we going to do

01:01:51.636 --> 01:01:55.176
today? Is we're going to take over the
world. You kind of assume everything goes

01:01:55.236 --> 01:01:58.176
flawlessly and it's kind of this dramatic

01:01:59.116 --> 01:02:02.776
picture of success versus how much
planning is there for all the different

01:02:02.816 --> 01:02:06.756
contingencies, levels of
including complete failure. Uh,

01:02:07.196 --> 01:02:09.816
it's like how much of the different
trajectories are considered?

01:02:10.336 --> 01:02:15.436
- Strategy in theory, first, it's a
plan that you're not going to change

01:02:17.336 --> 01:02:20.696
quickly or often because otherwise
it's not much of a strategy. But

01:02:21.836 --> 01:02:22.756
any good strategy

01:02:23.676 --> 01:02:27.276
must have core planning assumptions,
and if those assumptions are proven

01:02:27.376 --> 01:02:31.276
wrong, you should expect to adjust. Actually,
a lot of times strategy goes poorly is

01:02:31.296 --> 01:02:34.496
when people are trying to execute a strategy
even though their assumptions are clearly wrong.

01:02:35.076 --> 01:02:38.636
I can think of some wars we've been
in that looked a bit like that. Um,

01:02:39.536 --> 01:02:43.116
second, the strategy is iterative,
so it's interacting with the

01:02:43.176 --> 01:02:46.756
strategy of your opponent,
right? And so ideally, you're

01:02:46.816 --> 01:02:50.736
iterating, you're looking at what your
opponent's strategy is, how your strategy

01:02:50.755 --> 01:02:54.556
is performing against what they're trying to
do, and then you're changing it over time,

01:02:55.256 --> 01:02:58.916
right? But unlike tactics which change
quickly and have to keep pace with

01:02:58.936 --> 01:03:02.916
technology, and tactics inform strategy. If
strategy is divorced from tactics, then it's

01:03:02.976 --> 01:03:06.576
a great staff planning exercise. It may
be very well written, but the force can't

01:03:06.816 --> 01:03:09.396
execute that strategy,
right? And alternatively,

01:03:10.296 --> 01:03:13.816
if you just have tactics, you're just fighting
battles, then you don't have a campaign,

01:03:14.436 --> 01:03:18.316
okay? You have maybe good ways of fighting
a war, but you haven't thought through

01:03:18.356 --> 01:03:22.296
how you're going to win it. You don't have a
campaign thought through. It's not obvious

01:03:22.356 --> 01:03:26.656
how the battles you're fighting are
going to aggregate to military success.

01:03:27.376 --> 01:03:32.816
- Maybe we could take, given everything you
laid out now as a case study, the invasion

01:03:33.036 --> 01:03:35.996
of 2022, February 24th.

01:03:36.876 --> 01:03:40.796
What were the failures and what were the
effective decisions on the Russian side

01:03:40.836 --> 01:03:44.816
and the Ukrainian side in the early days,
in the early months of the war? Maybe,

01:03:44.916 --> 01:03:48.696
Mike, you could also talk
about what you predicted and

01:03:49.116 --> 01:03:53.016
what turned out to be a correct prediction
and what turned out to be not a correct

01:03:53.056 --> 01:03:53.496
prediction.

01:03:53.736 --> 01:03:56.856
- Yeah, absolutely. So first, I think

01:03:57.496 --> 01:04:00.976
amongst community of military analysts and
folks who were looking at the situation in

01:04:01.116 --> 01:04:06.456
2021, I was definitely one of the people
that thought Russia was preparing a

01:04:06.716 --> 01:04:08.316
full-scale military invasion of Ukraine.

01:04:09.356 --> 01:04:12.876
And even though we weren't
certain, and I wasn't certain

01:04:13.156 --> 01:04:17.036
of exactly how they would do it or what it
would look like, so there were definitely

01:04:17.076 --> 01:04:20.836
gaps in information. It's rare where
you have perfect knowledge of the

01:04:20.876 --> 01:04:24.676
adversary's plans. Although the U.S.
intelligence community had great knowledge

01:04:25.016 --> 01:04:28.876
of Russia's plans and were releasing that
information throughout the Russian military

01:04:28.956 --> 01:04:32.876
buildup, it was a very
difficult time because first,

01:04:33.916 --> 01:04:37.736
many folks did not want to believe that what they were
seeing on the ground, the Russian military buildup

01:04:37.836 --> 01:04:39.376
and the rhetoric leading up to it,

01:04:40.296 --> 01:04:44.216
was truly a buildup to a full-scale invasion. In
fact, I learned as an analyst that even if all

01:04:44.236 --> 01:04:45.656
the pieces kind of look right,

01:04:46.536 --> 01:04:50.376
people naturally want to believe it's
anything other than that. This is a very

01:04:50.416 --> 01:04:52.576
inconvenient truth. And

01:04:53.416 --> 01:04:57.156
there were a host of countries and
allies the United States had that had a

01:04:57.196 --> 01:04:58.675
different interpretation of the evidence,

01:04:59.976 --> 01:05:03.716
like Germany and others in Europe. And to
be frank, Ukrainian political leadership

01:05:03.876 --> 01:05:07.616
also had real misgivings about
the likelihood of a full-scale

01:05:07.656 --> 01:05:11.456
invasion. People thought if there
was going to be a breakout of

01:05:11.516 --> 01:05:15.176
conflict again, that it would just be
narrowly focused on the Donbas region

01:05:15.676 --> 01:05:19.496
and that much of the Russian buildup
was meant to coerce Ukraine.

01:05:20.236 --> 01:05:23.856
It was meant to be a part
of coercive diplomacy

01:05:24.116 --> 01:05:27.916
or to distract Ukrainian
forces. So there were

01:05:27.936 --> 01:05:30.096
competing interpretations. I
want to be clear about this.

01:05:31.436 --> 01:05:35.276
And people had different contingencies
in mind. Some thought it might be

01:05:35.336 --> 01:05:38.856
just another Russian attempt to seize the
Donbas. Some thought it might be something

01:05:38.916 --> 01:05:42.676
larger trying to capture parts of eastern
Ukraine. And some did think it was

01:05:42.716 --> 01:05:45.516
going to be a Russian attempt
to take most of the country.

01:05:46.496 --> 01:05:50.476
So I think I was amongst the folks that
predicted the invasion early on, Russian

01:05:50.516 --> 01:05:54.496
intentions and the scope of the
operation, but not the way they

01:05:54.536 --> 01:05:59.736
were going to do it. Because there,
I think we over-rationalized

01:06:01.216 --> 01:06:04.636
the way Russian military would actually
conduct the operation, not seeing

01:06:05.356 --> 01:06:09.056
how the military strategy and the
plan, the concept of operations,

01:06:09.356 --> 01:06:12.756
would be tied to Russian political assumptions.
And the Russian political assumptions were that

01:06:13.276 --> 01:06:18.236
Russian intelligence had set the conditions
in Ukraine for a speedy takeover.

01:06:18.276 --> 01:06:20.856
The Russian troops would be
greeted as liberators, okay?

01:06:21.816 --> 01:06:25.656
That they could conduct
essentially a coup de main, that

01:06:25.696 --> 01:06:29.896
they could very rapidly advance
into Ukraine, encircle the capital,

01:06:31.056 --> 01:06:35.116
either capture or isolate
Ukrainian political leadership,

01:06:36.076 --> 01:06:40.036
and that even though there would be an
initial phase of combat operations, it

01:06:40.056 --> 01:06:43.836
would be brief, and that they
could get Ukrainian forces to a

01:06:43.896 --> 01:06:47.836
large extent surrender or isolate them.

01:06:48.136 --> 01:06:50.616
These are the Russian assumptions.
I'm being clear about that.

01:06:51.432 --> 01:06:55.152
And that then they could quickly move
to a stabilization occupation phase.

01:06:55.512 --> 01:06:59.371
And all that stemmed from the fact that at the
end of the day, that Vladimir Putin did not

01:06:59.452 --> 01:07:03.172
see Ukraine as a real independent country
with a separate national identity,

01:07:03.692 --> 01:07:07.672
and really had not learned or updated his
views at all from the real beginning of

01:07:07.712 --> 01:07:11.672
the war, which was 2014, right? And we

01:07:11.732 --> 01:07:15.692
know this because he actually wrote
many of them in a July article in 2021.

01:07:15.772 --> 01:07:19.592
If we go back to that, folks who are interested,
it was published. And he laid out a whole

01:07:19.652 --> 01:07:26.172
series of views of how he saw Ukraine,
how he saw the sort of ongoing frozen

01:07:26.272 --> 01:07:30.252
conflict between Russia and Ukraine,
and his assumptions were fairly clear.

01:07:30.692 --> 01:07:34.452
I don't think we did a good enough job of tying
those assumptions to thinking through how

01:07:34.492 --> 01:07:37.752
Russia might actually do
this kind of operation.

01:07:39.132 --> 01:07:42.912
And secondarily, the toughest thing to predict,
the interaction between the two forces.

01:07:43.652 --> 01:07:47.552
Here, it's not a case of
overestimating the Russian

01:07:47.592 --> 01:07:51.512
military, just underestimating the Ukraine military,
although I think there's a fair argument to be said

01:07:51.552 --> 01:07:55.491
that we overestimated how Russian
military would perform in this case. We

01:07:55.571 --> 01:07:59.532
underestimated how the Ukraine military
would perform in the initial opening of

01:07:59.572 --> 01:08:03.332
the war, in part because we knew far less about the
Ukraine military. I'm gonna be very frank about that.

01:08:03.992 --> 01:08:07.952
Most expertise is usually against adversary
forces. We don't have nearly as much

01:08:08.012 --> 01:08:10.952
expertise amongst ally,
for allies and partners.

01:08:12.052 --> 01:08:15.152
That's just the nature of the field
and the profession we work in.

01:08:16.012 --> 01:08:19.952
And secondarily, I think actually more
was known about Russian plans to attack

01:08:19.992 --> 01:08:23.952
than Ukrainian plans to defend. So there's a
degree of anchoring bias that information has

01:08:24.392 --> 01:08:26.952
when you do analysis. But also

01:08:28.172 --> 01:08:32.152
much was contingent. So Ukrainian
forces perform, not only

01:08:32.192 --> 01:08:35.292
did they perform much better, but Ukrainian
volunteers showed up to fight in those early moments.

01:08:35.332 --> 01:08:38.652
But it was a very chaotic early period
of the war, the initial clash and

01:08:38.692 --> 01:08:42.412
reconciliation of the Russian
invasion and the Ukrainian defense.

01:08:43.192 --> 01:08:47.132
I think one challenge that analysts
have and folks who look at analysis

01:08:47.372 --> 01:08:50.952
is in understanding that military
power by itself cannot be

01:08:50.992 --> 01:08:54.952
abstracted. I often see it abstracted in
conversations, a sense the United States is number

01:08:55.052 --> 01:08:58.952
one and China's number two and Russia's maybe
number three or four or five, wherever you,

01:08:59.232 --> 01:09:02.912
you wanna put it. But there's no such
thing. Military power requires a

01:09:02.972 --> 01:09:08.052
context to express itself, right? So if
you're thinking of a potential, let's say,

01:09:08.932 --> 01:09:12.852
battle between United States
and China over Taiwan, you have

01:09:12.912 --> 01:09:16.712
one scenario in mind, okay? If you're
thinking of maybe a war between China and

01:09:16.752 --> 01:09:18.312
India, you have a different
scenario in mind.

01:09:19.332 --> 01:09:23.112
So this abstraction isn't
helpful. It actually really

01:09:23.152 --> 01:09:27.112
matters what the scenario is because the geography
matters, the political objectives matter.

01:09:27.232 --> 01:09:30.152
They will drive the military strategy
and the concept of operations,

01:09:31.232 --> 01:09:35.212
and that will then heavily decide how the

01:09:35.252 --> 01:09:38.392
forces will be employed, which is
the most important thing here.

01:09:39.492 --> 01:09:43.112
And so sometimes what happens is you
may know something about a military,

01:09:44.032 --> 01:09:47.052
or you may even know quite a
bit about both militaries, but

01:09:48.372 --> 01:09:52.272
knowing something about militaries
is not the same as knowing how

01:09:52.312 --> 01:09:55.952
the two of them will interact in a particular
fight. And that is subject to a lot of

01:09:55.992 --> 01:09:59.812
individual choices, political
choices, military choices, and also a

01:09:59.852 --> 01:10:02.252
great deal of serendipity and
chance, things that are contingent.

01:10:03.112 --> 01:10:05.092
Um, I'll say this about
the opening of the war.

01:10:06.432 --> 01:10:10.252
As poorly thought through the
Russian invasion was, and as

01:10:10.292 --> 01:10:13.852
unworkable as their assumptions
were, you know, in many cases, the

01:10:13.912 --> 01:10:20.972
Ukrainian defense itself was improvised.
And so that first clash was, in my view,

01:10:21.692 --> 01:10:26.572
contingent on a whole series of choices
and early battles that went a certain way.

01:10:27.532 --> 01:10:31.392
And we'll continue to debate this military
history for a long time. You know, I think I

01:10:31.452 --> 01:10:35.412
and other folks who are interested
in military history, we still debate

01:10:35.452 --> 01:10:37.192
what happened in World War II,
what happened in World War

01:10:37.992 --> 01:10:39.492
I, what happened in the Napoleonic Wars.

01:10:40.352 --> 01:10:44.012
The reason I bring this up is for
a degree of humility, this war is

01:10:44.052 --> 01:10:47.792
hardly over. We still have a long
ways to go to piece together what

01:10:47.832 --> 01:10:49.172
happened exactly and why.

01:10:50.152 --> 01:10:53.692
And often we don't have a great
understanding of the probability of a

01:10:53.752 --> 01:10:57.632
timeline, because people naturally assume
that whatever timeline they're living in,

01:10:57.652 --> 01:11:01.112
whatever happened, was the likeliest
series of events to take place.

01:11:01.792 --> 01:11:05.432
They rarely ever assume that
they're living in a fortunate

01:11:06.252 --> 01:11:11.812
version of that timeline. So I'll stop
there in this sort of discussion of,

01:11:12.812 --> 01:11:14.792
of what we thought and the initial

01:11:16.252 --> 01:11:19.992
reconciliation of the Russian plan to
invade and Ukrainian plan to defense.

01:11:20.392 --> 01:11:24.152
I learned a great deal myself professionally as an analyst,
I'll be honest, watching it, particularly from the

01:11:24.172 --> 01:11:27.572
things that... Not from the things necessarily they
got right, but from the things they got wrong.

01:11:27.672 --> 01:11:30.532
I think you end up learning far more
from what you get wrong professionally.

01:11:30.792 --> 01:11:31.772
- What surprised you the most?

01:11:32.132 --> 01:11:36.812
- What surprised me the most first,
how the Russians actually conceived

01:11:37.872 --> 01:11:41.732
of this operation. The
fact that they had, uh,

01:11:43.372 --> 01:11:48.132
fairly leverage and fairly brittle
force without any real plan B.

01:11:49.232 --> 01:11:51.192
There's no hedge in their invasion plan.

01:11:52.312 --> 01:11:56.252
Second, how poorly they performed at
the tactical level, just in terms of

01:11:56.312 --> 01:11:59.952
capability, quality of the
force. And it's difficult to

01:12:00.092 --> 01:12:03.872
judge seeing a military going through a
series of military reforms and modernization,

01:12:05.492 --> 01:12:09.372
how they might perform in a large scale
conventional war if you haven't seen a large

01:12:09.412 --> 01:12:13.312
scale conventional war. But I think there was definitely
an intervening period where we overrated them,

01:12:13.612 --> 01:12:16.872
which is a tendency when looking at adversaries,
because we tend to worst case them.

01:12:17.012 --> 01:12:20.352
I'll be very honest. This is often
a professional assumption that

01:12:21.392 --> 01:12:24.652
it's better to worst case and assume that

01:12:24.932 --> 01:12:28.732
adversaries can do the things they would
like to do and the things they talk

01:12:28.792 --> 01:12:29.352
about doing,

01:12:30.392 --> 01:12:34.372
and maybe get that wrong, in which case your own
forces have a good day. They perform much better

01:12:35.072 --> 01:12:39.952
than the opposite version, right?
Strategic surprise, Pearl Harbor, where

01:12:40.852 --> 01:12:44.832
you underestimate your opponent, and the
result of you underestimating your opponent

01:12:45.032 --> 01:12:46.692
is disastrous, right?

01:12:47.212 --> 01:12:51.012
- Yeah, and I guess there's a bunch of things
when you speak into the reality of war

01:12:51.132 --> 01:12:54.792
just went wrong. Like there's the columns
of tanks going towards Kyiv that weren't

01:12:54.832 --> 01:12:57.852
effective. There's... When
you take a military force

01:12:58.472 --> 01:13:02.112
and it connects with the
reality of the geography, the

01:13:02.152 --> 01:13:05.740
situation. Then you get, actually
get to see which were the effective

01:13:05.820 --> 01:13:08.240
tactical decisions, and that
was the illustration of that.

01:13:08.400 --> 01:13:12.180
- Well, ultimately, you have the interaction
between the two forces and the

01:13:12.220 --> 01:13:16.020
decisions that both sides make. And
I think that definitely the initial

01:13:16.120 --> 01:13:20.040
prediction that Russia would succeed
in this conventional phase of the

01:13:20.100 --> 01:13:24.080
war because it looked like, you know,
they had superiority in deployed

01:13:24.160 --> 01:13:27.940
forces. Ukrainian military
was fairly late to

01:13:28.060 --> 01:13:31.280
mobilize, and much of the Ukrainian
military assumptions seemed to be

01:13:32.000 --> 01:13:35.960
that the focus of the Russian invasion would come
in the Donbas, and that's where a lot of units

01:13:36.000 --> 01:13:39.900
were deployed. Many units were not
there available to either defend

01:13:39.940 --> 01:13:43.920
in the south, to even check the Russian
advance from Crimea or in the north.

01:13:43.960 --> 01:13:47.840
In fact, there were almost no regular military
units north of Kyiv to stop the advance from

01:13:47.880 --> 01:13:52.580
Belarus. So, it was difficult to
assess the interaction of those plans.

01:13:53.540 --> 01:13:57.400
I look back on it, and I think I learned
a tremendous amount, so did other

01:13:57.480 --> 01:14:01.180
analysts. And it's a case
that I think we're gonna

01:14:01.220 --> 01:14:04.740
continue to study. But this was just
the very initial period of the war.

01:14:05.400 --> 01:14:09.180
What's interesting is I think
military analysts got much better in

01:14:09.260 --> 01:14:13.060
correcting expectations, and then
predictions for follow-on offensives and

01:14:13.140 --> 01:14:17.040
counter-offensives became much more
accurate once we saw the two forces in the

01:14:17.080 --> 01:14:17.320
fight.

01:14:17.960 --> 01:14:21.920
- Preston, what surprised you most about the beginning
of the war as you look back now, especially

01:14:21.940 --> 01:14:23.460
as that war has evolved?

01:14:24.020 --> 01:14:27.920
- Well, there was something right out the gate,
Lex, that I think is still there today, and

01:14:27.960 --> 01:14:31.280
it shows this difference between,
we'll call it the Soviet

01:14:31.720 --> 01:14:35.560
military mindset and the Western
mindset, and we hadn't really seen this

01:14:35.620 --> 01:14:39.440
in play. In my experience, again, fighting
the global war on terror, it's not,

01:14:39.940 --> 01:14:43.560
you weren't running up against a conventional
force that was operating in any way

01:14:43.920 --> 01:14:47.700
similar to how we were. We push authority

01:14:47.780 --> 01:14:51.540
down and allow junior leaders
to make decisions in the

01:14:51.580 --> 01:14:55.380
moment. The whole idea is you
have a commander's intent.

01:14:55.780 --> 01:14:59.340
This is what we're trying to accomplish and
why, the task and purpose and all of that.

01:14:59.740 --> 01:15:03.260
And down the chain of command, the
soldiers, the individuals have the

01:15:03.280 --> 01:15:07.220
authority to make a decision on the spot
to decide, "I need to go left," or,

01:15:07.260 --> 01:15:10.940
"I need to dig in," or, "I need to call for
reinforcements," or, "I need to retrograde."

01:15:11.500 --> 01:15:15.460
That is pushed down to the lowest possible
level to where we've got, you know,

01:15:15.580 --> 01:15:20.820
18, 19-year-old kids making these
incredible decisions in combat.

01:15:20.960 --> 01:15:24.140
- We should say for a little bit of
context that you served in Afghanistan.

01:15:24.200 --> 01:15:27.980
And maybe you can connect some of the
experiences you remember from that, what things

01:15:28.040 --> 01:15:33.280
you've seen, in terms of examples
of how a Western force operates.

01:15:33.900 --> 01:15:37.820
- Well, that was very dispersed. We were
spread across a lot of small bases

01:15:37.840 --> 01:15:41.680
in Afghanistan, so you had
captains and below, sometimes 24,

01:15:41.900 --> 01:15:45.800
25-year-olds, making daily life
and death decisions for a lot of

01:15:45.840 --> 01:15:47.580
people, and we would push that down to

01:15:48.380 --> 01:15:52.340
20-year-old staff sergeants or
sergeants that were making a decision,

01:15:52.940 --> 01:15:55.120
that could cost the lives of their men.

01:15:55.420 --> 01:15:59.240
- So you're saying like a 19-year-old, 20-year-old,
if they have a good idea, if they have a good

01:15:59.280 --> 01:16:01.200
decision, that has power?

01:16:01.860 --> 01:16:05.800
- Yeah, there's a lot of leadership involved with that, 'cause
you can't just grab a random guy and say, "We're gonna

01:16:05.840 --> 01:16:09.700
let you make whatever decision you want."
But through the training and through

01:16:09.760 --> 01:16:13.660
the kind of briefings from the top down,
from brigade to battalion to company-level

01:16:13.680 --> 01:16:17.640
commanders to the squad leader, making sure
that your people know this is what we're

01:16:17.680 --> 01:16:21.500
doing. This is the mission, and this is what
we're trying to accomplish, so they have a

01:16:21.540 --> 01:16:25.160
full understanding. So then when
something inevitably goes sideways,

01:16:25.760 --> 01:16:29.460
they're not stuck like the Russian army
was- ... trying to advance on Kyiv.

01:16:29.760 --> 01:16:32.780
Where when they got stuck, they stopped.

01:16:32.860 --> 01:16:36.620
There was a shocking number of senior
Russian officers killed in the

01:16:36.700 --> 01:16:40.600
opening few weeks of the war because they kept
having to go closer and closer to the front

01:16:40.660 --> 01:16:43.140
to essentially move traffic jams.

01:16:43.200 --> 01:16:47.060
Because they didn't have the as they
went down the ranks, whereas we have,

01:16:47.100 --> 01:16:50.800
and, you know, again, this was a
Ukrainian victory, to be clear.

01:16:51.040 --> 01:16:54.660
The US and NATO had been
doing some work in training

01:16:54.720 --> 01:16:58.660
Ukraine for some time, and in
some units, it kind of instilled

01:16:58.720 --> 01:17:03.600
this idea of junior leadership and
empowering your junior soldiers to where

01:17:04.440 --> 01:17:08.420
if they don't have an order, they still
know what the mission is, and they're

01:17:08.500 --> 01:17:12.340
able to make the best decision on the spot.
We didn't see that from Russia, and I

01:17:12.380 --> 01:17:16.200
would argue that we're still seeing
that today. On the Ukrainian side,

01:17:16.280 --> 01:17:20.279
there is a crazy amount of innovation. I
know we've talked about it with drones, but

01:17:20.320 --> 01:17:24.060
even the tactics on how they're gonna advance in
certain are- it's different from one unit to the

01:17:24.100 --> 01:17:27.520
next. A lot of that is driven from
the bottom up, which is a very

01:17:27.880 --> 01:17:31.420
Western military type thing, whereas
on the Russian side, it's still,

01:17:31.980 --> 01:17:35.860
to today, is still top-down driven. They are
given their orders. They are given their

01:17:35.900 --> 01:17:39.660
place on a map. Go there.
So that was surprising to

01:17:39.700 --> 01:17:43.600
see for me right out the gate, and I think
it's pretty notable. I think as the war,

01:17:44.420 --> 01:17:48.340
you know, hopefully wraps up before too long
here, I think that's gonna be a big takeaway

01:17:48.540 --> 01:17:51.960
is that that method is preferred of

01:17:52.800 --> 01:17:55.580
pushing the authority as far down the
chain of command as you possibly can.

01:17:56.700 --> 01:18:00.460
- Yeah, absolutely. And but that's both
a conversation about leadership and

01:18:00.520 --> 01:18:04.420
military culture. And I think in
looking in the Russian military before

01:18:04.440 --> 01:18:08.420
this war, the gaps of knowledge
we may have had on how they would

01:18:08.480 --> 01:18:11.680
fight were filled with what
we knew about doctrine,

01:18:12.260 --> 01:18:16.200
operational concepts, and more should
have been done to fill the gaps

01:18:16.260 --> 01:18:20.060
looking at military culture
and some of the real

01:18:21.760 --> 01:18:24.500
deficits and shortcomings in
terms of military culture

01:18:25.280 --> 01:18:29.020
and leadership style that is preferred
in the Russian armed forces.

01:18:30.080 --> 01:18:34.520
And one could easily say that when
looking at the Ukrainian military,

01:18:35.360 --> 01:18:36.780
we could have done a much better job

01:18:37.640 --> 01:18:39.740
learning more about the Ukrainian
armed forces before the war

01:18:40.640 --> 01:18:44.400
to get better estimation on how they
would fight. But in many cases,

01:18:44.440 --> 01:18:49.300
talking to Ukrainian colleagues, at
the end of the day, you find that, uh,

01:18:50.100 --> 01:18:53.800
a lot of these battles, a lot of
these interactions were the result of

01:18:53.940 --> 01:18:57.660
initiative of units, of commanders
in those early days, and it was

01:18:58.180 --> 01:19:03.940
quite chaotic and the
success of volunteers, of

01:19:04.060 --> 01:19:08.020
troops, and people who essentially
mobilized themselves to fight

01:19:08.060 --> 01:19:11.100
and join the forces and filled the
ranks early on, swelled the ranks,

01:19:12.020 --> 01:19:14.500
in order to muster that defense.

01:19:15.120 --> 01:19:17.680
- You've written about
Prigozhin's mutiny in '23.

01:19:18.812 --> 01:19:22.472
What does that teach us about
the structure of the Russian

01:19:22.512 --> 01:19:26.112
military? And going to Perplexity
here, the Prigozhin mutiny

01:19:26.672 --> 01:19:30.632
was a short-lived armed rebellion
led by the Wagner mercenary chief,

01:19:30.732 --> 01:19:35.392
Yevgeny Prigozhin, in Russia
on June 23rd and 24th, 2023.

01:19:36.432 --> 01:19:40.332
- Yeah, a well-known
character from well before

01:19:40.372 --> 01:19:43.812
this war to people who followed
Russia, and particularly the role of

01:19:43.852 --> 01:19:52.272
Wagner in other wars like the, like the
Syrian Civil War, and their deployments to,

01:19:53.192 --> 01:19:59.092
to Africa and parts of the Middle East.
But Prigozhin rose to prominence,

01:19:59.952 --> 01:20:03.812
certainly amongst Western
audience, from outspokenness

01:20:04.232 --> 01:20:07.952
and public criticism and the
conflict that he developed with the,

01:20:08.852 --> 01:20:12.712
the Minister of Defense at the time,
Sergei Shoigu, and the Chief of General

01:20:12.792 --> 01:20:13.872
Staff, Valery Gerasimov.

01:20:14.832 --> 01:20:18.752
Maybe to quickly track at least how I
remember the role of Wagner in this war

01:20:18.752 --> 01:20:25.512
is Wagner comes in early on in 2022.
They get paired up with the Russian

01:20:25.552 --> 01:20:30.552
airborne. They show some success in May of

01:20:30.692 --> 01:20:34.972
2022 at a town in Bakhmut
called Popasna. They then

01:20:35.872 --> 01:20:39.832
are expanded in terms of the role
in this war, and Prigozhin is given

01:20:39.872 --> 01:20:43.572
the authority by the Russian government to go
recruit from Russian prisons. And he starts hiring

01:20:43.612 --> 01:20:47.452
convicts to build out special
convict-staffed assault formations,

01:20:47.512 --> 01:20:51.032
assault groups within Wagner. So the
size of Wagner grows in assault force.

01:20:51.772 --> 01:20:55.652
And Wagner is thrown then into a fight
for a city called Bakhmut, and enters a

01:20:55.712 --> 01:20:59.512
grinding fight for the city. And
I think you could periodize the

01:20:59.572 --> 01:21:02.872
battle, technically maybe starts
August or so, and it runs through

01:21:03.652 --> 01:21:08.632
fall of 2022, winter,
all the way till May.

01:21:08.812 --> 01:21:13.212
- We should say that's one of the
most important battles in the war.

01:21:13.872 --> 01:21:17.452
- It is one of the most important battles, particularly
in the early period of the war, certainly.

01:21:17.832 --> 01:21:21.732
- Can you actually take a small tangent and just
what are your memories of that? Can you describe

01:21:21.752 --> 01:21:24.072
what that battle was, and what did,

01:21:25.192 --> 01:21:29.032
what did it teach you about war?
What did it teach you about this,

01:21:29.192 --> 01:21:30.012
this particular war?

01:21:30.652 --> 01:21:37.152
- So the fight around Bakhmut
was, in some respects, a siege,

01:21:38.052 --> 01:21:40.952
a classical siege, because you
had both a battle for the city,

01:21:41.872 --> 01:21:45.812
and you had a decisive battle around the
city to try to control the roads, the

01:21:45.852 --> 01:21:49.632
ground lines of communications into the
city. And what was happening to Bakhmut,

01:21:49.852 --> 01:21:55.012
the reason why at that time period, this
is going back to February, March of 2023,

01:21:56.652 --> 01:21:59.912
the reason why Ukrainian defense of
Bakhmut increasingly looked precarious,

01:22:00.532 --> 01:22:04.352
it wasn't the urban battle for
the city. In the city, you had

01:22:04.852 --> 01:22:08.772
essentially a city that
was divided by a small

01:22:08.832 --> 01:22:12.312
river running through it, and there was a
kind of residential eastern side to it,

01:22:12.932 --> 01:22:16.812
and there's a more urban and industrial
side to the city. And the city, actually, I

01:22:16.852 --> 01:22:20.772
wanna be clear, this is a nice city. I have a
friend who was in the Peace Corps in the city.

01:22:20.852 --> 01:22:26.432
He knows it much better than I ever did.
But Bakhmut is a sort of medium-sized city,

01:22:27.252 --> 01:22:31.012
but not a known one in Ukraine.
So you had industrial zones.

01:22:31.652 --> 01:22:35.972
You had nine-story
apartment buildings. Um,

01:22:36.952 --> 01:22:40.932
you know, those of us who originally were born
or grew up in the Soviet Union can remember them

01:22:40.972 --> 01:22:44.092
well. And you had a kind of eastern residential
area on the eastern side of the river.

01:22:44.792 --> 01:22:45.632
And then you had

01:22:46.852 --> 01:22:50.652
terrain that was raised above the city. The city itself
was kind of on a punch bowl. It was on lower terrain.

01:22:50.692 --> 01:22:54.652
It was not an easy place to defend
because the terrain was higher, both

01:22:54.672 --> 01:22:58.412
in front, east of it, and west
of it, right? And so what

01:22:58.452 --> 01:23:02.392
happens, Russian forces end up occupying the positions
east of it, and then you have essentially two

01:23:02.472 --> 01:23:06.252
battles that take place. One is for
Bakhmut, as Russian forces steadily enter

01:23:06.332 --> 01:23:09.112
Bakhmut from the eastern side,
make progress from east to west.

01:23:09.952 --> 01:23:13.912
And the other one's a siege around the city,
because most sieges, if you want to take a city,

01:23:14.692 --> 01:23:18.432
you have to interdict, you
have to control your enemy's

01:23:18.472 --> 01:23:22.352
ability to reinforce the city, and you have
to take control of the logistics, right?

01:23:22.452 --> 01:23:25.712
Otherwise, they'll keep reinforcing it, and
it's going to be a very long fight in the slog.

01:23:26.632 --> 01:23:30.432
And so Ukrainian forces were essentially fighting
two battles. And in some ways, the more important

01:23:30.492 --> 01:23:32.612
one was the battle to prevent
encirclement of the city.

01:23:33.552 --> 01:23:36.952
And at that time, very briefly, where I
happened to be there with Ukrainian forces,

01:23:37.932 --> 01:23:41.792
Russian military had gone
around the northern side of the

01:23:41.832 --> 01:23:45.632
city and was really close to interdicting
one of the main roads running down

01:23:45.672 --> 01:23:49.432
from Chasiv Yar, this crumble road,
and in part because another town fell

01:23:49.572 --> 01:23:53.432
north of Bakhmut called
Soledar. This smaller city

01:23:53.452 --> 01:23:57.312
had fallen, and so the flank, essentially
the left flank, began to give, right,

01:23:57.712 --> 01:23:58.192
in this battle.

01:23:59.052 --> 01:24:02.892
And then the right flank slowly began to give
as well, as Russian forces entered the eastern

01:24:03.012 --> 01:24:05.172
part and then the southeastern part.

01:24:06.332 --> 01:24:09.952
And the city, like many cities that
face a battle like this, essentially

01:24:10.512 --> 01:24:11.792
saw systematic destruction.

01:24:12.672 --> 01:24:16.332
This was still very much an artillery
and infantry and tank fight.

01:24:17.212 --> 01:24:20.772
There were infantry
fighting vehicles, BMP-2s

01:24:20.872 --> 01:24:24.792
directly firing 30-millimeter cannon
in the city. There were tanks that

01:24:24.852 --> 01:24:28.692
came into the city, would be direct
firing down the street at buildings,

01:24:28.772 --> 01:24:32.592
enemy positions. And you had
close-quarters combat between infantry,

01:24:32.692 --> 01:24:36.172
fighting from buildings to buildings. You could
picture this as a fairly, in some ways, a

01:24:36.192 --> 01:24:39.512
traditional urban fight. But
on top of that, you also had

01:24:39.772 --> 01:24:42.112
drones. You had light bomber drones,

01:24:42.972 --> 01:24:46.832
At this time, FPVs were just
being experimented with

01:24:47.212 --> 01:24:51.112
in winter of 2023. You didn't really
see them at this point of the fight.

01:24:51.412 --> 01:24:55.132
But the battle was a grinding
fight. Wagner was throwing

01:24:55.872 --> 01:24:59.572
constant assault groups, exhausting
the Ukrainian defense with

01:25:00.172 --> 01:25:03.872
convict infantry. And many of them would
get killed, but Ukrainian units would get

01:25:03.912 --> 01:25:07.452
tired. They'd run out of ammo. And then
Wagner would send more experienced troops.

01:25:07.932 --> 01:25:10.572
And Russian airborne
essentially secured the flanks,

01:25:11.412 --> 01:25:15.352
and they were providing artillery support to them as well.
So it wasn't just Wagner fighting, although Prigozhin

01:25:15.392 --> 01:25:18.552
liked to take all the credit for the
fight. In reality, it was Wagner

01:25:19.360 --> 01:25:23.300
A large amount of artillery support because
the Russian military had a significant fires

01:25:23.340 --> 01:25:27.300
advantage, artillery advantage in this fight as
well. And this is what was leveling a lot of

01:25:27.320 --> 01:25:31.220
the city. And it also had elements of
Russian airborne securing the flanks.

01:25:31.540 --> 01:25:34.500
That way Wagner could focus on the
fight for the actual city itself.

01:25:35.420 --> 01:25:39.300
And it was a grinding battle. It was very
much a traditional battle that ultimately saw

01:25:39.380 --> 01:25:40.820
the destruction of the city.

01:25:41.820 --> 01:25:45.500
By the time Russian forces took it, there
was very little of the city left if you see

01:25:45.560 --> 01:25:46.320
images of it now.

01:25:47.380 --> 01:25:51.200
I will say it's, from my point of
view, not only is it a tragedy, but

01:25:51.880 --> 01:25:55.840
this is the fate that many other cities
have suffered in Ukraine. Essentially, i-

01:25:55.960 --> 01:25:59.920
in some ways, Russian forces at the end of the
battle took nothing because there was very little

01:25:59.960 --> 01:26:01.020
of the city left to take.

01:26:01.500 --> 01:26:03.700
- And you've referred to
the Bakhmut syndrome.

01:26:04.100 --> 01:26:08.400
- So Bakhmut syndrome is
Bakhmut became a fixing point

01:26:09.380 --> 01:26:13.320
in the war at a critical
period because what

01:26:13.340 --> 01:26:14.980
was happening in the war around this fight

01:26:15.960 --> 01:26:19.560
is in the fall, Ukraine had
two remarkably successful

01:26:19.600 --> 01:26:23.479
offensive operations. One was in Kharkiv,
where Russian military literally routed,

01:26:24.399 --> 01:26:28.260
and the other one was in Kherson,
which was much more slow

01:26:28.300 --> 01:26:32.000
going and plotting, but nonetheless forced
the Russian military to withdraw and

01:26:32.040 --> 01:26:36.020
retreat from the western side of the

01:26:36.060 --> 01:26:40.020
Dnipro riverbank. They were both successful,
and they began to set the expectation that

01:26:40.080 --> 01:26:43.420
actually this could be a shorter war, that Ukraine
could achieve a military victory in this war.

01:26:44.740 --> 01:26:48.680
And the Russian military
then tried to use Bakhmut

01:26:48.800 --> 01:26:52.180
as essentially a fixing fight, but
employing Wagner, which were kind of the

01:26:52.220 --> 01:26:54.280
expendables in this battle.

01:26:55.700 --> 01:26:59.580
Prigozhin may not have seen him and his
forces that way, but certainly that's the way

01:26:59.620 --> 01:27:03.520
they were being used. And
it became a grinding fight.

01:27:04.340 --> 01:27:09.180
And as it became a grinding, grinding fight
over time, it started to sap morale. But

01:27:10.960 --> 01:27:14.820
battles like this take on political
significance. I personally think

01:27:14.880 --> 01:27:16.120
that the loss of Bakhmut

01:27:16.960 --> 01:27:20.800
wasn't all that important or meaningful. In fact,
I don't just think that, I know that because it

01:27:20.820 --> 01:27:24.560
took the Russian military a whole year
to get from Bakhmut to Chasiv Yar, the

01:27:24.600 --> 01:27:26.460
next town over on higher terrain.

01:27:26.660 --> 01:27:27.440
- Still fighting for it.

01:27:27.480 --> 01:27:30.940
- And it took them even another year
to take a large part of Chasiv Yar,

01:27:31.300 --> 01:27:34.200
okay? And if you look at
where they are from Bakhmut,

01:27:34.900 --> 01:27:38.660
distance-wise, on the map,
it's not far. So one can

01:27:38.720 --> 01:27:42.600
safely now argue, I think the
evidence is in, incontrovertibly,

01:27:42.680 --> 01:27:44.440
that the loss of Bakhmut wasn't nearly,

01:27:45.640 --> 01:27:47.420
wasn't nearly that critical
at the end of the day.

01:27:47.600 --> 01:27:51.400
- Uh, critical for the broader
strategy of the war. So why,

01:27:51.620 --> 01:27:53.460
why over-fixate on Bakhmut?

01:27:53.520 --> 01:27:57.500
- Well, the challenge is that battles
become politically significant even if

01:27:57.560 --> 01:28:03.180
tactically they might not be justifiably
freighted with that significance.

01:28:03.420 --> 01:28:07.680
- And political when you mean, is
it like Stalingrad in, in the-

01:28:07.720 --> 01:28:11.340
... World War II? Because certain
leaders up, atop there's cer- certain

01:28:11.440 --> 01:28:14.420
fixation on a particular city as a symbol.

01:28:14.720 --> 01:28:18.240
- Yes. The battle for Bakhmut to me was one
freighted with political significance

01:28:18.340 --> 01:28:23.380
because it was a contest, right? And wars

01:28:24.120 --> 01:28:27.740
by their nature are fundamentally contests of
wills, and they're contests of systems between

01:28:27.820 --> 01:28:31.540
states. And these then
become bellwether events.

01:28:32.780 --> 01:28:36.220
And what happens is political
leaders often see the battle as

01:28:36.280 --> 01:28:39.960
significant. Maybe the city itself is not
critical, but they can't afford to lose it.

01:28:40.060 --> 01:28:42.440
Why? It became very important for Ukraine

01:28:43.340 --> 01:28:46.660
because Ukraine heavily dependent on
external support, on Western support,

01:28:47.600 --> 01:28:49.320
and wanted to maintain the perception

01:28:50.440 --> 01:28:54.320
after successful offensives in the
fall that the war was going their way,

01:28:54.380 --> 01:28:58.340
that they had the momentum, okay? And
believed that further expectations

01:28:58.380 --> 01:29:02.080
of support, the equipment they
wanted to receive, the additional

01:29:02.160 --> 01:29:05.400
capabilities and training they
wanted to receive, would be tied to

01:29:06.420 --> 01:29:09.980
the sense in Western countries that Ukraine
is winning. And so they were very concerned.

01:29:10.380 --> 01:29:14.140
This happens all the time. Wars are
full of sunk cost fallacies in this

01:29:14.180 --> 01:29:18.020
pathology of thinking. Naturally very
concerned that the loss of Bakhmut

01:29:18.440 --> 01:29:21.540
would lead to a turn in sort of Western

01:29:22.240 --> 01:29:26.180
public sentiment and the views of
political decision-makers in Western

01:29:26.240 --> 01:29:30.200
countries. And so there was this whole
campaign organized called Fortress Bakhmut.

01:29:30.280 --> 01:29:33.820
I always worry when any city, especially a
fairly small city, is declared a fortress,

01:29:34.380 --> 01:29:38.340
because then it means that forces there
will not be able to withdraw or retrograde

01:29:38.400 --> 01:29:42.220
in a timely manner. Most people who end up in
those battles, I know from Ukrainian troops,

01:29:42.380 --> 01:29:46.040
also always worry when, you know, something's
declared a fortress because then they're,

01:29:46.840 --> 01:29:50.800
then they know that's going to be a pretty
tough, pretty tough and bitter fight. But

01:29:52.520 --> 01:29:56.260
essentially what happened was this became
a prolonged attritional fight over

01:29:56.340 --> 01:30:01.920
Bakhmut. Ukraine was able to draw
the fight out, but I'm not sure

01:30:02.040 --> 01:30:05.980
necessarily to their benefit. And what
happened was it tied down quite a few units in

01:30:06.020 --> 01:30:06.940
the fight for Bakhmut.

01:30:07.800 --> 01:30:11.260
And this is an ongoing debate to what
extent did the battle for Bakhmut

01:30:11.380 --> 01:30:12.940
expend very experienced troops

01:30:14.020 --> 01:30:17.940
in exchange for holding onto a town
for some extra period of time. But

01:30:17.980 --> 01:30:21.720
on the other side, the Russian military
was fighting with these expendable

01:30:21.820 --> 01:30:25.720
Wagner forces. A lot of them were
convicts. Second, to what extent

01:30:26.220 --> 01:30:30.160
it pulled in forces that then
could have been used and trained,

01:30:30.320 --> 01:30:34.220
that were very experienced, that could have been used
and actually trained on Western equipment, and could

01:30:34.260 --> 01:30:40.200
have led the summer offensive in June
of 2023, instead of having green,

01:30:41.080 --> 01:30:44.540
new, inexperienced troops with
the better Western equipment, but

01:30:44.580 --> 01:30:48.540
nonetheless lacking experience, having to
conduct a very difficult breaching operation.

01:30:49.000 --> 01:30:51.320
So all these questions remain. I
know we'll continue to debate them.

01:30:52.360 --> 01:30:54.740
But the bottom line was Bakhmut
Syndrome. It was essentially

01:30:55.680 --> 01:30:57.340
choosing to defend the city

01:30:58.960 --> 01:31:02.440
past the point when it made sense. And
that would often happen because it's very

01:31:02.580 --> 01:31:06.520
difficult to withdraw from a population
center, right? Population centers

01:31:06.600 --> 01:31:09.120
often fix fights. That tends to be

01:31:10.424 --> 01:31:14.244
at least the character of this
war. And also, defense is often

01:31:14.424 --> 01:31:18.283
anchored around population centers, around
cities. They serve as a key part of

01:31:18.324 --> 01:31:21.904
defense, right? So once the fight
actually becomes a siege of a population

01:31:22.004 --> 01:31:25.804
center, you then get into
the situation where it's

01:31:25.884 --> 01:31:29.384
very hard to withdraw,
and you'll see Russians

01:31:29.424 --> 01:31:33.344
expend an immense amount
of troops and personnel to

01:31:33.384 --> 01:31:37.244
try to take it, destroy the city in
the process. But Ukraine also bitterly

01:31:37.304 --> 01:31:41.284
hold on to the city in an effort to try
to exhaust the Russian offensive there.

01:31:41.644 --> 01:31:45.364
- Before we return to
Prigozhin, and Wagner, and

01:31:45.464 --> 01:31:49.384
Prigozhin's mutiny, Preston, what
do you think about this maybe

01:31:49.424 --> 01:31:53.044
even in the modern context,
overfixation on particular

01:31:53.424 --> 01:31:56.564
symbolic regions or
territories or cities versus

01:31:57.084 --> 01:32:00.584
the territorial gains in the
broader context of the war?

01:32:00.904 --> 01:32:04.664
- It's difficult, and I think it's case by case
because as Mike mentioned, sometimes it gets this,

01:32:04.784 --> 01:32:08.684
this added value just because
it's held for a period of time,

01:32:08.924 --> 01:32:12.544
and it was hard to take for a month, and then two months,
and then three months. Then all of a sudden it's this

01:32:13.244 --> 01:32:17.184
fortress city when maybe it shouldn't
be that. But both sides, and that's

01:32:17.244 --> 01:32:20.564
exactly what we saw with Bakhmut, where
it was on both sides, and you saw Russia

01:32:21.044 --> 01:32:24.124
was very hyper-focused on taking the town-

01:32:24.124 --> 01:32:27.604
... to where they had to. And if they weren't going
to in a period of time, it was gonna be a failure.

01:32:27.624 --> 01:32:30.624
And we started to see them
claim ownership of the...

01:32:31.924 --> 01:32:35.744
pretty early on, and they were maybe exaggerating
their advances, but it showed just how

01:32:35.764 --> 01:32:37.664
important that was to Moscow.

01:32:38.504 --> 01:32:41.384
It made the same importance
then inside of Ukraine. So

01:32:42.644 --> 01:32:46.444
beforehand, I don't think anybody would've expected
that. Now, as Mike mentioned, looking back, there's some

01:32:46.484 --> 01:32:47.164
debate around it.

01:32:48.084 --> 01:32:51.884
But I think it's the kind of thing that
just happens upon a country, happens upon a

01:32:51.924 --> 01:32:54.604
military. And there was a lot of criticism

01:32:55.584 --> 01:32:59.144
on some Ukrainian military leaders at the
time, and we've seen that in a couple other

01:32:59.224 --> 01:33:03.064
areas as well, the Kursk offensive.
Avdiivka, I believe there was some,

01:33:03.184 --> 01:33:06.944
some debate around that as well.
Right now, I think we're gonna start

01:33:07.004 --> 01:33:10.084
seeing more around
Kostiantynivka. It's an area that

01:33:11.184 --> 01:33:15.384
it's starting to look like some of
these other areas that got encircled,

01:33:16.184 --> 01:33:20.083
and it's a big ask of the military leadership
to be able to recognize that. It's...

01:33:20.184 --> 01:33:23.984
Nobody wants to withdraw from that
area, especially in Ukraine's position.

01:33:24.024 --> 01:33:27.984
They're-- they would be giving up
terrain that is their country to the

01:33:28.024 --> 01:33:31.884
advancing Russian army. But at
some point, it's no longer worth

01:33:31.904 --> 01:33:35.584
holding on, and you're risking the troops
that are forward. It's a very difficult

01:33:35.624 --> 01:33:39.144
decision to make, and I
think it's more emotional

01:33:39.664 --> 01:33:41.204
than we can recognize from the outside.

01:33:42.144 --> 01:33:45.064
- Yeah. And that point is a judgment call.

01:33:46.224 --> 01:33:50.184
When are you no longer exhausting your
opponent with a very favorable attrition ratio

01:33:50.204 --> 01:33:52.204
in an urban fight 'cause
you're on the defense,

01:33:53.204 --> 01:33:57.184
and they're on the offense, and they're
likely to take more casualties, versus when

01:33:57.364 --> 01:34:01.424
are you facing potential encirclement?
There's a threat to your force.

01:34:02.564 --> 01:34:06.504
The lines of communication are being interdicted,
so you're losing personnel and equipment

01:34:06.624 --> 01:34:10.544
trying to sustain your positions. And
then lastly, usually when you can really

01:34:10.604 --> 01:34:14.444
see it, is when you're forced to counterattack
in order to maintain your positions because now

01:34:14.464 --> 01:34:18.264
you're taking casualties because you're the one going
on the offensive in order to try to even maintain the

01:34:18.284 --> 01:34:21.784
position. And we've seen that time
and time again in a number of fights.

01:34:22.384 --> 01:34:26.064
In some cases, justifiable, in
other cases, you can see it

01:34:26.364 --> 01:34:31.384
may be politically motivated. At the
end of the day, military strategy

01:34:32.204 --> 01:34:35.624
flows from decisions of
political leaders. Political

01:34:35.684 --> 01:34:39.604
decision-making holds supremacy,
and they decide what is a critical

01:34:39.644 --> 01:34:43.584
objective and which battle to them
is essential in the war. Even

01:34:43.604 --> 01:34:47.464
if analysts or somebody else or planners
looking at it might say, "This is not a

01:34:47.504 --> 01:34:51.484
critical fight," that doesn't matter. When political leaders
decide that it's a critical fight, and then when they

01:34:51.544 --> 01:34:55.484
raise political stakes and say that they're
gonna try to hold on to the city, then

01:34:55.524 --> 01:34:56.684
now it becomes important.

01:34:57.164 --> 01:35:02.804
- Can you speak to what does it
take in the modern context to

01:35:03.604 --> 01:35:08.124
gain control of a territory or to,
quote unquote, "take a territory"?

01:35:09.084 --> 01:35:12.924
There's something that I've heard both of you
speak about, which is that on the Russian

01:35:13.004 --> 01:35:17.724
side, the Russian military claims more
territory than they have actually taken-

01:35:17.724 --> 01:35:21.324
... and that, and the difference between
how much territory is actually taken

01:35:22.084 --> 01:35:25.564
and the claimed taken territory
has an effect on the actual

01:35:25.624 --> 01:35:29.404
operation of the military. Can you just
speak to what it takes to gain control of a

01:35:29.443 --> 01:35:29.864
territory?

01:35:30.364 --> 01:35:34.184
- I think that's up for debate,
and we see Russian analysts

01:35:34.304 --> 01:35:36.664
getting frustrated and debating
this day in and day out

01:35:37.604 --> 01:35:41.544
because it kind of happens in phases.
If you look at a, just a town

01:35:41.584 --> 01:35:44.844
in Ukraine, there's a period of time
where it's normal, and there's a

01:35:45.064 --> 01:35:48.944
population there, and the businesses are open.
But then as the front gets closer, you start to

01:35:49.004 --> 01:35:52.704
see the population leave, and the businesses
close, and the next thing you know, it's just

01:35:52.784 --> 01:35:56.484
military. And then at some point, there's
no military because it's in the gray

01:35:56.584 --> 01:36:00.044
zone, and you've got Russians in
one side, Ukrainians on the other.

01:36:00.664 --> 01:36:04.544
What Russia has done a lot
this... I feel like it's

01:36:04.604 --> 01:36:08.344
increased, is they will have
one soldier forward. They'll

01:36:08.384 --> 01:36:12.364
wave a flag for a drone to pick up.
They will mark that on a map and then

01:36:12.404 --> 01:36:15.004
say, "Everything from there
back we now control."

01:36:15.184 --> 01:36:18.984
- So like a video of that soldier
with that flag in that area.

01:36:19.324 --> 01:36:23.864
- Usually a video. This last week, we saw
some AI being utilized where it was a-

01:36:23.884 --> 01:36:24.644
- Like a fake video.

01:36:24.764 --> 01:36:28.724
- Yeah. It was a fake video called out
by the Russians themselves saying,

01:36:28.764 --> 01:36:32.104
"Guys, what are we doing here?
This is not good." Um, but-

01:36:32.144 --> 01:36:32.184
- Wow

01:36:32.224 --> 01:36:36.124
- ... even if it is a real soldier, that's
the PR victory of saying, "Look how

01:36:36.264 --> 01:36:39.064
far we've advanced. We now
control this territory."

01:36:40.224 --> 01:36:45.484
The Russian pushback there is one soldier
in a basement is not control of territory.

01:36:46.684 --> 01:36:50.384
Now, is it a platoon of
soldiers? Is it a regiment

01:36:50.664 --> 01:36:54.564
pushing forward beyond that town? That's
up for debate because even if you have

01:36:54.624 --> 01:36:58.324
a large number of troops in that
village, if you can't resupply them

01:36:58.904 --> 01:37:02.664
or if Ukraine has drone dominance in that entire
area, and you can't get anyone in or out,

01:37:03.404 --> 01:37:05.844
you know, what are we talking
about control of the village?

01:37:05.884 --> 01:37:09.684
You don't really have it.
So it's tricky in that

01:37:09.744 --> 01:37:13.444
sense, and I think if you're talking
about full-blown control, as in you can

01:37:13.524 --> 01:37:17.344
move, you can come and go from that
town as see fit, you're probably

01:37:17.404 --> 01:37:20.504
talking about 20-plus kilometers
back from the front line.

01:37:20.564 --> 01:37:24.444
And even then, you're still at risk of
getting hit every single day by drones.

01:37:24.504 --> 01:37:28.064
But these forward areas, it's
been interesting, man. Russia has

01:37:28.584 --> 01:37:32.464
... A concern that I have is we're
starting to see Putin more and more

01:37:32.644 --> 01:37:36.044
reference some of these claims
that are not in line with

01:37:36.144 --> 01:37:40.103
reality, and I really wonder where up

01:37:40.164 --> 01:37:43.984
that chain of command they understand that
they are sending a false report. The AI

01:37:44.024 --> 01:37:46.664
video is a great example.
Somebody made that.

01:37:46.964 --> 01:37:50.344
So where up that chain does somebody know

01:37:51.164 --> 01:37:55.044
this is not an accurate report and we're not
there, and they send it anyways? Or is it

01:37:55.084 --> 01:37:58.924
going all the way up, and everybody
genuinely believes that to be true?

01:37:59.004 --> 01:38:02.704
Because it paints a very different
picture of how the war is playing out.

01:38:03.244 --> 01:38:07.204
- I think on the Russian maps, they consistently
claim themselves to be something like

01:38:07.244 --> 01:38:12.024
15 kilometers, 10 kilometers at least
forward of where they actually are, and

01:38:13.104 --> 01:38:16.584
they regularly cite
positions they had from

01:38:17.244 --> 01:38:20.704
soldiers that they might have in place somewhere
that infiltrated, but they don't have them anymore.

01:38:20.724 --> 01:38:24.104
They've been lost. Or as Preston says,
they took pictures of some flags.

01:38:25.484 --> 01:38:29.464
And it actually very negatively
shapes Russian operations.

01:38:29.564 --> 01:38:33.244
In some ways, we are fortunate that they
have such a culture of falsification

01:38:33.584 --> 01:38:37.084
of battlefield data because
they then organize fire

01:38:37.184 --> 01:38:39.844
support around lines and positions
they don't actually hold.

01:38:41.084 --> 01:38:44.944
Their troops will often advance,
or units that rotate into an area

01:38:44.984 --> 01:38:49.844
will be given the wrong information,
and what they think are actually,

01:38:50.684 --> 01:38:56.724
their forward line of troops is contested
territory, right? So they'll end up,

01:38:57.864 --> 01:39:02.284
having their own soldiers killed because
the map they were given is not correct.

01:39:05.044 --> 01:39:08.344
I think Preston's also very right that it's
clear this information trickles up to political

01:39:08.424 --> 01:39:12.764
decision-making and to Putin, and that
that may be part of the reason why

01:39:13.764 --> 01:39:17.544
he holds the view that Russian
forces are still advancing in the

01:39:17.564 --> 01:39:22.884
Donbas. Because in fact, the maps being
supplied lie in a very literal sense.

01:39:23.844 --> 01:39:27.744
And also, believe it or not, there
is a pattern to Russian offensive

01:39:27.804 --> 01:39:30.064
operations where often we see them

01:39:31.164 --> 01:39:34.964
really intensify offensive efforts
right now during this time period from

01:39:35.004 --> 01:39:38.044
October to November. And,

01:39:38.924 --> 01:39:45.204
and their casualties go up, and it is often
because they find themselves pressed to

01:39:45.344 --> 01:39:48.504
actually take the territory that
they've been claiming to control-

01:39:48.504 --> 01:39:49.684
... in reports for months.

01:39:49.964 --> 01:39:50.484
- Right. Right.

01:39:50.504 --> 01:39:54.284
- Because now they're at a point where senior
commanders are basically saying, "Now show it to

01:39:54.344 --> 01:39:57.984
me," and they don't actually control
this terrain, and now they have to

01:39:58.144 --> 01:40:02.044
really throw men at it to try to
realize the lie that they've been

01:40:02.084 --> 01:40:06.484
telling for some time. And I
completely agree what Preston said.

01:40:07.704 --> 01:40:11.424
We have a very hard time
mapping it. It is a gray zone

01:40:12.084 --> 01:40:15.524
for some substantial distance, and to me,

01:40:16.864 --> 01:40:19.164
probably the best way
to describe control is

01:40:20.024 --> 01:40:23.964
you're able to use terrain
for your purposes.

01:40:24.044 --> 01:40:27.944
That is, not only do you ... Have you deny it
to your enemy, not only do you have presence

01:40:27.984 --> 01:40:31.944
there, but you are able to
largely use this terrain, and

01:40:31.984 --> 01:40:35.764
the enemy does not have substantial amount
of fire control over it. Because at this

01:40:35.804 --> 01:40:39.464
stage of the war, yes, you can go
15, 10 kilometers from the front

01:40:39.524 --> 01:40:43.284
line. Those are technically
your positions, but you can't

01:40:43.324 --> 01:40:47.084
freely move around on them.
You can't do much of what a

01:40:47.364 --> 01:40:51.084
ground force would expect to do. You
have presence, but you don't really have

01:40:51.124 --> 01:40:54.224
control. You're not able to use it for
your own purposes. And many places,

01:40:55.164 --> 01:40:59.064
Ukrainian-Russian positions in cities,
or sometimes in tree lines, are so

01:40:59.104 --> 01:41:01.344
commingled that they're marbled.

01:41:01.704 --> 01:41:05.244
And it's very hard to map control at all because
if there's hundreds of Russian troops in

01:41:05.264 --> 01:41:06.544
Kostiantynivka in basements

01:41:07.504 --> 01:41:10.844
commingled with hundreds of Ukrainian
troops in basements, who controls

01:41:10.904 --> 01:41:14.844
Kostiantynivka? How would you know that?
How would you empirically judge it?

01:41:15.664 --> 01:41:19.344
- It's crazy when you really think about
it, Lex. Like, I could color a map on the

01:41:19.384 --> 01:41:22.964
screen, and nothing matters. Doesn't
really change anything. The fact that

01:41:23.344 --> 01:41:27.164
leaders are making those changes and then
sending their troops forward with bad

01:41:27.204 --> 01:41:30.844
information is crazy. It's costing
lives. Like, at the very least,

01:41:31.464 --> 01:41:35.384
you know, when they say there was Kupiansk a little
over a year ago, Russia claimed ownership of

01:41:35.404 --> 01:41:39.344
it. This was one that Putin said that they had control
over. He famously invited international journalists.

01:41:39.384 --> 01:41:41.064
He said, "Go in and check
it out for yourselves,"

01:41:41.964 --> 01:41:45.864
and they didn't. And what that
did then was you could see

01:41:45.924 --> 01:41:49.604
Russian forces were able to
relocate assets to other areas.

01:41:50.384 --> 01:41:54.224
Air power and fire support that
might be needed in certain areas was

01:41:54.264 --> 01:41:57.444
either pushed forward or pulled back
because it wasn't needed there anymore.

01:41:57.804 --> 01:42:00.944
So you've got these
soldiers out there that now

01:42:01.004 --> 01:42:04.224
supposedly are 10 kilometers
forward. They can't get

01:42:04.724 --> 01:42:08.604
airstrikes on the Ukrainian positions because
if they call that up, somebody is gonna have to

01:42:08.644 --> 01:42:10.804
say, "Yeah, okay,

01:42:11.624 --> 01:42:15.484
the lines have changed a little bit."
So they're genuinely making these

01:42:15.524 --> 01:42:19.384
changes, making these false reports,
and it is ... It's hard to fully

01:42:19.424 --> 01:42:22.204
understand, to be hon- hard
to grasp. It's costing lives.

01:42:22.544 --> 01:42:26.344
- If you want an anecdote, I was just
west of Kupiansk at that time last year

01:42:26.444 --> 01:42:30.324
when Gerasimov declared to Putin in the
meeting that they had all the Ukrainian units

01:42:30.364 --> 01:42:33.824
in Kupiansk encircled. But I was there with a
colleague from one of the Ukrainian brigades,

01:42:34.984 --> 01:42:38.624
basically reading this news, and it,

01:42:39.664 --> 01:42:43.084
it's remarkable. I mean, when you're
actually right by there and you see the

01:42:43.144 --> 01:42:46.704
situation, these things don't pass
the giggle test, but nonetheless,

01:42:47.324 --> 01:42:51.124
this is what you see Russian senior military
leadership tell their political leadership.

01:42:51.684 --> 01:42:56.184
- To me, this is fascinating because
maybe you can educate me on this, but

01:42:58.074 --> 01:43:01.514
My guess would be throughout human history,
throughout all the wars, whether you look at

01:43:01.554 --> 01:43:05.294
Napoleon, Hitler, Churchill,
Stalin, maybe even Genghis

01:43:05.374 --> 01:43:09.314
Khan, although the interesting dynamic there,
there's always going to be some distortion of

01:43:09.374 --> 01:43:11.294
information as it travels
up the chain of command.

01:43:11.694 --> 01:43:15.114
And you can study where
the distortion is greatest

01:43:15.374 --> 01:43:19.154
depending on the culture. Who...
Is it a soldier lying to their

01:43:19.414 --> 01:43:23.374
commander? Is it the general lying
to the commander-in-chief? The,

01:43:23.474 --> 01:43:27.254
there's some distortion happening
somewhere. So maybe can you speak to the

01:43:27.294 --> 01:43:29.074
distortions happening on both sides here?

01:43:29.394 --> 01:43:32.954
- From what I can see, Ukraine
is getting a much better

01:43:33.074 --> 01:43:36.994
picture of the current status
along the front. Very hard to say

01:43:37.194 --> 01:43:41.134
where, and on both sides, very hard to
say if and where there's a breakdown,

01:43:41.174 --> 01:43:44.834
and you're absolutely right. Commanders
want to say that they're doing a good job.

01:43:44.874 --> 01:43:48.554
It takes a lot of integrity
to say, "We lost positions."

01:43:48.614 --> 01:43:50.134
And we've seen that in
the Ukrainian military-

01:43:50.214 --> 01:43:53.754
... where commanders have been relieved because
they've not reported that up correctly.

01:43:54.594 --> 01:43:58.354
It seems to be much more, much
more of a culture issue in the

01:43:58.394 --> 01:44:02.114
Russian military, top to bottom. But
yeah, it's definitely happening on both

01:44:02.153 --> 01:44:04.634
sides. Definitely, definitely
happening to a degree.

01:44:05.494 --> 01:44:08.014
Hard to tell where that line is.

01:44:08.794 --> 01:44:12.674
- I think it's worse on the Russian side.
It's because of the military culture.

01:44:13.114 --> 01:44:16.294
First, there is a real lack of
trust in the Russian military

01:44:16.654 --> 01:44:23.374
between soldiers and their commanders, and
that breeds a lack of cohesion. Second,

01:44:23.894 --> 01:44:28.814
the military runs on a very
coercive culture, so you expect

01:44:29.214 --> 01:44:31.634
that for any mistake, you're
going to be punished, right?

01:44:32.734 --> 01:44:40.594
And so there's a strong incentive
to lie. And then lastly, there is a

01:44:40.634 --> 01:44:44.414
centralization of command and
control, where rather than the

01:44:44.494 --> 01:44:48.414
junior officer who's actually at the position
commanding the troops who has greater

01:44:48.434 --> 01:44:50.674
authority, it's the senior officer
who has greater authority.

01:44:51.634 --> 01:44:55.594
Because they decide everything, and then
they kind of give the scheme of maneuver

01:44:55.634 --> 01:44:59.094
the orders for junior folks to execute.

01:44:59.874 --> 01:45:03.694
And for the Russian military, the
effects are worse because the

01:45:03.794 --> 01:45:07.533
way they plan military decision-making can

01:45:07.753 --> 01:45:11.594
often lead to, you know,
what in data is called a

01:45:11.614 --> 01:45:14.954
garbage in, garbage out problem,
where if the information

01:45:15.854 --> 01:45:19.574
on what's happening at the tactical
level is not correct, it's false,

01:45:20.234 --> 01:45:24.034
and the planning is based on that, and people
are executing combat tasks on the basis of

01:45:24.074 --> 01:45:29.034
things that aren't true, then it's very
problematic for that military, especially

01:45:29.094 --> 01:45:34.534
because they don't have the same
culture of having freedom to

01:45:34.594 --> 01:45:38.534
adjust, adapt, make decisions
at a more junior level

01:45:38.594 --> 01:45:42.074
on the ground, right? They're waiting for
information to reach the senior commander.

01:45:43.034 --> 01:45:44.974
Um, I don't wanna make it sound

01:45:45.834 --> 01:45:49.234
this kind of ridiculously regimented,
but nonetheless, it's a bigger

01:45:49.394 --> 01:45:53.114
problem for the Russian military, as we see
consistently. Even though the Ukrainian Armed

01:45:53.134 --> 01:45:55.874
Forces have also had issues with
falsification of information,

01:45:56.834 --> 01:46:00.714
and commanders, planning commanders getting
relieved, especially under the previous

01:46:00.774 --> 01:46:01.814
commander-in-chief, Syrskyi.

01:46:02.654 --> 01:46:05.654
And sometimes also because they were
placed in an untenable position,

01:46:06.634 --> 01:46:11.434
because they wouldn't have enough
men. They had to defend the position.

01:46:12.514 --> 01:46:16.414
They too were told that they can't
withdraw a position without approval from

01:46:16.434 --> 01:46:17.134
the general staff.

01:46:18.314 --> 01:46:21.874
And if they lost the position, they reported it,
they would be told they had to take it back,

01:46:22.294 --> 01:46:25.714
but they didn't have the forces to
take it back. And then a commander is

01:46:26.074 --> 01:46:30.054
sometimes faced with an ethical
dilemma. Do I sacrifice men to

01:46:30.094 --> 01:46:33.154
take a position that I know is
untenable or I can't take back?

01:46:34.514 --> 01:46:38.434
Or do I face the consequences of
failing to report it, but I preserve my

01:46:38.454 --> 01:46:42.054
own men? What do I do? These are not the
easiest choices, you know? It's not clean cut.

01:46:42.814 --> 01:46:46.574
- It seems from my just observational history,
there seems to be a, especially, like, at the

01:46:46.634 --> 01:46:52.634
general level, a kind of optimism that
there's a path towards winning this war at a

01:46:52.714 --> 01:46:56.594
strategic level soon. It
seems like United States

01:46:56.894 --> 01:47:00.754
and other nations have been
engaged in, you know, in,

01:47:00.874 --> 01:47:04.834
in case of Afghanistan and Iraq is
like something like forever wars. How

01:47:04.874 --> 01:47:08.434
does that... At which level
of distortion of information

01:47:09.214 --> 01:47:13.054
do you keep going with this
kind of momentum of a war going

01:47:13.154 --> 01:47:16.814
on for a prolonged period of time
without any strategic victory?

01:47:17.134 --> 01:47:20.294
Or is that not attributable to distortion
of information and something else?

01:47:20.794 --> 01:47:23.134
- Oh, I think Afghanistan's a
very interesting case study-

01:47:23.154 --> 01:47:26.994
... of how we ended up for 20 years in
that war. I would... Preston, I think you

01:47:27.054 --> 01:47:29.354
should talk a bit about that since
you were in that fight. But-

01:47:29.394 --> 01:47:33.134
- Well, there's a pride aspect to it
of can you get it done? Yes, I can.

01:47:33.694 --> 01:47:37.234
And it's not necessarily overstating
what you're able to do, but

01:47:37.294 --> 01:47:41.174
understanding you, there are
constraints on your resources

01:47:41.374 --> 01:47:45.034
and what you have available to
accomplish the mission, and

01:47:45.094 --> 01:47:48.054
nobody wants to be the one
to say, "I can't do that."

01:47:49.694 --> 01:47:53.634
And there's yeah, there's just
some pride in, in finding a way to

01:47:53.674 --> 01:47:57.154
make it work, and I think a
lot of that comes down to bad

01:47:57.214 --> 01:48:01.154
advice at some level of setting those
conditions. They, you should not

01:48:01.174 --> 01:48:05.074
be put in that position if you cannot accomplish
the task. Afghanistan, great example would be

01:48:05.554 --> 01:48:09.154
we had combat units flooded across the
entirety of the country. Great for

01:48:09.194 --> 01:48:13.074
security, not necessarily great for nation
building, not necessarily great for,

01:48:13.594 --> 01:48:17.394
developing, changing a culture, if
that was even possible in Afghanistan,

01:48:17.774 --> 01:48:21.494
to get the people out in Zhari
District in Kandahar Province to

01:48:21.534 --> 01:48:25.434
understand that the person in Kabul that
doesn't speak their language and they've

01:48:25.474 --> 01:48:29.354
never seen before represents
them. That's not, that's not a

01:48:29.414 --> 01:48:32.714
soldier's job. We don't know how to do
that. We're not very good at it. But

01:48:34.134 --> 01:48:37.974
we don't want to say we can't, so we
say, "We got it. We can figure it out.

01:48:38.434 --> 01:48:42.174
We'll learn counterinsurgency
operations. We will send

01:48:42.214 --> 01:48:45.854
people to language courses,"
when in reality, somewhere

01:48:45.934 --> 01:48:48.634
along the line, somebody should have
said, "You know what? This isn't

01:48:49.594 --> 01:48:54.274
what we can do." We need State
Department. We need USAID. We need XYZ

01:48:54.794 --> 01:48:58.774
to be able to step in and fill this. It's, I think it's
a military mindset, and I don't think that's unique

01:48:58.814 --> 01:49:02.714
to the United States. I imagine most
countries, the higher you get up the ranks,

01:49:02.754 --> 01:49:04.754
the generals are very much, "We got this."

01:49:05.374 --> 01:49:07.854
- I wanna add to Preston's
great perspective,

01:49:08.914 --> 01:49:12.334
the point of view from the
other side, having had a bit of

01:49:12.354 --> 01:49:14.794
experience dealing with
political leadership.

01:49:16.494 --> 01:49:19.814
Sometimes the information that the
military's passing up is overly

01:49:19.894 --> 01:49:23.734
optimistic. It's, you know, this mindset
of, "I don't wanna say it can't get done."

01:49:24.434 --> 01:49:28.054
But there are also definitely times
where the political leadership has clear

01:49:28.094 --> 01:49:31.574
assumptions and is very
resistant to information

01:49:32.434 --> 01:49:34.494
that is adverse to those
assumptions. First,

01:49:35.454 --> 01:49:39.274
one of the basic inputs into
wars when leaders think they are

01:49:39.314 --> 01:49:43.054
going to go to war is war
optimism. They have a clear set of

01:49:43.074 --> 01:49:47.054
assumptions. Often it's that the war
will be sharp and short, that it'll be a

01:49:47.094 --> 01:49:53.774
quick war. Good example, Russian full-scale
invasion in 2022 or our war with Iran

01:49:53.794 --> 01:49:58.894
this year. They are resistant
to information that

01:49:59.034 --> 01:50:03.154
actually it's quite risky, what will
happen if it becomes a prolonged war.

01:50:04.354 --> 01:50:08.234
When military leaders tell them that it might
be this, it might be that, they need all

01:50:08.254 --> 01:50:12.074
these forces, they face the
problem that if they basically

01:50:12.114 --> 01:50:15.974
tell them, "This can't be done," the
political leader will tell them,

01:50:16.054 --> 01:50:19.454
"No, you're just the wrong person for the job. I'll
find somebody who will tell me they can do it.

01:50:20.154 --> 01:50:23.874
You think you need 300,000 troops for Iraq? I'll
find someone who will do it for a lot less,"

01:50:24.054 --> 01:50:29.254
okay? And lastly, there are often cases
also, let's say you have a prolonged

01:50:29.274 --> 01:50:32.354
conventional war like Afghanistan,
and political leadership might ask

01:50:33.214 --> 01:50:36.634
not what do you need to win
the war. It's not clear that

01:50:36.814 --> 01:50:42.674
victory defined in their terms is
necessarily possible. They'll ask, "Really,

01:50:43.514 --> 01:50:46.994
what is the amount of troops you need
for me to kick this can down the road

01:50:47.614 --> 01:50:51.574
so that we don't lose the war on my watch,
and then it goes to the next election

01:50:51.674 --> 01:50:54.114
and then maybe becomes somebody
else's problem?" Okay?

01:50:55.134 --> 01:50:57.234
So there are many times
where I've seen that

01:50:58.054 --> 01:51:01.474
it's not the military leadership that's
to blame, it's that the political

01:51:01.554 --> 01:51:05.334
leadership, which at the end of the day
makes key decisions on the war, sets

01:51:05.354 --> 01:51:08.934
constraints on the military of how they can
fight, what forces they have available to fight,

01:51:09.674 --> 01:51:13.313
have certain assumptions, have
certain ideas, and they're

01:51:13.454 --> 01:51:17.414
not necessarily amenable to military advice.
Where they take-- They're listening to the

01:51:17.454 --> 01:51:21.174
military advice, but they're also
considering domestic politics, they're

01:51:21.214 --> 01:51:24.774
considering international politics.
There's other things that

01:51:25.514 --> 01:51:29.494
predominate in their
calculus other than strict

01:51:29.534 --> 01:51:33.394
kind of operational planning
or military analysis.

01:51:33.774 --> 01:51:37.534
And I think that's important to consider because
wars at the end of the day are started by

01:51:37.574 --> 01:51:41.474
political leaders. They're the ones who decide
to go to war, and they have very strong

01:51:41.534 --> 01:51:45.514
views and assumptions, and some extent also
emotions and fears that play into that.

01:51:46.354 --> 01:51:50.034
- How often is it that military
leadership, like the generals,

01:51:50.574 --> 01:51:53.814
try to say, "Don't do this"?
In terms of starting wars,

01:51:54.474 --> 01:51:58.134
this comes to a bigger theme
is I would love to see a long

01:51:58.234 --> 01:52:02.154
distance future of human civilization
that doesn't have wars. And it

01:52:02.214 --> 01:52:07.514
seems like we start wars,
we broadly, humanity.

01:52:07.574 --> 01:52:14.234
And I would love to know which are the
humans amongst us that can stop them

01:52:15.194 --> 01:52:15.934
from starting.

01:52:16.414 --> 01:52:22.114
- I think there are plenty of times
where military leaders give the advice

01:52:23.194 --> 01:52:26.894
that a war could be a risky or a costly

01:52:26.994 --> 01:52:30.734
misadventure. They don't
necessarily say, "Don't

01:52:30.934 --> 01:52:35.434
do this," but the advice
they give and they lay out,

01:52:36.334 --> 01:52:38.514
right, has clear implications to it.

01:52:39.814 --> 01:52:43.794
And there are plenty of times where
military leaders or not military

01:52:43.854 --> 01:52:48.194
leaders, let's say civilian
leaders, in our case, uh,

01:52:49.314 --> 01:52:54.254
what was the Department of Defense,
will advise against a war, right?

01:52:55.414 --> 01:53:01.474
Yes, there are plenty of times where
they don't give that advice. But,

01:53:03.054 --> 01:53:04.674
I'd be open to Preston's views on this,

01:53:05.794 --> 01:53:09.674
but if there's some public sense
of the military profession as

01:53:09.714 --> 01:53:13.654
folks that are actually eager to go to a war,
that's often not the case, and that's not been

01:53:13.694 --> 01:53:17.614
my experience. People who have
commanded other folks in combat

01:53:17.734 --> 01:53:20.954
and who have lost lives and who understand
the implications of that endeavor

01:53:21.734 --> 01:53:24.734
are very rarely eager to pursue it,

01:53:25.874 --> 01:53:29.754
right? Especially if they're not sure
that the cause is right or that it's

01:53:29.794 --> 01:53:33.674
been well thought through or that the
strategy makes sense or that this is very

01:53:33.714 --> 01:53:36.974
much a necessary war. So
that's not been my impression.

01:53:37.814 --> 01:53:41.774
- One of the challenges there, Lex, is it's not, at least
the way we operate in the United States, it's not

01:53:41.794 --> 01:53:45.414
the military's job to make the decision
to go to war or not. It's their job to

01:53:45.454 --> 01:53:49.414
advise on what the capabilities
are, what the options are for

01:53:49.454 --> 01:53:53.074
the president to eventu- the commander
in chief to eventually make that

01:53:53.094 --> 01:53:57.774
decision. So I think we have to be
careful with that, that bleeding into...

01:53:58.694 --> 01:54:00.574
War is a political endeavor.

01:54:00.614 --> 01:54:04.494
So when the military starts advising on
we should or we shouldn't go to war,

01:54:04.974 --> 01:54:08.534
I think that's a very slippery slope.
They can say, "This will or will not

01:54:08.594 --> 01:54:09.894
accomplish our objectives.

01:54:10.694 --> 01:54:14.654
You know, if we drop a couple bombs, it might not
get us to the point where we actually can say

01:54:14.694 --> 01:54:16.614
that we got what we wanted out of this."

01:54:17.414 --> 01:54:21.374
But broadly speaking, yes or no
to war, I think is probably not

01:54:21.414 --> 01:54:22.634
best reserved for the military.

01:54:23.034 --> 01:54:27.014
- Absolutely. War is a sociopolitical
phenomenon, and it is political

01:54:27.054 --> 01:54:31.014
leadership that has to not just make these
decisions, but holds the supremacy in making

01:54:31.034 --> 01:54:34.694
them and the accountability for it. And
in our system, specifically in the United

01:54:34.794 --> 01:54:35.074
States,

01:54:36.234 --> 01:54:39.974
the senior-most military officer, the chairman
of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, does not

01:54:40.014 --> 01:54:43.574
command forces. They are
the top advisor on military

01:54:43.614 --> 01:54:47.534
affairs to the president. Actually
quite different than other systems

01:54:47.554 --> 01:54:53.294
where you might have,
Commander in chief or some-

01:54:53.514 --> 01:54:57.394
someone else that actually c- directly c--
For example, in the case of Ukraine's or the

01:54:57.434 --> 01:55:01.374
Russian military, where you have
a chief of general staff or a

01:55:01.414 --> 01:55:05.054
commander in chief that's a military position
that actually can directly command forces.

01:55:06.114 --> 01:55:09.654
- Speaking of structure and the hierarchy of
the different organizations in the military,

01:55:10.013 --> 01:55:11.074
getting back to Prigozhin.

01:55:11.214 --> 01:55:13.334
- Yes. Yes. Love this. Forgot about him.

01:55:13.414 --> 01:55:17.314
- We talked about 17 hours ago. So
what happened, what happened there?

01:55:17.374 --> 01:55:20.954
You talked about the Wagner Group
and Bakhmut. What was with the

01:55:20.994 --> 01:55:24.094
mutiny? What does it represent? What
does it show about the Russian military?

01:55:24.754 --> 01:55:28.494
- Okay, so Prigozhin had this ongoing
conflict with Gerasimov and

01:55:28.534 --> 01:55:32.314
Shoigu. Why? He was basically positioning
Wagner as this more successful

01:55:32.314 --> 01:55:36.234
alternative, okay? And I think he was
allowed to position Wagner in this way, and

01:55:36.294 --> 01:55:40.154
I think there were elites in Russia
who were backing him as well. So

01:55:40.194 --> 01:55:44.574
in some extent you had an internal
battle between different elite clans,

01:55:45.434 --> 01:55:49.034
one associated with the Ministry of Defense,
the military, another one backing Prigozhin.

01:55:50.094 --> 01:55:52.214
And Prigozhin was given this leeway.

01:55:53.234 --> 01:55:57.114
He would be constantly complaining that he
wasn't being given enough artillery ammo and so

01:55:57.174 --> 01:56:01.094
on and so forth. Even actually his effort was a priority
effort, and he was given a lot of artillery ammo

01:56:01.174 --> 01:56:06.494
to sustain it. And he was also building
out this kind of popular following and to

01:56:06.574 --> 01:56:11.914
some extent trying to see if he
could expand his political cachet.

01:56:13.014 --> 01:56:16.894
Ultimately, that competition between him and the
military, which played out at the leadership

01:56:16.954 --> 01:56:20.934
level, thus he actively attacked the
Ministry of Defense and the Chief

01:56:20.954 --> 01:56:24.854
of General Staff. But also
Wagner forces looked down on

01:56:24.874 --> 01:56:28.854
the regular Russian military, seeing them as incompetent,
ineffective, and the regular Russian military looked down on

01:56:28.894 --> 01:56:32.794
Wagner forces, seeing them as either
hired mercenaries or convicts,

01:56:32.934 --> 01:56:34.974
right? So there was this,
these clashes between them.

01:56:36.374 --> 01:56:40.254
And they competed with each other during
the Battle of Bakhmut, because even in the

01:56:40.294 --> 01:56:42.274
fight for Soledar and later
in the Battle of Bakhmut,

01:56:43.474 --> 01:56:46.274
um, Wagner and the Airborne
competed for who would take credit,

01:56:47.154 --> 01:56:50.554
right? And later on in the battles, we got
to April, May, Prigozhin started saying

01:56:51.994 --> 01:56:55.694
the regular military has pulled
artillery and fire support from him

01:56:55.794 --> 01:56:59.774
because they don't want Wagner to win. They want
Wagner to lose more personnel, and then they want

01:56:59.794 --> 01:57:02.994
to come in at the end and take the credit. He
basically advanced this conspiracy theory.

01:57:03.954 --> 01:57:06.014
But the mutiny really kicks off because

01:57:07.254 --> 01:57:10.874
at a certain point, the Russian
Ministry of Defense has had it,

01:57:11.474 --> 01:57:14.993
and Shoigu essentially, clearly a much more
powerful player at the end of the day,

01:57:15.614 --> 01:57:22.754
has an ultimatum issued that after the Battle
of Bakhmut is decided, that all Wagner

01:57:22.854 --> 01:57:26.594
personnel have to sign contracts
with the military, right? For a long

01:57:26.674 --> 01:57:30.554
time, Wagner was essentially an
autonomous force supported by the Russian

01:57:30.614 --> 01:57:33.794
military and generally integrated into

01:57:34.434 --> 01:57:38.134
an operational command, right?
And logistically all supported

01:57:38.174 --> 01:57:42.074
by the regular Russian armed forces. But

01:57:42.134 --> 01:57:46.014
with a huge amount of autonomy, where Prigozhin
was essentially running his own army and his

01:57:46.054 --> 01:57:48.794
own fight in coordination
with the Russian Airborne.

01:57:49.834 --> 01:57:52.934
And Shoigu's ultimatum trapped Prigozhin,

01:57:53.574 --> 01:57:56.954
essentially, and it put them in this
position. Wagner had withdrawn from Bakhmut

01:57:57.694 --> 01:58:07.514
and was replenishing personnel and was
essentially in rest in Luhansk. And

01:58:08.734 --> 01:58:11.794
Prigozhin sensed that he
was trapped, that either

01:58:13.034 --> 01:58:16.854
Shoigu was going to force the complete surrender
of Wagner's autonomy, and he would lose

01:58:16.874 --> 01:58:18.794
control, command and
control of his personnel,

01:58:19.594 --> 01:58:23.234
or he had to go on this reckless adventure.
And I think he may have conceived of this

01:58:23.434 --> 01:58:27.514
not as any attempt at regime
change, but some analysts

01:58:28.394 --> 01:58:33.214
that I know saw the mutiny as
essentially a petition. He then

01:58:34.214 --> 01:58:37.954
tries to advance on Rostov in
Russia, which is where the

01:58:37.994 --> 01:58:41.634
headquarters of the Russian military effort
for the war is. Wagner, if you remember those

01:58:41.714 --> 01:58:45.194
scenes, the footage,
actually encircles and

01:58:45.394 --> 01:58:47.614
occupies the headquarters.
There's a standoff.

01:58:47.754 --> 01:58:52.434
- I mean, it does look from the outside
as a, an attempt to take over power.

01:58:52.894 --> 01:58:56.794
- Well, absolutely. It is a mutiny. It is
a mutiny in the midst of a war, and he

01:58:56.834 --> 01:58:59.234
wants Shoigu and Gerasimov. He's

01:59:00.234 --> 01:59:05.134
essentially stuck though because they
are not in the Rostov headquarters,

01:59:06.074 --> 01:59:10.294
and Putin comes out very vocally

01:59:11.114 --> 01:59:14.454
seeing this and declaring this as
a betrayal in the midst of a war.

01:59:15.214 --> 01:59:20.394
And a whole set of Wagner columns, there
are two Wagner columns that are advancing

01:59:20.494 --> 01:59:22.533
past Rostov down the road

01:59:23.454 --> 01:59:26.774
towards Moscow. And by the way, at the
same time, they're shooting down Russian

01:59:26.853 --> 01:59:30.834
helicopters. They're shooting down Russian
planes. Actually, Wagner's mutiny is

01:59:30.874 --> 01:59:35.234
quite damaging for the Russian Air Force.
It kills a lot, quite a few personnel.

01:59:36.094 --> 01:59:39.754
And they're advancing towards Moscow. There's
some degree of panic because elites in Moscow

01:59:39.794 --> 01:59:43.614
themselves are not sure, 'cause all the Russian
forces are deployed in around Ukraine, and they're

01:59:43.654 --> 01:59:44.714
not prepared to face

01:59:45.774 --> 01:59:49.454
these Wagner troops who are actually fairly
experienced. And I want to be clear,

01:59:50.834 --> 01:59:54.594
they have tanks. They have air
defense with them. They have armored

01:59:54.674 --> 01:59:58.314
fighting vehicles. They brought a lot of
equipment with them to the fight. They're this...

01:59:58.454 --> 02:00:02.434
I want to be clear, they're not advancing
in trucks and pickups. This is actually

02:00:02.474 --> 02:00:03.414
a mechanized formation.

02:00:03.534 --> 02:00:05.974
- So how close did that come to
overthrowing power in Moscow?

02:00:06.554 --> 02:00:10.294
- Oh, I don't think very close. I don't think that
they had any chance of doing it, and Moscow's a

02:00:10.334 --> 02:00:14.174
massive city, so if they'd gotten in there, I
don't know what they could have hoped to achieve.

02:00:14.334 --> 02:00:16.274
- Was there some degree to
which that was the goal?

02:00:16.614 --> 02:00:22.614
- Look, I think what the goal was,
once they got to Rostov and had no

02:00:22.754 --> 02:00:26.194
chance of either getting their hands

02:00:26.734 --> 02:00:30.594
on Shoigu and Gerasimov, and Putin had
essentially ruled against them, their

02:00:30.674 --> 02:00:34.034
only leverage was to advance on Moscow.
They used the advance on Moscow,

02:00:34.374 --> 02:00:41.114
Prigozhin used it, to then
negotiate a stand down and

02:00:41.974 --> 02:00:45.514
amnesty and the h- and then a handover of

02:00:45.634 --> 02:00:49.514
assets and control of Wagner
operations over time. That's what

02:00:49.554 --> 02:00:51.154
happened. He used that
as bargaining leverage.

02:00:52.214 --> 02:00:56.194
I think he also early
on saw that he did not

02:00:56.234 --> 02:00:59.934
have support from the Russian elite. The Russian
elite did not see him as an alternative to

02:00:59.954 --> 02:01:02.974
Putin, so there's no chance
of success in regime change.

02:01:03.854 --> 02:01:07.834
And the reason for that is straightforward.
In personalist authoritarian regimes-

02:01:08.738 --> 02:01:10.738
The first thing they do is
eliminate alternatives.

02:01:11.598 --> 02:01:15.498
And most coups you never even hear of,
they fail. They take, they never take off

02:01:15.538 --> 02:01:18.618
the ground. This is even a
rare case of a real mutiny

02:01:19.578 --> 02:01:23.558
that becomes this phenomenal 24-hour
event. Most attempts at coups

02:01:23.578 --> 02:01:27.398
in personal authoritarian regimes are stopped by
intelligence. They never take off the ground.

02:01:27.878 --> 02:01:31.698
Because typically the number of elites and people
you have to involve in organizing a coup makes it

02:01:31.738 --> 02:01:35.038
very difficult to keep
it secret. So if that

02:01:35.078 --> 02:01:37.858
authoritarian system is at all
effective, it's going to discover it.

02:01:39.158 --> 02:01:42.498
But that's what I think ultimately
happened. And of course lastly,

02:01:42.918 --> 02:01:46.198
uh, I remember having these
discussion at the time, folks thought

02:01:46.978 --> 02:01:50.838
that Prigozhin somehow got off easy
because he negotiated a hand off

02:01:50.878 --> 02:01:54.838
or a sell off of his assets. He was still alive
after the coup. But it was clear to those of us

02:01:54.858 --> 02:01:57.918
who followed Russia that there was
no way he was going to survive this.

02:01:58.398 --> 02:02:01.878
- Speaking of which, going to
Perplexity, Yevgeny Prigozhin died

02:02:02.318 --> 02:02:06.258
on August 23rd, 2023, aged 62, when his

02:02:06.298 --> 02:02:09.918
private jet crashed in Russia's
Tver region while flying from

02:02:10.398 --> 02:02:12.458
Moscow to St. Petersburg.
Do you think he was killed?

02:02:12.598 --> 02:02:16.098
- Uh, it exploded in midair. It was very obvious
that he was killed, and he wasn't killed alone.

02:02:16.138 --> 02:02:19.438
He was killed along with several
senior commanders of Wagner.

02:02:20.278 --> 02:02:28.518
And to me, the only explanation is this
plane exploded in midair on video. Um,

02:02:30.098 --> 02:02:38.238
it was clear that he was never going to
survive this mutiny. And that very likely

02:02:39.158 --> 02:02:43.078
top folks involved in Wagner weren't
going to survive it either, and

02:02:43.138 --> 02:02:47.038
he was killed very demonstratively
as a demonstration to others.

02:02:47.338 --> 02:02:50.658
And so that outcome to me, and many other
folks who had watched Russia for years,

02:02:51.178 --> 02:02:52.358
was not surprising.

02:02:52.898 --> 02:02:54.878
- This is a fascinating
chapter in the war, Lex.

02:02:54.918 --> 02:02:58.898
I mean, there was a period of time there, that
48, 72 hours, where it was unknown, like what

02:02:58.918 --> 02:03:03.538
was gonna happen. There was a real consideration
that this might be the end of the war.

02:03:03.638 --> 02:03:07.578
Uh, a funny aspect here
is Prigozhin was one

02:03:07.618 --> 02:03:11.458
of his many companies, very wealthy, ran the
Internet Research Agency, which is kind of

02:03:11.518 --> 02:03:13.038
the bot farms in Russia,

02:03:13.938 --> 02:03:17.798
and Prigozhin and Wagner was so popular
at the time. But as Mike was talking

02:03:17.838 --> 02:03:21.758
about, one of the issues here was they were
the ones being celebrated. If you go back

02:03:21.778 --> 02:03:25.598
to this time period around Bakhmut,
the biggest, most significant battle

02:03:26.038 --> 02:03:27.338
of the war likely to date-

02:03:27.358 --> 02:03:31.498
- Yeah, nobody can tell you the other Russian
units that were operating anywhere else.

02:03:32.338 --> 02:03:36.298
But people know Wagner. They know that
name. They've probably seen pictures of

02:03:36.338 --> 02:03:40.298
Prigozhin. He was becoming famous.
So when this march happened,

02:03:40.658 --> 02:03:44.618
you had these bots. They
didn't know which direction

02:03:44.638 --> 02:03:48.198
to go. Are they still on team Wagner
because Prigozhin's their guy and he's

02:03:48.278 --> 02:03:52.138
capturing all of these cities? Or
wait a minute, is he against Putin

02:03:52.178 --> 02:03:53.798
now? Should we sit this one out? Mm-hmm.

02:03:54.058 --> 02:03:57.798
- And another interesting aspect
here is this, in my opinion, was

02:03:57.858 --> 02:04:01.778
something that Russia was doing very well,
and it almost worked out too well, and

02:04:01.798 --> 02:04:05.298
they didn't know how to kind
of direct it in the right way.

02:04:05.978 --> 02:04:09.338
Wagner was a private military company,
a private military group, kind of.

02:04:10.378 --> 02:04:13.878
They were not really a part of the Russian
government, and this gave them this

02:04:14.078 --> 02:04:17.878
weird autonomy to operate in places like
Syria. I mean, they went head-to-head.

02:04:17.998 --> 02:04:20.718
Wagner forces went head-to-head
with the US forces in Syria.

02:04:20.978 --> 02:04:22.258
- It went very badly for them.

02:04:22.558 --> 02:04:23.178
- Did not go well for Russia.

02:04:23.178 --> 02:04:26.798
- It was, I think it was 2018. Might have
been February 2018 outside of Deir ez-Zor.

02:04:27.118 --> 02:04:31.018
- Yeah. But it gave Russia this ability to
have deniability. Say, "These, this is a

02:04:31.038 --> 02:04:34.938
private company. This isn't our military."
But they effectively operate. I mean, they

02:04:34.958 --> 02:04:38.318
would fly in on Ministry of Defense aircraft.
They would train in Ministry of Defense,

02:04:38.898 --> 02:04:42.658
locations, and it was,
all things considered, a

02:04:42.718 --> 02:04:46.238
pretty effective mechanism of state
power that Russia had put together.

02:04:47.258 --> 02:04:51.178
They were pretty innovative in the
war. The concept of pulling these

02:04:51.238 --> 02:04:55.178
convicts in, which I just interviewed a
soldier that fought in Bakhmut, talked to

02:04:55.198 --> 02:04:59.058
him a couple weeks ago, and he talked about how
exhausting it was that these guys just kept

02:04:59.118 --> 02:05:03.918
coming. He said one after the other,
after the other. You couldn't stop.

02:05:04.858 --> 02:05:08.718
They were innovative in that sense,
but Wagner wasn't just prisoners. They

02:05:08.758 --> 02:05:14.458
had very lethal- ... well-trained assault
groups that were kind of leading the way

02:05:14.518 --> 02:05:17.658
in some of these new tactics because
in a sense it was like this,

02:05:18.538 --> 02:05:21.878
again, privatized army.
Prigozhin was competing

02:05:22.538 --> 02:05:26.358
to try to make them better, and I don't know
the different directions that could have

02:05:26.397 --> 02:05:30.378
gone, whether they branch out or
became a kind of elite assault

02:05:30.457 --> 02:05:37.758
force type thing, but they became too
popular. And then Shoigu and Gerasimov

02:05:37.798 --> 02:05:41.558
didn't really know. It seems like there
was a bit of a panic, and just shut it,

02:05:41.738 --> 02:05:47.818
shut it all down, and now it doesn't exist.
They did. There's, uh, the Africa Corps-

02:05:47.818 --> 02:05:51.358
... is operating in Africa. It's got
some of the old Wagner leadership,

02:05:51.898 --> 02:05:55.258
but they effectively did away
with all of that private military

02:05:55.918 --> 02:05:59.278
stuff, and said, "We gotta bring this all
in-house. We can't do that again." But again,

02:06:00.018 --> 02:06:02.198
the interesting piece there is I do think

02:06:03.098 --> 02:06:06.518
Wagner was onto something. They
were operating brutal, to be clear.

02:06:06.778 --> 02:06:10.338
Incredibly brutal organization.
I mean, there was an awful

02:06:10.438 --> 02:06:14.218
video of one of their guys,
I believe surrendered,

02:06:14.838 --> 02:06:17.978
and they got ahold of him, or he wouldn't
go on an assault or something. They,

02:06:18.918 --> 02:06:22.778
smashed his head with a hammer against
a cinder block, their own guy. So

02:06:22.838 --> 02:06:26.698
that's the group we're talking about,
to be clear. But they were effective,

02:06:27.218 --> 02:06:29.578
and Russia destroyed the organization.

02:06:30.618 --> 02:06:34.338
- There's an effect that Wagner did
have, and like referred to as the

02:06:34.378 --> 02:06:38.158
Wagnerization of the Russian military,
which is what happens after Wagner is

02:06:38.198 --> 02:06:43.298
dismantled. The Russian military
widely adopts Wagner tactics. It

02:06:43.338 --> 02:06:47.098
begins to contract convicts
in very large numbers.

02:06:47.638 --> 02:06:52.178
It creates assault companies
called Shtorm-Z and Shtorm-V.

02:06:53.078 --> 02:06:56.218
It begins to deploy assault
companies of convicts

02:06:56.998 --> 02:07:01.658
structured as assault groups across
the battalions and regular formations.

02:07:03.198 --> 02:07:07.038
It basically takes much of
what made Wagner successful

02:07:07.678 --> 02:07:11.458
and begins to spread this across the Russian
military. And then the Russian military

02:07:11.538 --> 02:07:15.010
begins to actually fight
Having seen how Wagner fought,

02:07:16.010 --> 02:07:19.810
primarily with assault groups and
assault detachments, using them

02:07:20.430 --> 02:07:25.010
as a constantly replenishable
assault force that they

02:07:26.350 --> 02:07:30.330
throw at the Ukrainian forces. And
for a period of time, believe it

02:07:30.350 --> 02:07:34.290
or not, in '23 and '24, many of

02:07:34.330 --> 02:07:38.010
these assault units are convict
staffed, and many others are not

02:07:38.050 --> 02:07:43.090
convict staffed, but people take special
contracts that pay more, essentially

02:07:44.170 --> 02:07:47.010
taking on the risk of
being an assault group.

02:07:47.870 --> 02:07:51.830
And so the Russian Ministry of Defense
and the military actually begins

02:07:51.850 --> 02:07:55.830
to copy and imitate a lot of Wagner tactics.
The Russian airborne also builds out assault

02:07:55.890 --> 02:07:57.990
groups and assault detachments. And

02:07:58.950 --> 02:08:02.390
following this for years, do
you see the Russian military

02:08:03.370 --> 02:08:06.370
actually fighting primarily
with these assault formations?

02:08:07.470 --> 02:08:12.150
And over time now, you have fewer and fewer
convict staff groups, but nonetheless,

02:08:13.030 --> 02:08:16.710
thousands upon thousands of convicts were
used by the Russian armed forces in assault

02:08:16.770 --> 02:08:20.670
units after Wagner.
Prigozhin said that he lost,

02:08:20.790 --> 02:08:24.730
killed in action 20,000
people in just the Battle of

02:08:24.750 --> 02:08:28.450
Bakhmut, which lasts somewhere, I
think, from August 2022 to May of '23.

02:08:29.130 --> 02:08:32.890
But the Russian military lost far more over the
course of the years in terms of convicts they

02:08:32.970 --> 02:08:36.890
used. So I just want to put this postscript that
Wagner as an organization may have died, but it,

02:08:37.230 --> 02:08:41.150
it in some ways infected the Russian
military, and these tactics spread across the

02:08:41.190 --> 02:08:45.110
Russian armed forces, which
both led to some success,

02:08:45.770 --> 02:08:49.610
but also led the Russian military to
overspecialize in using assault groups,

02:08:49.970 --> 02:08:53.690
which eventually led to diminishing
returns. They specialized in

02:08:53.790 --> 02:09:00.370
small-scale combat action, and over time
struggled to recover the ability to

02:09:00.410 --> 02:09:05.030
conduct larger-scale, more complex
operations by focusing on fighting this way.

02:09:06.350 --> 02:09:10.510
- I should mention for the listeners, for the
audience, that it is now past midnight,

02:09:11.790 --> 02:09:15.470
which is the only time we could do it.
So there's two people in front of me.

02:09:15.530 --> 02:09:19.450
They're real warriors and just powering
through. I really appreciate it, but quick

02:09:19.490 --> 02:09:21.150
bathroom break. All right, we're back,

02:09:22.030 --> 02:09:25.990
trying to power through. This might
be a good moment to ask about the,

02:09:26.110 --> 02:09:29.770
the very top, the leadership,
the two commanders in chief

02:09:29.870 --> 02:09:33.670
Vladimir Putin and Volodymyr
Zelenskyy. What are, from a military

02:09:33.730 --> 02:09:36.470
perspective, the strengths
and weaknesses of the two

02:09:37.290 --> 02:09:40.550
in terms of their role as commander in
chief of their respective militaries?

02:09:40.790 --> 02:09:44.750
- In my opinion, it appears as
though Zelenskyy has offloaded

02:09:44.790 --> 02:09:48.450
a lot of the military responsibilities
to the military, and he's not making

02:09:49.130 --> 02:09:54.590
tactical or operational level decisions.
Generally speaking, that's a good thing.

02:09:55.670 --> 02:09:59.430
I don't think that that is
happening in Russia. It's harder to

02:09:59.490 --> 02:10:03.470
say based off of how Putin
is reporting at times.

02:10:03.510 --> 02:10:07.410
It seems like he might be a little bit
more involved than Zelenskyy. But I get

02:10:07.450 --> 02:10:11.030
the impression that he's pretty
removed from the military

02:10:11.050 --> 02:10:12.330
decision-making process, which

02:10:13.330 --> 02:10:17.150
is generally what you want to see.
Historically, the more that a leader gets

02:10:17.190 --> 02:10:20.630
directly involved in that type of
stuff, the messier it becomes in either

02:10:20.710 --> 02:10:26.250
direction. In terms of the negatives,
I would say, well, they both have

02:10:26.290 --> 02:10:30.190
very different dynamics at play.
Zelenskyy is constantly appealing to an

02:10:30.230 --> 02:10:33.990
international audience, trying to
solicit support from around the world.

02:10:34.030 --> 02:10:37.630
That's not really something that
Putin has to do. So Putin much more

02:10:37.670 --> 02:10:41.330
focused on, I'd say, domestic and
then kind of presenting a strong,

02:10:42.070 --> 02:10:45.790
picture to the world that they're not on the
verge of collapse, they're not on the verge of,

02:10:46.530 --> 02:10:48.710
of falling back anytime soon.

02:10:49.270 --> 02:10:51.370
- Is Putin the same? Does he have any direct

02:10:52.490 --> 02:10:56.390
input into the military operation at
the strategic operation or tactical

02:10:56.430 --> 02:10:57.510
level, as you understand?

02:10:57.850 --> 02:11:01.790
- At strategic, I'm sure. It does not
look like from what I've seen that he

02:11:01.830 --> 02:11:05.490
has much input at the tactical or operational
level, but I'd be interested in Mike's

02:11:05.530 --> 02:11:06.990
thoughts there as well.

02:11:07.530 --> 02:11:11.330
- I think there was a period early on in 2022
when things were going very badly for the

02:11:11.370 --> 02:11:15.190
Russian military that he
was actually contacting

02:11:16.090 --> 02:11:19.830
commanders, which are still very command,
senior commanders at the level of the grouping

02:11:20.430 --> 02:11:26.490
of troops, which is more the
operational level formation above that

02:11:26.670 --> 02:11:29.790
of the combined arms army. But

02:11:30.769 --> 02:11:36.050
neither leader, in my view, really
micromanages the fight. I think

02:11:36.870 --> 02:11:40.630
Zelenskyy is getting a much fairer
and more honest picture of the

02:11:40.670 --> 02:11:46.150
situation, has a much better
assessment of Ukraine's position and

02:11:47.010 --> 02:11:50.970
how Ukraine can best align its resources,
can best advocate for resources

02:11:51.010 --> 02:11:57.410
with allies, and has more realistic goals,
more importantly, more realistic goals

02:11:58.370 --> 02:12:00.810
for the military. If you look at Putin,

02:12:01.950 --> 02:12:05.890
Russian political goals, to
me, not only seem difficult

02:12:05.930 --> 02:12:09.810
to achieve, but for a long time
I had argued that Russia has

02:12:09.870 --> 02:12:13.330
a fundamental mismatch in this
war between its political goals

02:12:14.290 --> 02:12:18.190
and the military means available.
And the fault of that lies squarely

02:12:18.210 --> 02:12:22.070
with Vladimir Putin because he started the
war, and it's the job of political leadership

02:12:22.650 --> 02:12:26.630
to see, and certainly if you can't see
it four years in or five years in,

02:12:27.890 --> 02:12:29.630
should be asking hard
questions of yourself.

02:12:29.790 --> 02:12:31.070
- Can you speak to that mismatch?

02:12:31.630 --> 02:12:33.190
- You can periodize this
war a number of ways,

02:12:33.990 --> 02:12:37.810
and I've done so in my own writing.
You can look at different operations,

02:12:37.850 --> 02:12:41.750
offenses, whatnot, or you can periodize
it one big way, which is to say

02:12:43.150 --> 02:12:44.870
that you essentially have

02:12:45.850 --> 02:12:49.630
the first 30 days, this attempt
at a coup d'état that fails, the

02:12:49.650 --> 02:12:53.310
Russian attempt to take over Ukraine,
to replace Ukraine's government

02:12:53.710 --> 02:12:57.450
with one imposed by Russia. And
this is really the contest for

02:12:58.150 --> 02:13:01.750
whether Ukraine will remain an independent
state with its own national identity.

02:13:01.830 --> 02:13:02.370
And this

02:13:03.330 --> 02:13:07.290
invasion fails, this attempt fails
decisively, and it cannot be recreated in the

02:13:07.330 --> 02:13:11.290
context of this war. And then everything else that
follows, you know, and this I'm talking about

02:13:11.330 --> 02:13:15.130
from February 24th to March 25th.
Then everything else that follows

02:13:16.418 --> 02:13:20.398
is a war, you know, that is a difficult,

02:13:20.578 --> 02:13:28.038
a tragic, prolonged conventional war defined
by attrition, defined by exhaustion.

02:13:28.838 --> 02:13:34.478
And this war will define potentially the
borders of the independent Ukrainian state.

02:13:35.938 --> 02:13:39.598
What will be the, you know, the economy, to
what extent the state will be economically

02:13:39.658 --> 02:13:43.118
sustainable, and whether or not Ukraine
will ultimately have to make any

02:13:43.158 --> 02:13:46.978
concessions in terms of
its strategic orientation,

02:13:47.078 --> 02:13:50.558
what organizations it can join or not
join, like NATO or the European Union.

02:13:51.098 --> 02:13:55.058
But it's not a war to decide whether or not Ukraine
will be independent or whether or not Ukraine will

02:13:55.098 --> 02:13:59.058
have its own national identity. That's
already clear. That was the first 30 days.

02:14:00.738 --> 02:14:05.518
And so to me, the war is in part Russia's
attempt to impose its will on Ukraine,

02:14:06.658 --> 02:14:10.278
but it's become eminently
clear for some time that

02:14:10.558 --> 02:14:14.518
Russia does not have the military
means to achieve Putin's more

02:14:14.578 --> 02:14:20.758
maximalist objectives in this conflict.
And if anything, in the past year,

02:14:21.657 --> 02:14:24.838
even if you don't agree with this
assessment for the early years of this war,

02:14:25.878 --> 02:14:29.018
in the past year it's become
eminently clear that Russia's not

02:14:29.698 --> 02:14:33.278
coming closer to its goals. In fact, its
military performance is getting worse,

02:14:33.858 --> 02:14:37.258
and that's been going on for some time. I
would say the inflection point in this war

02:14:38.138 --> 02:14:41.898
for Russia, certainly, on
the tactical level, was,

02:14:42.858 --> 02:14:48.878
back in summer, fall of twenty
twenty-five. And so you can see that

02:14:49.798 --> 02:14:53.578
Ukraine had actually narrowed
its position in negotiations

02:14:55.078 --> 02:14:59.658
fairly closely to its combat performance.
Ukraine's political demands are ceasefire

02:15:00.858 --> 02:15:04.678
along the line of control, repatriation
of lost people and stolen children.

02:15:05.478 --> 02:15:09.098
But it's not these outsized demands that
Russian political leadership is making.

02:15:09.158 --> 02:15:12.458
You see Russian demands as not being
tied to Russian military performance.

02:15:13.418 --> 02:15:17.358
They seem much more tied to aspirational
performance that Russia wish it had in this war but

02:15:17.418 --> 02:15:21.318
actually doesn't. That's why they want Ukraine
to concede the rest of Donbas, because they're

02:15:21.378 --> 02:15:25.278
not able to take it, and they're not on
a path to taking it anytime soon. So

02:15:25.358 --> 02:15:30.358
even if we assess this war for most
minimalist territorial war aims of

02:15:31.438 --> 02:15:34.758
Russia prioritizing the occupation
of the rest of the Donbas,

02:15:35.358 --> 02:15:40.398
the last remaining some twenty plus
percent, we have no way to even predict

02:15:41.638 --> 02:15:43.938
if they are able to take it in this war

02:15:44.838 --> 02:15:48.518
given how poorly the Russian military is performing
right now. And so here I am kind of making a

02:15:48.598 --> 02:15:50.858
judgment, because if you see

02:15:51.798 --> 02:15:55.618
a country prosecuting a war with a
clear mismatch between military means

02:15:55.658 --> 02:15:57.638
available and the political
goals of the war,

02:15:58.638 --> 02:16:02.638
and the leader of that country unwilling
to revise down the political aims,

02:16:04.098 --> 02:16:08.058
willing to pay a cost that
would be ruinous for Russia. I

02:16:08.118 --> 02:16:11.958
mean, this war will be seen as a strategic
failure for Russia with generational knock-on

02:16:11.998 --> 02:16:15.918
effects. Whatever Russia takes will not be
worth the cost. That's very clear at this

02:16:15.958 --> 02:16:16.198
point.

02:16:17.398 --> 02:16:20.838
It's very difficult to see how Vladimir
Putin goes down in history from this war,

02:16:21.698 --> 02:16:25.138
other than, you know,
in ignominy. And then

02:16:26.978 --> 02:16:30.658
even this many years into
it, you now clearly see that

02:16:30.718 --> 02:16:34.658
Russia is not attaining its military
objectives on any reasonable

02:16:34.758 --> 02:16:38.658
timelines. It's not revising the
negotiating position, and there's

02:16:38.698 --> 02:16:41.578
not been a dramatic shift in how Russia's
forces are fighting the war either.

02:16:41.918 --> 02:16:45.838
- Can you linger on what Vladimir
Putin's maximalist objectives might

02:16:45.878 --> 02:16:50.378
be? Which territory is included in
those maximalist objectives? Donbas?

02:16:50.998 --> 02:16:53.998
- Absolutely, Donbas. As much of

02:16:54.698 --> 02:16:58.658
Kharkiv, Zaporizhzhia, Dnipropetrovsk as they
could potentially occupy, because you don't

02:16:58.698 --> 02:17:02.618
see Russian forces advancing in Donbas. You
see them advancing on a broad twelve hundred

02:17:02.678 --> 02:17:06.618
kilometer front. Donbas may be
the priority effort, but there's

02:17:06.718 --> 02:17:10.698
ongoing offensive operations by Russian troops.
For a long time, they were taking territory

02:17:10.738 --> 02:17:13.458
where they could, and so they
were actually advancing more

02:17:14.438 --> 02:17:16.898
in Zaporizhzhia and
Dnipropetrovsk last year

02:17:17.758 --> 02:17:20.538
because it was easier to advance
there. Less urban terrain,

02:17:22.138 --> 02:17:25.978
fewer of the Ukrainian army's better troops
there since the rest were focused on

02:17:26.018 --> 02:17:27.058
defending the Donbas.

02:17:28.398 --> 02:17:32.198
And also obviously seeking national
concessions, right? Limits on the size of

02:17:32.238 --> 02:17:36.178
Ukrainian armed forces, limits on what kind
of capabilities Ukraine can have, limits

02:17:36.217 --> 02:17:39.238
on Ukraine's strategic
orientation or ability to join,

02:17:40.118 --> 02:17:43.998
uh, NATO, limits on whether
or not Western forces

02:17:44.658 --> 02:17:48.538
can ever be deployed or
based in Ukraine. Other

02:17:48.778 --> 02:17:52.438
national concessions dealing with the
status of Russian language, maybe the

02:17:52.498 --> 02:17:58.058
status of the, uh, Russian Orthodox
Church, and so on and so forth. Those are

02:17:58.138 --> 02:18:03.878
still there, you know, amongst Russian
demands negotiations, and also seeking,

02:18:04.838 --> 02:18:11.498
recognition, to what extent they can
get recognition for territory that they

02:18:11.578 --> 02:18:13.658
occupied and annexed. So

02:18:14.498 --> 02:18:17.378
the reason I say all this is, I
think it is a fair criticism.

02:18:18.518 --> 02:18:24.478
I don't know how Preston sees this matter.
I think that Ukrainian goals in this war

02:18:25.298 --> 02:18:29.278
are not only far more reasonable, but
well-constructed and at least tied to what

02:18:29.318 --> 02:18:34.378
the military can achieve. There's an
observable strategy that integrates

02:18:35.898 --> 02:18:40.258
military operations, stabilizing the
front line and liberating territory,

02:18:41.358 --> 02:18:45.558
trying to protect critical infrastructure,
air and missile defense, to,

02:18:46.618 --> 02:18:51.938
to reduce the impact of
Russia's strike campaign, and

02:18:53.018 --> 02:18:56.698
prevent the Russian leadership from thinking
that they have the ability to somehow

02:18:57.078 --> 02:19:01.858
bomb Ukraine into conceding these
demands. And the third one is

02:19:03.638 --> 02:19:09.698
exhausting the Russian effort by striking
the key components of the Russian economy:

02:19:10.998 --> 02:19:15.138
energy export, refining capacity,

02:19:16.118 --> 02:19:19.558
other targets. Now more lately
we have data centers, but

02:19:19.598 --> 02:19:23.578
essentially things that are relevant to the
functioning of the Russian economy or the

02:19:23.618 --> 02:19:25.518
Russian government's
ability to raise revenue.

02:19:26.148 --> 02:19:30.228
- So is it fair to say
that wars come to an end

02:19:31.388 --> 02:19:37.008
when both sides, their demands
match their military performance?

02:19:37.748 --> 02:19:41.648
- To put it simply, it's when both
sides see that negotiations and a

02:19:41.688 --> 02:19:45.448
negotiated settlement is a better alternative
to fighting than what they can hope to

02:19:45.488 --> 02:19:47.448
expect to gain on the battlefield.

02:19:47.868 --> 02:19:51.568
- I think that's a good way of putting
it, when military capabilities

02:19:51.628 --> 02:19:55.288
match what the political concessions
are looking like, and I think that's

02:19:55.328 --> 02:19:59.248
exactly why a lot of folks are not
very optimistic about an end to

02:19:59.288 --> 02:20:02.988
this war anytime soon, 'cause as
Mike mentioned, the demands from

02:20:03.048 --> 02:20:06.948
Russia are largely unchanged from the
beginning of the war. Just this past week,

02:20:07.428 --> 02:20:11.328
Putin talked about how Zelenskyy
did not have the authority to talk

02:20:11.408 --> 02:20:15.188
about the future of Zaporizhzhia and
Kherson and Donetsk and Luhansk.

02:20:15.408 --> 02:20:19.268
He said those are historic Russian lands.
So it's not even up to Zelenskyy to

02:20:19.308 --> 02:20:20.928
talk about territorial concessions.

02:20:21.948 --> 02:20:25.708
That's where Russia's mindset is,
or Putin specifically, where his

02:20:25.768 --> 02:20:29.588
mindset is in the war, is the territory
that they haven't taken yet isn't

02:20:29.628 --> 02:20:35.408
even up for discussion. That's a foregone
conclusion that they will own that. And as

02:20:35.468 --> 02:20:39.368
Mike mentioned, they are so far from taking that
territory right now. I mean, part of that they would

02:20:39.388 --> 02:20:43.048
have to cross the Dnieper
River. There's no one in the

02:20:43.088 --> 02:20:46.308
world that looks at this right now and
thinks that Russia is anywhere near

02:20:46.808 --> 02:20:51.348
crossing a water feature to, to take
Kherson City and the rest of that oblast-

02:20:51.348 --> 02:20:56.128
... let alone the little portions
of terrain in Luhansk and Donetsk.

02:20:56.768 --> 02:21:00.548
- So given that if there's a
mismatch between demands and,

02:21:00.828 --> 02:21:05.288
and military performance, there's other
factors, forces that come into play,

02:21:06.308 --> 02:21:09.548
like the state of the economy, the
will to fight amongst the people,

02:21:10.848 --> 02:21:14.828
like very simply manpower, as
you've spoken about. Like, in

02:21:14.868 --> 02:21:18.848
order to fight a war, you need humans
to be doing that fighting. So how

02:21:18.868 --> 02:21:22.708
does that come into play? How long can Ukraine
last in this fight? How long can Russia

02:21:22.748 --> 02:21:23.468
last in this fight?

02:21:24.048 --> 02:21:27.888
- Admittedly, the economy, I thought
that the sanctions would have

02:21:27.928 --> 02:21:31.788
done a lot more damage. At this point,
I think a lot of people did, and it

02:21:31.828 --> 02:21:35.527
just hasn't. Russia has found a lot
of ways to bypass that. I thought the

02:21:35.568 --> 02:21:39.348
Russian people would have been frustrated
long ago with the change in their

02:21:39.388 --> 02:21:43.328
status of living. That didn't
materialize. So there's no

02:21:43.368 --> 02:21:47.248
doubt that the Russian economy is feeling
some pressure. To the extent that that

02:21:47.328 --> 02:21:51.148
will impact the end of the war or could drive
the end of the war, it's a bit of a black

02:21:51.168 --> 02:21:54.908
box from my perspective.
The other issue that

02:21:54.948 --> 02:21:58.828
Russia is playing with right now
is manpower. Look, overall, Russia

02:21:58.868 --> 02:22:02.728
has the manpower advantage, and it's not close. They
have more people than Ukraine, and it's not close.

02:22:03.388 --> 02:22:07.128
Ukraine has done a good job
of while they are mobilizing

02:22:07.548 --> 02:22:11.168
and conscripting people into the
force, they are doing what they can

02:22:11.508 --> 02:22:16.168
to minimize soldiers at the
front in dangerous positions.

02:22:16.228 --> 02:22:20.288
They're handing a lot of this
off to robots when possible.

02:22:21.608 --> 02:22:25.468
Russia is continuing to push men
forward, trying to take terrain,

02:22:25.648 --> 02:22:29.588
not with robots and not with drones, but
with soldiers, and that's costing a lot

02:22:29.608 --> 02:22:33.468
of lives. Uh, Russia
in certain regions has

02:22:33.788 --> 02:22:37.468
started to really struggle in filling
their recruitment demands, and we're

02:22:37.528 --> 02:22:41.488
seeing what... In fact, I think Mike wrote
a piece about this recently, but there's,

02:22:41.588 --> 02:22:45.488
kind of a shadow mobilization, if you
will, inside of Russia, where they're

02:22:45.548 --> 02:22:48.908
starting to coerce more and more
people into joining the military.

02:22:49.648 --> 02:22:53.548
Again, they have far more people,
but what does it look like when you

02:22:53.608 --> 02:22:57.408
start having to pull in more people that don't
wanna fight? Ukraine has already had to deal with

02:22:57.448 --> 02:22:57.648
that.

02:22:58.648 --> 02:23:02.288
They've already had to go through a number of
people, a number of different groups within

02:23:02.328 --> 02:23:06.188
society that maybe didn't volunteer right out
the gate, or I just read an article earlier

02:23:06.228 --> 02:23:09.968
today about a soldier who now is doing
an incredible job in the Ukrainian

02:23:10.008 --> 02:23:13.968
military. They grabbed him at the border. He was
trying to leave the country, and now he's kind of

02:23:14.008 --> 02:23:19.028
found a purpose- ... as a drone mechanic.
Ukraine has done a lot of that.

02:23:20.008 --> 02:23:23.488
Russia is starting to get into
that, and that's a big unknown,

02:23:23.928 --> 02:23:27.528
is how will the country react when
they start having to tap into other

02:23:27.628 --> 02:23:31.608
portions of the population? I'm
not overly optimistic because

02:23:31.648 --> 02:23:35.608
we've seen a number of things at this point that
I thought would've pushed the Russian population

02:23:35.988 --> 02:23:39.748
over the edge. The casualties,
for one, shocking.

02:23:39.988 --> 02:23:43.708
I, at about the three-month mark, I thought
that that would be enough for the people in

02:23:43.748 --> 02:23:47.488
Moscow to rise up and say, "We're not
gonna do this," and my God, here we are,

02:23:47.768 --> 02:23:51.568
30-plus thousand casualties a month, and
they're just... There's no end in sight.

02:23:52.368 --> 02:23:56.288
So I'm not optimistic that should
there be a mobilization or a push to

02:23:56.348 --> 02:23:59.548
pull in more people that have not been
fighting, that it'll change anything

02:23:59.648 --> 02:24:03.588
drastically inside of Russia,
but that dynamic is changing.

02:24:04.368 --> 02:24:09.808
- I think from a resource perspective, both
of these are fairly large countries,

02:24:10.848 --> 02:24:17.528
and both of them have considerable
external support. Ukraine

02:24:18.668 --> 02:24:22.648
has demonstrated immense resolve
and will to fight, but clear

02:24:22.668 --> 02:24:26.548
without Western support, Western
capital, Western military capabilities,

02:24:26.948 --> 02:24:30.008
okay, would not have been able to
sustain the war for this long.

02:24:31.248 --> 02:24:34.528
Russia too has benefited significantly

02:24:35.368 --> 02:24:40.308
from China, Chinese economy, access to
machine tools, components to China,

02:24:41.588 --> 02:24:43.308
to a lesser extent, North Korea,

02:24:44.228 --> 02:24:48.188
and much lesser extent, Iran, but North Korea
ships so much-- You know, at one point in the war,

02:24:48.268 --> 02:24:52.028
North Korea was providing 40% of Russian
artillery ammunition in this war,

02:24:53.108 --> 02:24:57.088
and shipping artillery, and
actually North Korean troops, if

02:24:57.128 --> 02:25:03.248
you remember, fought on the Russian side
in Kursk during that battle for Kursk. So

02:25:05.428 --> 02:25:07.868
both sides benefit considerably
from external support that...

02:25:10.088 --> 02:25:13.868
And this is what enables Russia to
continue the war. I think from an

02:25:13.928 --> 02:25:17.228
economic point of view, it's difficult
to predict. It's clear Russia has the

02:25:17.268 --> 02:25:19.868
resources to continue to fight into 2027,

02:25:20.868 --> 02:25:24.708
but key economic elites in Russia
early this year in the spring

02:25:25.668 --> 02:25:28.148
began to publicly state that

02:25:29.312 --> 02:25:32.892
this war and this level of government spending
is not sustainable. Russia's spending forty-four

02:25:32.952 --> 02:25:35.572
percent of the government
budget on the war,

02:25:36.492 --> 02:25:40.192
and maybe somewhere around nine
to ten percent of GDP. Uh,

02:25:41.072 --> 02:25:44.952
the Russian economy has structural
issues, and so much of the

02:25:44.992 --> 02:25:48.692
money is going towards the military effort and
production of military goods, which are not

02:25:49.032 --> 02:25:52.932
economically valuable that the economy
is slowly consuming itself. So

02:25:52.972 --> 02:25:57.132
they have issues. For
Ukraine, the main challenge

02:25:58.132 --> 02:26:02.072
is that the war continues to
damage the Ukraine economy and

02:26:02.232 --> 02:26:05.852
also drive people from the country, since a
big part of the Russian military campaign

02:26:06.452 --> 02:26:13.252
is to try to hurt the, economic
viability of Ukraine and

02:26:13.492 --> 02:26:17.272
sustainability of life in Ukrainian
cities. And so even though

02:26:17.332 --> 02:26:20.692
Ukraine's doing well on the
battlefield, continuing the war

02:26:21.232 --> 02:26:25.072
naturally comes a cost. I don't know any country
that hosts a conventional war in its territory

02:26:25.152 --> 02:26:27.152
that was the better off
for it. It's very clear.

02:26:28.652 --> 02:26:32.412
And Ukraine's main challenge is it
depends so heavily on financial

02:26:32.492 --> 02:26:35.972
support, military support, intelligence
support from the West as well,

02:26:37.552 --> 02:26:41.232
that these are things that have to constantly
be renewed. And increasingly, you also see that

02:26:41.272 --> 02:26:44.992
Ukraine is subject to some extent
to the mercurial nature or

02:26:45.012 --> 02:26:49.852
capricious nature of policy of some
countries like the United States

02:26:50.772 --> 02:26:54.332
that-- And we should
speak frankly about it,

02:26:54.732 --> 02:26:58.812
right? There's genuine concern,
I think, in Ukraine that

02:27:00.432 --> 02:27:04.052
folks here want to do
business with Russia,

02:27:04.932 --> 02:27:10.592
and they want to do it no matter the
implications for the allies and no

02:27:10.652 --> 02:27:13.252
matter the implications
for Ukraine and for this

02:27:14.072 --> 02:27:17.272
critical conflict that's going to
shape future security in Europe.

02:27:18.332 --> 02:27:22.212
- How long can Ukraine last
without US support? We

02:27:22.252 --> 02:27:23.772
should mention that there's no direct

02:27:25.352 --> 02:27:29.092
financial aid, as I understand, from
the United States, but there is,

02:27:29.292 --> 02:27:34.272
like you mentioned, intelligence and
otherwise actual ammunition and weapons.

02:27:35.152 --> 02:27:38.932
- The reality is the United States still
provides considerable military equipment,

02:27:39.111 --> 02:27:42.872
ammunition, other things Ukraine needs.
Just Europeans are paying for it, okay?

02:27:43.052 --> 02:27:44.312
The United States is also

02:27:45.332 --> 02:27:49.312
offering quite a bit, again, through foreign
military sales. Europeans are paying for

02:27:49.372 --> 02:27:53.212
that. There's a fair amount of
intelligence support that I can't

02:27:53.332 --> 02:27:59.072
speak to, but you should consider it's
probably still important to the effort. So

02:28:00.672 --> 02:28:03.652
Ukraine may not be dependent nearly
on the United States as it was

02:28:04.772 --> 02:28:08.512
earlier in the war, where we were providing
not only a lot of the funding, but

02:28:08.572 --> 02:28:10.952
artillery ammunition,
equipment, what have you.

02:28:11.752 --> 02:28:15.572
But in key areas, it does still depend on the
United States. We're dependent on the United

02:28:15.652 --> 02:28:19.152
States certainly not to
try to hamper Ukraine's

02:28:19.412 --> 02:28:22.152
military effort, where the US still
has considerable power and influence.

02:28:22.772 --> 02:28:26.012
- If you can evaluate, what has Trump
done as our commander-in-chief

02:28:26.912 --> 02:28:29.612
in the context of helping bring
this war to a resolution?

02:28:30.052 --> 02:28:33.832
- To be blunt, I don't know that there's a lot
that's been, from my opinion, that's been good

02:28:34.672 --> 02:28:38.692
so far. I think we have ceded our position

02:28:39.632 --> 02:28:43.352
in the world was looking to us, and
I think still will in a lot of ways.

02:28:43.792 --> 02:28:47.512
Should we take that mantle back up? I think
the world is looking to us to take lead,

02:28:47.832 --> 02:28:51.772
to take point. That doesn't necessarily mean that
we're shipping the most equipment or we're spending

02:28:51.792 --> 02:28:55.592
the most money, but kind of leading the process
and what this will look like going forward.

02:28:55.672 --> 02:28:58.952
And I think we stepped away from
that early in the administration.

02:28:59.692 --> 02:29:03.252
I get the impression that maybe the
president thought this would be easier to

02:29:03.292 --> 02:29:06.212
resolve than it has been,
maybe lost some focus.

02:29:07.392 --> 02:29:11.372
We've lost leverage over Ukraine as well,
which is kind of a cold thing to say, but

02:29:11.672 --> 02:29:13.892
when we were providing weapons to Ukraine,

02:29:14.892 --> 02:29:18.292
we had the ability to kind of,
you know, navigate the peace

02:29:18.332 --> 02:29:20.732
process with more power, more leverage.

02:29:21.552 --> 02:29:25.312
We did away with that. That
hurt Ukraine, but it also

02:29:25.992 --> 02:29:29.492
gave us less ability to kind of dictate
the direction for things to go.

02:29:30.412 --> 02:29:33.972
We've not seen the sanctions layered on
Russia that I think could have been.

02:29:34.512 --> 02:29:36.352
And unfortunately, I think there's been

02:29:37.592 --> 02:29:41.392
just some doubt thrown
on the structure of the

02:29:41.432 --> 02:29:45.392
NATO alliance. Will the alliance hold?
And I think there's probably just

02:29:45.431 --> 02:29:49.212
enough there for Putin to
irrationally but possibly still

02:29:49.251 --> 02:29:52.692
think that it would be worthwhile to poke
and prod to see if there's an opening there.

02:29:52.812 --> 02:29:52.932
So

02:29:54.492 --> 02:29:58.332
personally, I don't think there's been a lot
of good. I think we've kicked the can down the

02:29:58.372 --> 02:30:03.492
road. I think we've seen-- We have--
we've opened the door for Europe to take

02:30:03.612 --> 02:30:07.352
lead here. In some ways, they've
started to. There's still a lot to be

02:30:07.392 --> 02:30:10.932
done. But I'm not overly
optimistic. I think we've,

02:30:11.632 --> 02:30:15.012
we've made some mistakes
here in ways that not just

02:30:16.212 --> 02:30:19.272
difficult for Ukraine, but I
think for the United States.

02:30:19.912 --> 02:30:23.492
- The engagement with it has
been pretty fitful, Lex.

02:30:24.052 --> 02:30:27.612
they seem to be coming back to it every
three, four months. We're heavily

02:30:27.652 --> 02:30:35.692
distracted by the Iran war. And it
almost seems that Trump administration

02:30:36.952 --> 02:30:40.732
had first settled on trying to coerce
Ukraine, seeing Ukraine as the problem,

02:30:41.492 --> 02:30:45.452
then began to recognize that Russia was the problem,
but doing nothing to compel Russia to change its

02:30:45.512 --> 02:30:46.532
negotiating position.

02:30:48.112 --> 02:30:51.652
The meeting in Anchorage, Alaska seemed
to begin to bring Russia out of the

02:30:51.732 --> 02:30:55.332
isolation that we and other
Western countries had agreed on,

02:30:55.952 --> 02:30:59.872
and we can see the knock-on
effects of that. And then

02:31:00.792 --> 02:31:05.512
more recently, we had gone back to
trying to compel Ukraine once again.

02:31:06.752 --> 02:31:13.992
Um, and I just don't think that the
way we've engaged this process, uh,

02:31:15.272 --> 02:31:19.232
is the right approach. I agree with Preston.
The only thing I might say, 'cause I

02:31:19.252 --> 02:31:23.852
think there needs to be a balanced view of
this, the only positive thing I would say

02:31:24.852 --> 02:31:28.092
is that even if it was probably
for the wrong reasons,

02:31:29.032 --> 02:31:32.172
prioritizing attaining an end to the war

02:31:33.044 --> 02:31:36.464
and pushing all the
parties involved towards

02:31:36.944 --> 02:31:40.644
making clear what their requirements
are to end the war and some kind of

02:31:40.704 --> 02:31:45.624
process and essentially an alignment
of positions on that topic

02:31:46.484 --> 02:31:50.324
was helpful. Because the previous
administration had no plan. As

02:31:50.364 --> 02:31:52.184
long as it takes is a slogan, not a plan.

02:31:52.584 --> 02:31:56.464
If anyone knew what the Biden administration's
plan was to either win the war or end the war,

02:31:57.684 --> 02:32:03.244
I don't know. And our European allies,
well, they had the opportunity for quite

02:32:03.284 --> 02:32:07.124
a few years to come up with some kind of
plan or negotiating process, and they did

02:32:07.184 --> 02:32:11.064
not. Their consensus overall
was to support Ukraine, that

02:32:11.104 --> 02:32:15.324
was the common denominator,
and to sustain the war.

02:32:16.264 --> 02:32:20.184
But given that this is a war principally
fought in Europe, they too did

02:32:20.244 --> 02:32:24.004
not take lead on a peace plan
for all those years until the

02:32:24.064 --> 02:32:28.944
Trump administration showed up. And
I'm using this as an opportunity,

02:32:30.024 --> 02:32:33.944
I think for some constructive and hopefully
polite criticism, is that of course,

02:32:34.124 --> 02:32:37.744
ideally, you should have an alignment
of positions between the United States

02:32:38.424 --> 02:32:43.504
and European allies in managing
European security. Okay? However,

02:32:44.384 --> 02:32:48.284
it is not as though the Trump administration
came into office and European allies

02:32:48.344 --> 02:32:52.144
had a plan or vision, and the
Trump administration had a

02:32:52.204 --> 02:32:55.824
worse one or threw that
plan and vision out. So

02:32:57.524 --> 02:33:01.484
then European allies were forced
to play catch up and maintain

02:33:01.584 --> 02:33:04.644
relevance and whatnot.
Now, all that being said,

02:33:05.664 --> 02:33:09.344
I think the Trump administration's
approach overall has

02:33:09.464 --> 02:33:13.384
not been a good one. They haven't
done a great job consulting

02:33:13.604 --> 02:33:16.504
the key allies involved
in this. They've been

02:33:17.064 --> 02:33:20.724
capricious and in many ways
unpredictable in their

02:33:20.764 --> 02:33:25.064
behavior both towards allies
and towards Ukraine. Um,

02:33:25.904 --> 02:33:29.784
it's clear that parts of the administration wants
to normalize relations and do business with

02:33:29.884 --> 02:33:33.604
Russia and sees the whole war as kind of an
impediment to that. So their motivations,

02:33:33.964 --> 02:33:36.904
I think objectively to
some extent suspect.

02:33:38.004 --> 02:33:41.764
And the way they've engaged
with it has been kind of from a

02:33:41.844 --> 02:33:43.803
let's make a deal without looking at

02:33:44.604 --> 02:33:48.524
does this deal make sense, and is this
going to be a lasting agreement, or

02:33:48.544 --> 02:33:52.244
is this just going to be a piece of paper
with the word ceasefire on it that won't,

02:33:52.504 --> 02:33:55.984
frankly will not be worth the paper it's
on. So there are real issues you can

02:33:56.024 --> 02:33:58.564
objectively take with the way
negotiation's been handled.

02:33:58.744 --> 02:34:02.504
- One thing I would add there as well is I think it's
important to look at all of this. We're Americans.

02:34:02.624 --> 02:34:06.384
It's important to look at this through the American
lens, and it's not just a matter of were these

02:34:06.424 --> 02:34:10.224
decisions good or bad for Ukraine, because at the end of
the day, we should be thinking about are they good or bad

02:34:10.264 --> 02:34:14.144
for the United States and our interests. Ukraine
has raised their hand a couple times and said,

02:34:14.164 --> 02:34:17.404
"How can we help?" Especially when it came to
the war in Iran and some of these interceptors.

02:34:18.004 --> 02:34:21.824
There is so much opportunity
there in terms of collaboration,

02:34:22.124 --> 02:34:26.004
joint production, standing up
facilities. We don't have to give money

02:34:26.044 --> 02:34:29.884
to Ukraine. We don't have to give equipment to
Ukraine. But there is a mutually beneficial

02:34:29.924 --> 02:34:33.824
relationship to be had there where we
can help them produce, and maybe we get

02:34:33.884 --> 02:34:37.604
some rights for development. Because
we need a lot of that stuff too,

02:34:38.024 --> 02:34:39.064
and I just feel like, again-

02:34:39.064 --> 02:34:41.044
... I think the best way to put it
is we've left a lot on the table.

02:34:41.604 --> 02:34:45.484
- And certainly not used our leverage,
and have been very, very resistant to

02:34:45.544 --> 02:34:46.944
using any of it to compel Russia.

02:34:47.884 --> 02:34:51.704
The approach has been unhelpful
and hasn't really used the

02:34:51.744 --> 02:34:55.384
power in the world we have. And I think part of
the problem was how we started, because when,

02:34:55.684 --> 02:34:59.364
when this began, as an effort
on Trump administration,

02:35:00.464 --> 02:35:04.344
for some reason they decided to immediately
maneuver the United States out of the leading

02:35:04.384 --> 02:35:08.224
role of a coalition supporting Ukraine, trying
to achieve a favorable outcome in this war,

02:35:08.924 --> 02:35:12.784
to a neutral party who will now referee
and arbitrate the outcome and try to

02:35:12.844 --> 02:35:16.524
make a deal pretending as though the
previous history of how this war

02:35:16.564 --> 02:35:18.604
began, our whole role in
it didn't happen. Like,

02:35:19.664 --> 02:35:23.164
and it was a very strange approach
to take because it gave up our,

02:35:24.204 --> 02:35:27.404
influence and leverage over
both our European allies,

02:35:28.324 --> 02:35:31.684
who didn't trust our intentions and could see that
we basically were no longer on the same team,

02:35:32.844 --> 02:35:34.184
and our

02:35:35.784 --> 02:35:39.444
partner in Ukraine, who immediately began to
think that we were about to sell them out

02:35:39.484 --> 02:35:45.744
because we turned around and abandoned
our role, which was critical to

02:35:45.804 --> 02:35:50.544
supporting them, into one that now placed
them on the equal footing with Russia.

02:35:50.984 --> 02:35:54.864
- Which to be clear, Russia
even today laughs at the idea

02:35:54.903 --> 02:35:58.744
that we are trying to be this neutral party.
They do not view us that way. They view us

02:35:58.824 --> 02:36:02.464
as firmly in Ukraine's camp.
Understandably so. Um,

02:36:02.844 --> 02:36:06.824
so these moves to kind of separate, I
understand on paper it may have been,

02:36:07.124 --> 02:36:10.924
well, now we're neutral, and we're not
directly aligned with one side or the other

02:36:11.084 --> 02:36:13.304
that didn't go over in
Russia. Didn't carry.

02:36:14.044 --> 02:36:17.284
- At this late hour, this is the
most challenging question.

02:36:18.044 --> 02:36:19.664
What are the possible paths to peace

02:36:20.584 --> 02:36:24.464
in the war in Ukraine? Can you speak
to the different trajectories?

02:36:24.644 --> 02:36:25.764
What are the things you hope to see?

02:36:26.764 --> 02:36:29.784
- I don't see a willingness on
the Russian side right now to

02:36:30.424 --> 02:36:32.524
negotiate. There are
negotiations taking place,

02:36:33.544 --> 02:36:35.004
and there's another push, another effort.

02:36:36.244 --> 02:36:39.924
Maybe some sort of partial compartmentalized
ceasefire will be achieved. For

02:36:39.944 --> 02:36:46.444
example, a ceasefire on energy strikes
between the two sides, or a ceasefire halting

02:36:47.244 --> 02:36:51.684
strikes against shipping in the Black
Sea, which have escalated this year.

02:36:52.744 --> 02:36:57.384
I think that's possible. I think
the problem we have is that

02:36:58.464 --> 02:37:00.184
the Russian position remains

02:37:01.084 --> 02:37:04.764
that fighting's their preferred
alternative to negotiation

02:37:05.484 --> 02:37:10.684
and to a revision of their
current demands. And I think from

02:37:11.544 --> 02:37:15.504
Putin's perspective, he is likely
to wait to see the outcome

02:37:15.544 --> 02:37:18.564
of two things: the strike campaign
over the course of the winter,

02:37:19.644 --> 02:37:23.544
right, and to see if
that places Ukraine in

02:37:23.584 --> 02:37:26.884
a weaker bargaining position. Because
Ukraine right now has considerable leverage.

02:37:26.944 --> 02:37:30.664
Ukraine's done much better this year at the
front line than Russia, and Russia does

02:37:30.844 --> 02:37:35.432
not have great prospects for advancing
along pretty much any part of the front.

02:37:35.752 --> 02:37:39.672
- Can you explain why that is, and why you've
already mentioned a couple of times that winter

02:37:39.692 --> 02:37:40.992
is a very difficult time for Ukraine?

02:37:41.232 --> 02:37:44.732
- The winter is the hardest not for troops at the
front line, it's the hardest for the civilian

02:37:44.812 --> 02:37:47.012
population. But a country is not

02:37:47.832 --> 02:37:51.612
just the map or the front. It is the economy, it is
the cities, the people. That's actually what you're

02:37:51.632 --> 02:37:57.372
fighting for, right? And so that's who
the winter affects the most. The second

02:37:57.392 --> 02:38:01.152
thing Putin may be waiting for is the outcome of
certain elections in Europe in the spring, like

02:38:01.192 --> 02:38:04.252
France's election, to see if the

02:38:04.712 --> 02:38:07.572
overall situation could be
more favorable to Russia.

02:38:08.752 --> 02:38:12.212
Right now, I don't see any
serious effort other than Ukraine

02:38:12.332 --> 02:38:18.092
strikes to compel Russia, right? Or to
meaningfully exhaust it economically.

02:38:19.392 --> 02:38:23.732
And so far they have been impactful,
but at the end of the day,

02:38:25.152 --> 02:38:29.052
it's clear that Russia still has the resources and the
will to continue the war into twenty twenty-seven,

02:38:29.132 --> 02:38:31.092
and so does Ukraine. And

02:38:31.992 --> 02:38:35.972
Europeans, if they have to, are likely to
come up with more funding for Ukraine next

02:38:36.052 --> 02:38:39.712
year. I think so. And we
have not cut off military

02:38:39.812 --> 02:38:43.332
support, the things we are sending and
providing that Ukraine depends on.

02:38:45.212 --> 02:38:48.952
We have made threats recently,
unfortunately, to suspend it, but we haven't

02:38:49.672 --> 02:38:55.012
really cut these things off. So
even though negotiations continue,

02:38:56.152 --> 02:38:57.232
as they did last year,

02:38:58.172 --> 02:39:01.972
that's why I have a bit of a pessimistic
view, and I could be wrong. I hope I'm

02:39:01.992 --> 02:39:05.732
wrong. Those of us who have been working on this
for a long time would like to see it end in the

02:39:05.772 --> 02:39:09.512
right way, would like to see an acceptable
outcome, if not a favorable one to Ukraine.

02:39:10.912 --> 02:39:13.132
And also, a deal would
like to end it in a way

02:39:14.012 --> 02:39:16.972
that it doesn't just lead to
a third continuation war.

02:39:18.052 --> 02:39:21.992
This is the pessimistic prospect I
want to raise. A lot of folks think of

02:39:22.052 --> 02:39:25.832
this as, "Let's just get to a deal.
Let's get to a ceasefire. Why

02:39:25.872 --> 02:39:27.652
won't Ukraine, why won't
Russia just agree?"

02:39:29.472 --> 02:39:33.372
Because ceasefires can often just
become rearmament periods to renew

02:39:33.392 --> 02:39:35.672
hostilities. Ukraine is very vulnerable

02:39:37.332 --> 02:39:41.272
some months after a ceasefire. Eventually, it'll
have to demobilize all the men at the front line

02:39:41.292 --> 02:39:43.332
who want to go back to their
lives, to their families.

02:39:45.312 --> 02:39:49.972
Russia's force is largely contracted.
Russia may not demobilize its force.

02:39:51.092 --> 02:39:54.712
And then Ukraine could be very
vulnerable after a ceasefire

02:39:55.332 --> 02:39:59.132
is signed six to eight months into it if
there are no genuine security guarantees from

02:39:59.172 --> 02:40:00.352
United States or anyone else.

02:40:01.512 --> 02:40:05.292
All they got was a piece of paper saying,
"Ceasefire." Russia had the opportunity to

02:40:05.332 --> 02:40:09.072
rearm, and the war is renewed.
Except this time, Russia's in a

02:40:09.132 --> 02:40:12.532
much more advantaged position
given how things developed

02:40:13.312 --> 02:40:17.052
after the ceasefire. And for
those not familiar with this

02:40:17.092 --> 02:40:19.312
war, it starts in twenty fourteen,

02:40:20.212 --> 02:40:24.152
and there were two failed ceasefires that never
led to a political settlement, the first Minsk and

02:40:24.172 --> 02:40:26.792
the second Minsk agreement.
And so naturally,

02:40:27.772 --> 02:40:31.652
those of us who are familiar with this
history both want to see a ceasefire but

02:40:31.692 --> 02:40:35.512
also wary that a ceasefire is just a piece of
paper with those words on it but doesn't lead to

02:40:35.572 --> 02:40:39.472
a lasting peace. And this is my
concern that we will potentially

02:40:39.532 --> 02:40:43.252
see a third iteration of this war, that how
this war ends takes us to the next one.

02:40:43.632 --> 02:40:46.552
And there are plenty of conflicts like
this, wars between India and Pakistan,

02:40:47.232 --> 02:40:48.372
Arab-Israeli conflict,

02:40:49.752 --> 02:40:53.232
uh, Armenia-Azerbaijan, where there
are multiple wars in that series.

02:40:54.592 --> 02:40:58.312
- You're implying there's a low probability
of success in the near term about the

02:40:58.372 --> 02:41:02.052
negotiation efforts. If it
turns out that they are

02:41:02.132 --> 02:41:06.012
successful, what does that look
like? What do you think? What is the

02:41:06.092 --> 02:41:10.772
inkling of light as we search for some
optimism that they are successful?

02:41:11.292 --> 02:41:14.232
- They get at least a partial
ceasefire in place,

02:41:14.872 --> 02:41:18.372
compartmentalizing this
war, ideally beginning with

02:41:18.752 --> 02:41:20.832
strikes against critical
infrastructure by both sides,

02:41:22.172 --> 02:41:24.392
maybe also introduction
strikes in the Black Sea.

02:41:26.092 --> 02:41:28.672
Okay. And then maybe we start to see,

02:41:29.572 --> 02:41:35.052
an agreement on a ceasefire
for some period of days to

02:41:36.012 --> 02:41:39.752
then show both sides' commitment to the fact that
they want to engage in this political process in

02:41:39.792 --> 02:41:45.012
earnest. You usually have to start
somewhere, and having a ceasefire lasting

02:41:46.012 --> 02:41:50.212
some period of days, a week, two
weeks, a month, is that beginning.

02:41:51.291 --> 02:41:54.671
Right now, I don't see Russia there
willing to agree to something like this.

02:41:55.171 --> 02:41:58.932
Because if they were, there's nothing that's been stopping
them. That proposal's been on the table for a long

02:41:59.012 --> 02:42:02.592
time now. Right? They're not interested. Their
position is they don't want to agree to a

02:42:02.632 --> 02:42:03.192
ceasefire.

02:42:04.012 --> 02:42:07.912
They wish to settle all these complex,
difficult issues, and only then will

02:42:07.932 --> 02:42:13.552
they offer a ceasefire. But the thrust
of that position is they don't see

02:42:14.772 --> 02:42:18.752
a ceasefire and negotiations right now as a
better alternative to fighting at the end of the

02:42:18.792 --> 02:42:22.772
day. And that's because Putin's
perception of the situation, from

02:42:22.812 --> 02:42:26.712
my point of view, is different
from ours, and also I

02:42:26.752 --> 02:42:30.512
think differs to some extent from objective
reality, or at least I suspect that

02:42:30.552 --> 02:42:32.572
that's one of the inputs
into his calculus.

02:42:33.032 --> 02:42:37.012
- If we look at longer
term future, what does

02:42:37.072 --> 02:42:41.612
Ukraine look like after Zelenskyy? What
does Russia look like after Putin?

02:42:42.112 --> 02:42:44.872
- This war will leave lasting
damage on Ukraine and on Russia.

02:42:46.472 --> 02:42:50.352
That's fundamentally it's
a tragic war. And, um,

02:42:51.232 --> 02:42:56.072
even though I think we will judge Ukraine
ultimately as having been successful

02:42:57.172 --> 02:43:01.992
in defending its independence,
the cost will be great. And

02:43:03.112 --> 02:43:08.012
I do imagine that better future
because I do think that ultimately,

02:43:09.252 --> 02:43:13.032
this will pass. It's far from the
first destructive European war.

02:43:13.812 --> 02:43:16.532
And unfortunately, in
the history of Ukraine

02:43:17.792 --> 02:43:21.472
and Russia, it's far from the first
destructive war that's been visited

02:43:21.752 --> 02:43:23.752
on these countries and on
the people who live in them.

02:43:25.072 --> 02:43:28.932
So in that regard, it's
doubly tragic, and so many

02:43:28.972 --> 02:43:32.012
battles have been fought
before in these same areas.

02:43:32.972 --> 02:43:36.752
It's a tragic history to
see that in the 2020s

02:43:37.372 --> 02:43:40.752
we still have a large scale
conventional war taking place

02:43:41.592 --> 02:43:45.492
here. Um, I think one of
the outstanding questions,

02:43:46.352 --> 02:43:49.732
and to me this is kind of an implied question
in what you're asking about the future.

02:43:51.572 --> 02:43:52.952
Is Ukraine going to be viable

02:43:54.112 --> 02:43:57.312
as a state and as a nation
after this war? I think so.

02:43:58.012 --> 02:44:01.692
Obviously, it depends somewhat on how the
war ends, but more importantly, the kind of

02:44:01.732 --> 02:44:05.672
support Ukraine can hope to have
from European countries. But if the

02:44:05.712 --> 02:44:12.652
rest of the nations in Europe, or
at least the key ones, see Ukraine

02:44:13.572 --> 02:44:17.212
as European and as essential to European
security, and as a member of that community,

02:44:17.812 --> 02:44:21.552
they will see to it, right? They will
work with Ukraine to ensure that it's

02:44:21.592 --> 02:44:24.032
successful. It will have to
depend on them for some time.

02:44:25.532 --> 02:44:29.472
The question about Russia is a challenging
one because Russia is not a problem

02:44:29.532 --> 02:44:35.472
of one person, right? Russia's a large
country with a lot of people in it.

02:44:36.552 --> 02:44:40.432
people matter because they make decisions.
Vladimir Putin cares far more about Ukraine

02:44:40.452 --> 02:44:44.352
than other Russian elites. In fact, many of them I
think are mystified by this war that he's launched.

02:44:45.352 --> 02:44:51.052
But nonetheless, some
percentage of the population

02:44:52.032 --> 02:44:55.972
supports it, acquiesce to it, and hundreds
upon hundreds of thousands of Russian men

02:44:56.052 --> 02:44:59.972
sign contracts to fight in it. This is the
first large scale conventional war that Russia

02:45:00.012 --> 02:45:03.972
has fought where very few of the men
were mobilized and many essentially

02:45:03.992 --> 02:45:07.312
took money and contracts to fight in
them. So it's not just Putin's war.

02:45:08.092 --> 02:45:11.332
The question we are in some
respects dealing with is one

02:45:12.512 --> 02:45:14.632
of Russia's failed imperial transition,

02:45:15.672 --> 02:45:19.532
right? And the fact that Russia has not
become a post-imperial nation state in

02:45:19.572 --> 02:45:24.892
Europe. And I wanna make a broader
point here that to me, this is

02:45:24.932 --> 02:45:28.892
in some respects a war of, I
hate to frame it this way,

02:45:28.972 --> 02:45:35.792
but I think it's useful, Soviet
succession because it's resultant of that

02:45:37.092 --> 02:45:40.832
history that goes back to the
dissolution of the Soviet Union, which

02:45:40.852 --> 02:45:44.572
historians that I know argue was
both an event, we remember it as

02:45:44.652 --> 02:45:48.052
happening and the dates it
happened, but also a process.

02:45:48.772 --> 02:45:52.752
And that process is really one of
imperial collapse, not just of

02:45:52.792 --> 02:45:56.672
the Soviet Union, but
the Russian Empire that

02:45:57.872 --> 02:46:02.572
lay under it as a foundation. And this
is fundamentally an imperialistic war

02:46:03.652 --> 02:46:07.232
by a state and by an
elite, by a leader that

02:46:09.572 --> 02:46:13.952
couldn't see another way of being
and another vision for Russia.

02:46:16.992 --> 02:46:20.792
And so one of the challenges
we have is this failed

02:46:20.872 --> 02:46:24.312
transition from Russia. It's not just
that Russia doesn't become democratic,

02:46:24.892 --> 02:46:30.252
it's that whatever the political system
may be, that the state has not stopped

02:46:30.312 --> 02:46:34.172
being a revisionist, revanchist,
imperial state in Europe.

02:46:35.012 --> 02:46:43.732
But I don't see this as deterministic or
something that, is certain or forever.

02:46:45.072 --> 02:46:48.092
The truth is that Europe was filled with
countries that were imperial powers

02:46:49.252 --> 02:46:52.552
that also did not surrender
their empires very easily.

02:46:53.472 --> 02:46:55.412
- Sherman famously said, "War is hell."

02:46:56.312 --> 02:47:00.052
I'd like to talk to you about the nature of war,
having studied it for so long. What do those

02:47:00.112 --> 02:47:00.812
words mean to you?

02:47:01.332 --> 02:47:05.212
- Feels like a sickness we can't kick.
For all the ways we've gotten better

02:47:05.532 --> 02:47:09.472
as people, as humans throughout
our evolution, the one thing

02:47:09.532 --> 02:47:13.452
we keep coming back to is we kill
each other over disputes, and it's,

02:47:13.772 --> 02:47:15.952
pretty crazy that we're still doing
that when you really think about it.

02:47:16.832 --> 02:47:20.812
There's an aspect of war that's
fascinating to me because it

02:47:20.852 --> 02:47:24.512
brings out the-- along that worst, it
brings out the best and we see some

02:47:24.552 --> 02:47:28.552
wonderful stories, and we're
seeing that right now in Ukraine.

02:47:29.412 --> 02:47:33.392
but it is, when you really-- when I really step
back and think about it, it is depressing that

02:47:33.432 --> 02:47:35.392
this is still the way that we

02:47:36.352 --> 02:47:41.772
resolve differences. It's crazy that we
get to a point of who can kill more people

02:47:42.812 --> 02:47:44.992
to come to a conclusion. So it's

02:47:45.852 --> 02:47:50.492
unfortunately no, no end in sight, I
would say, but it's a human condition.

02:47:52.072 --> 02:47:56.031
- What gives you hope for the future of human
civilization? We've been talking about some

02:47:56.072 --> 02:47:59.632
dark topics. You ever sit back, look up
at the sky, both of you have families,

02:48:00.291 --> 02:48:04.272
look up at the sky and say, "I
think we're gonna be all right.

02:48:04.332 --> 02:48:07.312
We're gonna make it as a species"?

02:48:08.432 --> 02:48:12.132
- I'll give you two real quick here. We
just-- my wife and I just watched the

02:48:12.252 --> 02:48:16.092
Stanford Prison Experiment on, on Netflix. You
know what I'm talking about, the show that just

02:48:16.132 --> 02:48:18.072
came out? And at the end of that-

02:48:18.072 --> 02:48:22.052
... at the end of it, you've got all these people
that had nothing in common, and they just spent a

02:48:22.072 --> 02:48:25.912
bunch of time together kinda
trapped. They all became friends,

02:48:26.072 --> 02:48:29.552
and they came out the other side, and
there was no political debates or

02:48:29.752 --> 02:48:33.652
anything like that. It was just cool
to see so many different Americans

02:48:33.812 --> 02:48:37.592
in one spot come out the other
side friendly, having a good time.

02:48:38.132 --> 02:48:42.032
That was great. That was a pick me up. In the
context of what we've been talking about today with

02:48:42.252 --> 02:48:46.132
war, and specifically in Ukraine,
there's a new innovation on the robotics

02:48:46.212 --> 02:48:49.252
front that rolled out this past week-

02:48:49.252 --> 02:48:54.952
... where they saved a soldier with
a drone flying 30 kilometers away.

02:48:55.112 --> 02:48:58.712
So they put a soldier- ...
it's a company called SkyFall,

02:48:59.552 --> 02:49:03.632
and they have essentially a casket-

02:49:03.632 --> 02:49:07.432
... underneath a drone. They
flew this thing up to the front

02:49:07.472 --> 02:49:09.552
because they couldn't get this
soldier out. There were two of them.

02:49:09.872 --> 02:49:13.772
They couldn't get him out any other way. They
couldn't walk him out. They couldn't get the UGV

02:49:14.032 --> 02:49:15.132
ground robots in there to get him.

02:49:15.152 --> 02:49:18.832
- It's CODE 9.2, the regiment I mentioned
earlier. It's them, and they're one of the most

02:49:18.892 --> 02:49:19.612
innovative units.

02:49:20.172 --> 02:49:24.132
- They flew these in. On, in one of
them, the guy's buddies brought him

02:49:24.212 --> 02:49:28.012
out, packed him in, and they flew out.
The other one, the guy crawled into

02:49:28.072 --> 02:49:34.472
the container himself. And then once they
are in that pod, they have the ability to

02:49:34.532 --> 02:49:38.492
talk back with the crew so they're
not alone. And they can, "Hey, we're

02:49:38.512 --> 02:49:42.332
getting there. Just so you know, we're taking off.
Here we go. A few more minutes. You're doing great.

02:49:42.652 --> 02:49:46.552
Can't wait to see you." And they're talking
to him the whole way back. And the final,

02:49:46.972 --> 02:49:49.992
the video they shared with it, one
of the guys was in the hospital

02:49:50.680 --> 02:49:54.060
- Uh, being treated the day after with
his kid right there on his lap.

02:49:54.060 --> 02:49:57.920
And I thought, "That is so cool."
So much money and technology and

02:49:57.980 --> 02:50:01.640
effort, and it wasn't just one drone went
out, and you push a button, and it go.

02:50:01.660 --> 02:50:06.180
They... There was shaping operations and
coordination and infantry and armor-

02:50:06.180 --> 02:50:08.900
... and everything went into
the role to get this one guy,

02:50:09.740 --> 02:50:12.280
24-year-old, I think. Just
go get him. Loved it.

02:50:12.940 --> 02:50:16.860
- Can I give you another anecdote, also
from Cool Knight 2, ironically the

02:50:16.920 --> 02:50:21.600
same unit? Um, the... You asked,
you know, what gives me hope. So

02:50:23.039 --> 02:50:26.920
spend a lot of time studying war, dealing with war,
and so you get to see, I think, some of the worst

02:50:26.960 --> 02:50:30.840
of humanity and also some of the best humanity,
often in the same spaces. And if you travel

02:50:30.880 --> 02:50:33.060
the front line, you will see
that, right? You will see

02:50:33.940 --> 02:50:37.420
basically the worst that humanity
has to offer and the best,

02:50:38.320 --> 02:50:39.320
sometimes on the same day.

02:50:40.240 --> 02:50:44.180
Another anecdote from Cool Knight
2. You know, this fantastic video,

02:50:44.740 --> 02:50:49.200
A challenging one, but a great one, where

02:50:50.480 --> 02:50:54.100
they have two soldiers that are wounded.
This is before Skyforger system was

02:50:54.140 --> 02:50:56.700
available. This is someone's
pack. Two soldiers are wounded.

02:50:57.760 --> 02:51:01.260
They're being evacuated by UGV,
an uncrewed ground vehicle,

02:51:01.900 --> 02:51:06.000
and they're both in it kind
of like a, think of it as a,

02:51:07.120 --> 02:51:10.780
almost like a small truck,
and it gets hit by a Russian

02:51:10.860 --> 02:51:14.800
drone, and it's disabled, and they're stuck
out there, okay? They're both wounded.

02:51:14.820 --> 02:51:19.880
They're stuck in this UGV, and the
company commander, without waiting,

02:51:20.940 --> 02:51:24.460
see them on video in a
pickup truck racing to them,

02:51:25.840 --> 02:51:29.700
pulls up, backs up to them, gets
out. He's a really big guy, you can

02:51:29.740 --> 02:51:31.880
see in the video, not
wearing any body armor-

02:51:31.940 --> 02:51:35.640
... didn't even bother to throw on his
plate carrier. Goes and grabs one guy,

02:51:35.740 --> 02:51:39.420
throws him in the pickup, goes and grabs a
second guy, throws him in the pickup, and

02:51:39.620 --> 02:51:41.680
speeds out of there, and
they all make it out.

02:51:42.940 --> 02:51:46.860
Obviously incredibly dangerous.
There are many moments

02:51:46.900 --> 02:51:50.780
like this that you can see in
a war that they do give you

02:51:50.820 --> 02:51:54.260
hope. And also the-- I mean, I'll tell
you the last part. This may sound silly.

02:51:55.660 --> 02:51:59.579
It's a grim enterprise. The thing
that gets everyone through it is a

02:51:59.620 --> 02:52:04.740
dark sense of humor, which you need to
have in this profession- ... as well. Um-

02:52:04.740 --> 02:52:08.940
... people who have been
fighting for a long time,

02:52:09.940 --> 02:52:11.760
still you can see haven't
lost their humanity.

02:52:12.580 --> 02:52:16.940
- At every level. The humor, the wry smile.

02:52:17.520 --> 02:52:24.440
The ability to, yeah, show love towards
each other. It's great. That's a, that's a,

02:52:24.920 --> 02:52:28.580
man, that's such a complicated thing about
war. It really does show some of the

02:52:28.640 --> 02:52:31.680
most beautiful aspects of human nature.

02:52:32.360 --> 02:52:32.680
- It does.

02:52:33.400 --> 02:52:38.080
- Sometimes we need tragedy to, like,
really bring out the best in us.

02:52:38.360 --> 02:52:41.540
- It's a stark contrast
when you see it, right?

02:52:42.200 --> 02:52:46.120
It's contrasted much... It's in
sharper relief than if you are going

02:52:46.200 --> 02:52:47.400
day to day in your normal life.

02:52:48.400 --> 02:52:52.180
- Well Preston, first of all, I wanna
say thank you. You gave me this gift.

02:52:53.740 --> 02:52:55.860
- Absolutely, man. How about that?

02:52:55.980 --> 02:52:58.820
- That I'm gonna have to drink soon,

02:52:59.840 --> 02:53:03.800
and all of us like ships in
the night literally met at

02:53:03.860 --> 02:53:07.460
night and made this happen. This is a huge
honor to finally meet the both of you.

02:53:08.320 --> 02:53:12.260
Thank you so much for everything
you do in the world, and thank you

02:53:12.340 --> 02:53:14.680
so much for this conversation.
This was awesome.

02:53:15.220 --> 02:53:15.280
- Thank you.

02:53:15.300 --> 02:53:16.160
- Thank you, Lex. It's been great.

02:53:16.180 --> 02:53:18.400
- Yeah. Thanks for hosting
this and for inviting us.

